window.DC_BANK._journey = {"track":{"id":"lj","name":"LAW302 Conflict of Laws","short":"LAW302","color":"#2A7A8C","dark":"#1D5966","since":"2026-10-28","levels":{"L1":"Foundations","L2":"Rules & cases","L3":"Apply","L4":"Traps & critique"}},"weeks":[{"id":"w12","num":"12","label":"Week 12","t":"Foreign Judgments I","full":"Recognition and enforcement: principles","sub":"What a foreign judgment can do in Singapore, which regime applies, why Singapore recognises it, the common law requirements, and its effect as res judicata.","color":["#2A7A8C","#1D5966","#E4F1F4"],"about":{"built":"the Week 12 handout (AY2026-27), the FJ Part 1 slides (Dr Adeline Chong), Chong & Yip Ch 5 (Sections A, B and D, with in-scope material from E and G), and every case and statute on the Week 12 reading list **that is in the project**. Two required secondary readings are missing; see Part B.","abbr":"C&Y = Chong & Yip, *Singapore Private International Law: Commercial Issues and Practice*; CL = common law; REFJA = Reciprocal Enforcement of Foreign Judgments Act 1959; CCAA = Choice of Court Agreements Act 2016; RECJA = Reciprocal Enforcement of Commonwealth Judgments Act (repealed); FLPA = Foreign Limitation Periods Act 2012; LA = Limitation Act 1959; MoG = Memorandum of Guidance; FNC = forum non conveniens; SG = Singapore. Paragraph pinpoints are in [square brackets].","secs":[{"title":"Readings covered","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Statutes:** CCAA ss 2(1), 13, 20, 21; REFJA (the whole Act); FLPA s 5."},{"t":"***Merck Sharp & Dohme Corp v Merck KGaA*** [2021] 1 SLR 1102 (CA): the full judgment."},{"t":"***Ocean Tankers (Pte) Ltd v DGJ*** [2024] 2 SLR 790 [93], [95]–[96]."},{"t":"***Poh Soon Kiat v Desert Palace Inc*** [2010] 1 SLR 1129 (CA) [25]–[34], [36]–[54]."},{"t":"***Republic of India v Deutsche Telekom AG*** [2024] 1 SLR 56 (CA) [65]."},{"t":"***Adams v Cape Industries plc*** [1990] Ch 433 (CA): an extract of the passages on presence and residence, which is the focus the handout asks for."},{"t":"***Lucasfilm Ltd v Ainsworth*** [2010] Ch 503 (CA) [187]–[195]."},{"t":"***WSG Nimbus Pte Ltd v Board of Control for Cricket in Sri Lanka*** [2002] 1 SLR(R) 1088."},{"t":"***IGCF SPV 21 Ltd v Al Jomaih Power Ltd*** [2025] UKPC 54."},{"t":"***Giant Light Metal Technology (Kunshan) Co Ltd v Aksa Far East Pte Ltd*** [2014] 2 SLR 545."},{"t":"***Vizcaya Partners Ltd v Picard*** [2016] 3 All ER 181 (PC) [32]–[72]."},{"t":"***Lakshmi Anil Salgaocar v Jhaveri Darsan Jitendra*** [2019] 2 SLR 372 (CA) [99]–[105]."},{"t":"***Sang Cheol Woo v Spackman*** [2022] SGHC 298 [19]–[48], [106]–[112]."},{"t":"***Gonzalo Gil White v Oro Negro Drilling Pte Ltd*** [2024] 1 SLR 307 (CA) [83]–[93]."},{"t":"**C&Y Ch 5** Sections A, B and D."},{"t":"**Slides:** FJ Part 1."}]}]},{"title":"Missing (both are required secondary readings)","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Yeo, \"Common Law Developments Relating to Foreign Judgments\", 9th Yong Pung How Professorship of Law Lecture (2016)."},{"t":"Yeo, \"The Changing Global Landscape for Foreign Judgments\", Yong Pung How Professorship of Law Lecture (2021)."},{"t":"**Effect on this file:** the doctrine below is fully sourced from the cases, the statutes, C&Y and the slides. I have **not** seen the lectures. Going by their titles, they are most likely to add commentary on:","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"reciprocity and the rationale for recognition (Module 2);"},{"t":"presence versus residence, and the MoGs (Module 3.4);"},{"t":"submission and *Henry v Geoprosco* (Module 3.5);"},{"t":"non-money judgments (Module 3.3);"},{"t":"the Hague Judgments Convention 2019 and the \"global landscape\" (Stage 1)."}]}]},{"t":"These spots carry a \"Missing reading\" badge. Treat any academic view stated there as coming from C&Y only."}]}]},{"title":"Notes on the handout and the textbook","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**REFJA section numbers.** The handout cites older numbering. In the current Act (the version in the project):","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"\"s 4(3A)\" is now **s 4(4)** (registration of non-money judgments only if \"just and convenient\");"},{"t":"\"s 5(2)(a)\" and \"s 5(2)(a)(iv)\" are now **s 5(3)(a)** and **s 5(3)(a)(iv)** (the deemed grounds of jurisdiction)."},{"t":"(The Week 13 handout's \"s 5(3)(b)\" is likewise now s 5(4)(b).) This file uses the current numbering."}]}]},{"t":"***WSG Nimbus* citation.** The handout cites the case both as [2002] 1 SLR(R) 1088 and as [2002] 3 SLR 603. These are the same case in two different law reports."},{"t":"**C&Y is out of date on two points:**","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"It says the RECJA repeal takes effect \"from a yet to be determined future date\" (5.01). The slides and *Venetian Macau v Hu* treat the RECJA as already repealed."},{"t":"It says \"only Hong Kong SAR is gazetted under the REFJA\" (5.151). The handout now lists Hong Kong, Brunei, Australia (Commonwealth, states and territories), India, Malaysia, New Zealand, Pakistan, Papua New Guinea, Sri Lanka and the UK."},{"t":"Follow the handout and the slides on both points."}]}]}]}]},{"title":"Textbook enrichment (this edition)","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"The lessons were expanded with in-scope material from **C&Y Ch 5**: Sections A (introduction, corrected as in B3), B.1–B.6 (the common law rules, defences, severance and estoppel), D (REFJA), E (CCAA Part 3) and G (anti-enforcement injunctions)."},{"t":"Mentioned only, not tested: B.7 (judgments in rem beyond the in personam / in rem distinction), C (the repealed RECJA, where the cases still refer to it) and F (the Hague Judgments Convention 2019)."},{"t":"Left out as outside the course: H (the Singapore Convention on Mediation)."},{"t":"Where the textbook (2023) and the course materials differ, the lessons follow the handout, the slides and the later cases."}]}]}]},"stages":[{"id":"w12.s0","n":"0","title":"Why a foreign judgment matters (strategic uses)","sub":"Explain what a party can do with a foreign judgment in Singapore. This is the handout's first learning objective.","lessons":[{"id":"w12.s0","code":"Stage 0","kind":"stage","t":"Why a foreign judgment matters (strategic uses)","blocks":[{"k":"obj","t":"Explain what a party can **do** with a foreign judgment in Singapore. This is the handout's first learning objective."},{"k":"big","t":"A foreign judgment can be used as a **sword** (enforced to collect money) or as a **shield** (recognised to stop the other side relitigating). Recognition always comes first. Enforcement is recognition plus extra requirements."},{"k":"step","t":"Two uses: a sword and a shield"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"A foreign judgment is like a winning ticket from another country. You can **cash it in** (enforcement: make the loser pay), or you can **hold it up as a shield** (recognition: \"this was already decided, stop suing me\"). Either way, Singapore must first accept that the ticket is genuine."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Mei sued Bob in Country X and won. If Bob has money in Singapore and Mei wants it, she seeks **enforcement**: making the **judgment debtor** (Bob) pay the **judgment creditor** (Mei). If Bob instead sues her here over the same dispute, she wants the court to treat the matter as already decided. That is **recognition**: giving the judgment effect as **res judicata** (\"a matter already judged\"), usually through an **estoppel**, a rule that stops a party from reopening what has been decided (C&Y 5.08, 5.10)."},{"p":"\"Recognition\" can mean every effect of a judgment, enforcement included. Chong & Yip use the narrower sense: every effect *except* enforcement (C&Y 5.08). In that sense the two are steps on one staircase. Singapore cannot make Bob pay on a judgment it has not first accepted as having legal effect."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Recognition** (narrow sense): giving the foreign judgment legal effect, typically as res judicata by way of an estoppel (C&Y 5.08, 5.10)."},{"t":"**Enforcement:** making the debtor pay."},{"t":"**Recognition is a prerequisite to enforcement** (C&Y 5.08–5.09)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-0.6"]},{"k":"step","t":"One checklist, with an extra box for enforcement"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Whatever Mei wants to do with the ticket, Singapore runs the same checks. Cashing it in needs one extra thing: the ticket must be for a fixed amount of money."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Sword or shield, the core questions are the same. Is the judgment **on the merits**? Did the foreign court have **international jurisdiction** over Bob by Singapore's rules? Is it **final and conclusive**? Is there **no defence**? (Stage 3; Week 13.) Only enforcement adds the **fresh money judgment** requirement. The reason lies in the form enforcement takes at common law: Mei sues on Bob's obligation to pay, and that obligation is limited to paying a sum of money (C&Y 5.12 n 27, 5.31). A shield involves no debt, so the extra box does not apply."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"The same requirements apply to recognition and enforcement: on the merits, international jurisdiction, final and conclusive, no defences (C&Y 5.09, 5.20)."},{"t":"**One exception:** the **fresh money judgment** requirement (a fixed sum that is not a tax, fine or penalty) applies to **enforcement only** (C&Y 5.09, 5.20)."},{"t":"Whether **reciprocity** is a further requirement for both is uncertain after *Merck* [39] (C&Y 5.09; Stage 2).","b":["open"]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-0.1"]},{"k":"step","t":"The shield in action"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Suppose Mei loses abroad and then brings the same claim in Singapore, hoping for a better result. Bob holds up the foreign judgment: \"We have done this already, and I won.\""}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"That is Chong & Yip's own example. Nothing automatically stops Mei from issuing the claim here; it is up to Bob to raise the foreign judgment (C&Y 5.10). He pleads it as a **cause of action estoppel**: once a competent court has finally decided a claim between the same parties, neither can bring that claim again (Stage 4). Bob is asking for **recognition**, not enforcement, so he needs no special application or registration. He simply pleads the judgment under the normal pleading rules (C&Y 5.10; *Merck* [35])."}]},{"k":"call","c":"why","n":[{"p":"The shield serves the finality of litigation (Stage 2). Without it, a claimant who lost abroad could make the winner fight the same case twice."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Shield scenario:** A sues B abroad and loses, then sues B again in Singapore on the same cause of action. B pleads the foreign judgment as a defence (or counterclaim), raising a **cause of action estoppel** (C&Y 5.10, 5.105)."},{"t":"**No special procedure:** the normal pleading rules apply (C&Y 5.10; *Merck* [35])."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-0.7"]},{"k":"step","t":"No merger: why the winner can sue again"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"When a Singapore court gives judgment, the original claim is \"swallowed\" by it: from then on you hold the judgment, not the claim. On the Court of Appeal's current (preliminary) view, a foreign win does not swallow the claim. So you can still sue on the original claim and point to the foreign judgment as proof that it has already been decided."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"The swallowing is called **merger**. A cause of action merges into a **Singapore** judgment (*Ocean Tankers* [93]), but the Court of Appeal's preliminary view is that merger does not extend to foreign judgments (*Ocean Tankers* [95]–[96])."},{"p":"Why would a winner sue on the original claim? Because some foreign judgments cannot be enforced here. If Country X (not a REFJA or CCAA country) ordered Bob to transfer shares to Mei, that is not a money judgment (Module 3.3). Mei can instead sue Bob again on the original cause of action and plead the Country X judgment as res judicata. If it meets the requirements for recognition, the merits need not be argued again (C&Y 5.105). **Declaratory judgments**, which only state the parties' rights (\"the contract was validly terminated\"), work the same way: there is nothing to enforce, but they can found a res judicata (C&Y 5.11)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Merger applies to **Singapore** judgments (*Ocean Tankers* [93]) but **does not extend to foreign judgments**: a preliminary view in *Ocean Tankers* [95]–[96], approving *Malaysia Credit Finance* and matching the English common law."},{"t":"**Consequence:** a successful foreign claimant can sue again on the **original** cause of action and plead the judgment as res judicata. This is useful where the judgment cannot be enforced, for example a non-money judgment outside REFJA and the CCAA (C&Y 5.105)."},{"t":"Prof Tham's suggestion that merger might occur once a judgment is registered under statute was **not decided** (*Ocean Tankers* [95]).","b":["open"]},{"t":"**Declaratory judgments** cannot be enforced; their value is in raising res judicata (C&Y 5.11)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-0.2"]},{"k":"step","t":"In personam or in rem? (a short note)"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Most judgments settle a quarrel between two people: \"Bob owes Mei.\" A few settle something about a thing, or a person's status, for everyone: \"this painting belongs to Mei\", whoever asks."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"A judgment **in personam** binds the parties (and their privies, those who stand in their shoes). A judgment **in rem** pronounces on rights to a thing, such as title to property, or on a person's status, against the whole world (C&Y 5.127). The label turns on what the judgment is **for**, not on how the foreign court took jurisdiction. The Singapore court decides it by looking at the nature of the foreign proceedings and the effect the foreign court intended (C&Y 5.129). The course focuses on in personam judgments."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**In personam:** binds the parties. **In rem:** pronounces on rights to a thing as against the whole world (handout note; C&Y 5.127–5.128)."},{"t":"The purpose of the judgment decides this, not how the foreign court took jurisdiction (handout note). One judgment can have both aspects (C&Y 5.127)."},{"t":"Characterisation is for the Singapore court (C&Y 5.129)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-0.3"]},{"k":"take","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"A foreign judgment can be a sword (enforcement) or a shield (recognition). Recognition always comes first."},{"t":"The requirements are the same for both, except that only enforcement needs a fresh money judgment."},{"t":"Recognition needs no special procedure: the judgment is simply pleaded."},{"t":"On *Ocean Tankers*' preliminary view there is no merger, so a winner abroad can sue again on the original claim and plead the judgment as res judicata."}]}]},{"k":"traps","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Do not say a foreign judgment \"merges\" the cause of action. *Ocean Tankers* says it does not."},{"t":"Do not apply the money-judgment requirement to recognition."},{"t":"Do not treat an unenforceable judgment as useless: non-money and declaratory judgments can still be recognised as res judicata."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":true,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-0.8"]}],"ic":"compass","nq":6}]},{"id":"w12.s1","n":"1","title":"The regimes and the common law procedure","sub":"Pick the correct regime, explain how the regimes fit together and why registration is the easier route, and describe how enforcement works at common law.","lessons":[{"id":"w12.s1","code":"Stage 1","kind":"stage","t":"The regimes and the common law procedure","blocks":[{"k":"obj","t":"Pick the correct regime, explain how the regimes fit together and why registration is the easier route, and describe how enforcement works at common law."},{"k":"big","t":"Try the doors in order: the CCAA, then REFJA, then the common law. The statutes replace a fresh lawsuit with an application, and REFJA, where it applies, is compulsory."},{"k":"step","t":"Three doors into Singapore"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Which door do you use? If the judgment comes from a **chosen** court in a Hague Convention country, use the CCAA door. If it comes from a **listed (gazetted)** country, use the REFJA door, where you simply register the judgment. You *must* use that door for judgments it covers. For **everyone else**, use the common law door: start a fresh case in Singapore saying, \"they owe me under this judgment.\""}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Two of the doors are statutes, each tied to an international arrangement; the common law catches everything else. Recent reforms, enacting the Hague Choice of Court Convention through the CCAA and widening REFJA in 2019, are part of a broader drive to make Singapore judgments, especially those of the Singapore International Commercial Court (SICC), enforceable abroad. The government hopes to use the amended REFJA to negotiate reciprocal arrangements with more countries (C&Y 5.02)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**CCAA Part 3:** judgments of the **chosen** court (the court named in an exclusive choice of court agreement) of a **Contracting State** (a party to the Hague Convention on Choice of Court Agreements), or of a court it transferred the case to (s 2(1)). On application under s 13 they are recognised and enforced like a General Division judgment; judicial settlements can be enforced (s 20); s 21 preserves other routes. The CCAA prevails over REFJA (next step; Week 13)."},{"t":"**REFJA:** judgments of **recognised courts** (the courts of a gazetted country that its gazette order covers) of countries **gazetted** (listed by Ministerial order) under s 3, which requires \"substantial reciprocity\" (s 3(1); C&Y 5.151). For a judgment within its scope, REFJA **bars** any proceedings to recover the sum payable other than registration, so a common law action on it is not allowed (s 7(1))."},{"t":"**Gazetted countries** (handout): Hong Kong, Brunei, Australia (Commonwealth, states and territories), India, Malaysia, New Zealand, Pakistan, Papua New Guinea, Sri Lanka and the UK. C&Y 5.151 (only Hong Kong) reflects an earlier position."},{"t":"**Common law:** all other judgments."},{"t":"**RECJA:** repealed (*Venetian Macau* [29]; FJ Part 1 slide 2); C&Y 5.01 (repeal at a future date) reflects an earlier position. The countries it covered are being moved to REFJA (C&Y 5.02)."},{"t":"**The global landscape:** the Hague Judgments Convention 2019 sets out indirect rules on jurisdiction and exhaustive grounds for refusal. Singapore took part in the negotiations but has not decided whether to join (C&Y 5.03).","b":["miss"]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-8.4"]},{"k":"step","t":"The order of the doors"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Picture the doors along a corridor. Try the CCAA door first. If your judgment does not fit, try REFJA. Only if neither fits do you reach the common law. If your judgment fits the REFJA door, the common law door is locked. The picture breaks down at one point: the CCAA door does **not** lock the common law door."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"The regimes overlap: a chosen court's judgment from a country that is both a Contracting State and gazetted fits both statutes, and any judgment could in principle be sued on at common law. The statutes settle the order. The CCAA wins over REFJA. REFJA, where it applies, bars any common law action to recover the judgment sum, even after its six-year registration period has passed. The CCAA shuts out nothing, though it is almost always the better choice."}]},{"k":"call","c":"example","n":[{"p":"Mei's money judgment comes from a recognised court in Country Y, a REFJA country, and falls within Y's gazette order; there was no choice of court agreement. She must register it; a common law action is barred. Had it come from the chosen court in Country Z, a Contracting State that is also gazetted, she would use the CCAA, not REFJA, and could fall back on the common law if she had to."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**CCAA over REFJA:** REFJA does not apply to any judgment within the CCAA (REFJA s 2A; C&Y 5.173), so if a judgment falls within both, only the CCAA route is available. After a refusal under the CCAA, the creditor cannot register under REFJA (C&Y 5.200)."},{"t":"**REFJA over the common law:** s 7(1) bars a common law action to recover the sum due under a judgment within REFJA (C&Y 5.173). There is no discretion to allow one, and an expired registration period does not reopen the common law."},{"t":"**CCAA and the common law:** the CCAA does not exclude the common law (s 21), though the CCAA is almost always more advantageous: simpler procedure, and non-money judgments are enforceable (C&Y 5.201).","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"After a CCAA refusal, the common law is open in principle, unless pursuing it is an abuse of process, such as re-arguing the same issues (C&Y 5.201).","b":["acad"]}]}]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-0.4"]},{"k":"step","t":"Why registration is the easier door"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Suing at common law means starting a whole new case just to collect money a court has already ordered Bob to pay. Registering under REFJA is more like getting the foreign judgment stamped at a counter. Once stamped, it works like a Singapore judgment."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Chong & Yip identify two practical benefits (C&Y 5.149). Once registered, the judgment can be enforced as if it were a Singapore judgment, and Mei needs no court permission to serve notice of the registration on Bob abroad. At common law she would have to start an action, serve it, and, if Bob is overseas, obtain permission to serve him out of Singapore (C&Y 5.13–5.14)."},{"p":"Easier is not automatic. REFJA is modelled on the common law (C&Y 5.147–5.148), and Bob can apply to set the registration aside on the grounds in s 5 (C&Y 5.159; Week 13). What REFJA adds is breadth: since 2019 it reaches many judgments the common law cannot enforce, including orders made before the final judgment and outcomes the parties agreed."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Benefits** (C&Y 5.149): a registered judgment has, for enforcement, the same force and effect as a local judgment (REFJA s 4(7)(a)); no permission is needed to serve notice of registration abroad (ROC 2021 O 60 r 7(2))."},{"t":"**Same basic framework** as the common law: international jurisdiction, final and conclusive, no defences (C&Y 5.148)."},{"t":"**Scope after 2019:** money and non-money judgments (such as injunctions, specific performance and **freezing orders**, which stop a party moving its assets away), lower-court judgments, **interlocutory judgments** (made before the final judgment, such as orders preserving assets meanwhile), **consent judgments** and orders (on terms the parties agreed), and **judicial settlements** (settlements approved by, or concluded before, a court) (REFJA s 2(1) definition of \"judgment\", s 3; C&Y 5.152–5.153, 5.178)."},{"t":"**Gazette orders** are negotiated country by country and may cover less than the Act allows (C&Y 5.151). The handout's list describes \"final money judgments of superior courts\"; that reflects the gazette orders, not the Act's outer limit."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-8.1"]},{"k":"step","t":"Registering: time limits, bars and limits"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"The counter has opening hours and house rules. Come within six years. You cannot register a debt that has already been paid. Some items are accepted only in part, or only if the court thinks it sensible."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"The registration rules keep Singapore in control of what it enforces. A non-money order, such as an injunction, needs a closer look than a debt, so the court asks whether enforcing it here would be \"just and convenient\". Damages that go beyond compensating Mei's actual loss, such as punitive damages, are cut back to the compensatory amount."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Time:** within 6 years of the judgment; if there was an appeal, the appellate judgment may be registered at any time after the last appellate judgment (s 4(1); C&Y 5.150). The High Court has said the 6-year limit should apply to appellate judgments too (C&Y 5.150).","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"Compare the common law: 6 years under LA s 6(1)(a), running from when the judgment became final and conclusive (*Sang Cheol Woo*; Module 3.6)."}]}],"b":["obiter"]},{"t":"**Bars:** no registration if the judgment has been wholly satisfied or discharged, or cannot be enforced in the country of the original court; if partly satisfied, only the balance (s 4(3), (9); C&Y 5.160)."},{"t":"**No taxes, fines or penalties**; registrable parts can be severed (C&Y 5.154)."},{"t":"**Non-money judgments:** registered only if enforcement is \"just and convenient\", otherwise the monetary equivalent may be registered (s 4(4)). Factors include whether Singapore has an equivalent remedy, whether an injunction is territorially limited, and any risk of illegality or breach of privilege (C&Y 5.156)."},{"t":"**Non-compensatory damages:** registered only for the compensatory amount, considering whether the award covers the costs of the foreign proceedings (s 4(5)–(6); C&Y 5.155)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-8.2","LAW302-Q12-8.6"]},{"k":"step","t":"Recognition under REFJA needs no registration"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Registration is for cashing in. If you only want to hold the judgment up as a shield, REFJA lets you do that without visiting the counter."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"REFJA has its own version of the Stage 0 shield. If Bob sues Mei here on a claim he has already lost in a REFJA country, Mei simply pleads that judgment in her defence. She has nothing to collect, so she never needs to register it (C&Y 5.172)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**REFJA s 11(1):** a judgment within the Act is conclusive between the parties in all proceedings founded on the same cause of action, and may be relied on by way of defence or counterclaim, without registration (C&Y 5.172)."},{"t":"**s 11(2):** the defences that bar enforcement also bar recognition (C&Y 5.172)."},{"t":"Under the UK equivalent, the judgment must be **on the merits** (*Black-Clawson* (HL); C&Y 5.172)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-8.7"]},{"k":"step","t":"Memoranda of Guidance"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"A Memorandum of Guidance is like two libraries swapping booklets that explain their own house rules. Visitors know what to expect, but no rule changes."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"The Singapore court has signed **Memoranda of Guidance** (MoGs) on enforcing money judgments with various foreign courts. They state each side's existing law and bind no one, so they may seem to achieve little. But they clarify the law and are symbols of judicial **comity**, the respect that courts of different countries show one another (C&Y 5.02). They do not gazette a country (only a Ministerial order under REFJA s 3 does that) and they create no rule of law. That matters for a handout study question: some MoGs describe residence as a common law ground of international jurisdiction."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"MoGs are **non-binding**. They state each country's existing law, help clarify it, and are \"symbols of judicial comity\" (C&Y 5.02; handout study question)."},{"t":"Some MoGs list residence at commencement as a common law ground of international jurisdiction. Whether that is still good law: see Module 3.4.","b":["open","miss"]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-0.5"]},{"k":"step","t":"The common law door: a fresh action on the judgment"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"At common law the foreign judgment works like an IOU. Here the Stage 0 ticket picture needs correcting: Mei does not cash the foreign ticket directly. She starts a Singapore case saying \"Bob owes me this sum because of the judgment\", obtains a Singapore judgment, and enforces that."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Mei sues on the obligation the foreign judgment creates, treated as an implied debt: the obligation theory from Stage 2 at work. Strictly, then, it is a misnomer to speak of \"enforcing\" a foreign judgment; what is enforced is the Singapore judgment Mei obtains (C&Y 5.12). And because the obligation sued on is a debt, only an order to pay a fixed sum can be enforced this way (C&Y 5.12 n 27, 5.31). In practice Mei will normally apply for **summary judgment**, a judgment given without a full trial where the defendant has no real defence."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"The creditor brings a **fresh action** on the obligation created by the judgment (an implied debt, so money only), usually seeking summary judgment. The resulting **local** judgment is what is enforced (C&Y 5.12–5.13, 5.31; *Poh Soon Kiat* [41]–[42])."},{"t":"**Summary judgment:** the creditor bears the burden. The debtor needs only a fair or reasonable chance of a real or bona fide defence to obtain leave to defend (C&Y 5.13)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-0.9"]},{"k":"step","t":"Serving Bob abroad, and the forum non conveniens question"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"If Bob is overseas, Mei needs the court's permission to bring him into a Singapore case. Normally the court would ask, \"Is Singapore the right place for this dispute?\" But Mei is not asking Singapore to decide the dispute again; she only wants help collecting. Whether the \"right place\" question still applies is unsettled."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"In legal terms, to serve Bob abroad Mei must show that Singapore \"is the appropriate court\" (C&Y 5.14). **Forum non conveniens** is the doctrine under which a court declines a case because another forum is clearly more appropriate. In *Rodriguez Licea*, the High Court said it does not arise when a creditor enforces a foreign judgment, because that is not a fresh suit (C&Y 5.15)."},{"p":"England takes a different line. In *Tasarruf Mevduati Sigorta Fonu v Demirel*, a creditor sought to enforce a Turkish judgment against a debtor with no presence or assets in England. The Court of Appeal held that assets were not a prerequisite for service out, but that permission should not ordinarily be given without a **real prospect of a legitimate benefit** to the creditor. On the facts one was shown, chiefly because the debtor had been found to have acted fraudulently, so his assets might be hard to trace (C&Y 5.16)."},{"p":"Chong & Yip see force on both sides. Forum non conveniens picks one forum for the merits, whereas enforcement may need several countries, and the enforcing court only helps to enforce an obligation already decided. Yet a creditor might sue to harass the debtor, or to \"launder\" the judgment through a Singapore judgment, so some filter is appropriate (C&Y 5.19)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Service out:** the claimant must show Singapore is the appropriate court (ROC 2021 O 8 r 1(1)); PD para 63(3)(m) supplies the nexus. The preferred English view is that assets are not a prerequisite, though their absence may affect the discretion (C&Y 5.14)."},{"t":"**Forum non conveniens** (C&Y 5.15–5.19):","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"*Rodriguez Licea* (HC): it does not arise in enforcement, which is not a fresh suit. Art 13(2) of the Hague Judgments Convention is to the same effect (C&Y 5.15). Prof Yeo has queried whether the court stated a rule of law or a view on the facts (C&Y 5.15 n 35).","b":["miss"]},{"t":"*Tasarruf Mevduati Sigorta Fonu v Demirel* (England): a \"real prospect of a legitimate benefit\" is ordinarily required. It shades into forum (non) conveniens, which the court did not discard (C&Y 5.16–5.17)."},{"t":"*Chevron v Yaiguaje* (Canada): no \"real and substantial connection\" is needed, but the debtor may still argue forum non conveniens (C&Y 5.18)."},{"t":"C&Y: some filter is appropriate, but requiring both forum conveniens and a legitimate benefit would be \"overkill\" (C&Y 5.19).","b":["acad"]}]}],"b":["open"]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-8.9"]},{"k":"take","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Three regimes: CCAA Part 3, REFJA and the common law. The RECJA is repealed."},{"t":"The CCAA prevails over REFJA; REFJA bars a common law action on judgments within it; the CCAA does not."},{"t":"A registered judgment works like a local one, and notice can be served abroad without permission. Recognition under REFJA needs no registration."},{"t":"At common law the creditor sues on an implied debt, usually by summary judgment. Whether a forum non conveniens or \"legitimate benefit\" filter applies is open."}]}]},{"k":"traps","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Do not run a common law action on a judgment of a REFJA-gazetted court that falls within the Act. Section 7 bars it."},{"t":"Do not apply REFJA s 7(1) to a judgment within the CCAA. REFJA does not apply to it, and the CCAA leaves the common law open."},{"t":"Do not treat MoGs as law."},{"t":"Do not confuse the Hague Convention on Choice of Court Agreements (enacted by the CCAA) with the Hague Judgments Convention 2019 (not adopted)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":true,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-8.3","LAW302-Q12-8.5","LAW302-Q12-8.8","LAW302-Q12-8.10","LAW302-Q12-8.11"]}],"ic":"branch","nq":14}]},{"id":"w12.s2","n":"2","title":"Why Singapore gives effect to foreign judgments","sub":"State the three rationales the Court of Appeal considered, which one it preferred, and what follows from that choice, including the reciprocity question.","lessons":[{"id":"w12.s2","code":"Stage 2","kind":"stage","t":"Why Singapore gives effect to foreign judgments","blocks":[{"k":"obj","t":"State the three rationales the Court of Appeal considered, which one it preferred, and what follows from that choice, including the reciprocity question."},{"k":"big","t":"*Merck* placed comity at the centre. A comity-based rationale is what opens the door to a possible reciprocity requirement."},{"k":"step","t":"Why respect a foreign court at all?"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Why should Singapore care what a foreign court said? Three reasons were discussed. First, the loser now owes a duty to obey the judgment (obligation). Second, courts should respect each other (comity). Third, arguments must end at some point (finality)."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"A foreign judgment has no standing in Singapore unless Singapore recognises it (*Poh Soon Kiat* [41]). So the law needs a reason for giving it effect, and that reason shapes the rules: what is required, how strictly, and whether Singapore asks for anything in return."},{"p":"In *Merck*, the Court of Appeal had to decide whether an English ruling on a clause in a 1970 co-existence agreement, made by the predecessors of two companies called Merck, bound them in Singapore (Module 4.2). It first asked why foreign judgments are recognised at all."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"***Merck* [29]:** two older theories (implied contract, and allegiance to a foreign sovereign) were rejected long ago. The remaining contenders are **obligation**, **comity** and **finality** (C&Y 5.05)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-1.1"]},{"k":"step","t":"Obligation: the judgment creates a duty to pay"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Once a proper court has ordered Bob to pay, Bob has a new duty: to obey that judgment. On this view, Singapore is not obeying Country X. It is holding Bob to his own duty."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"The **doctrine of obligation** is the traditional common law basis. If the requirements are met, a foreign judgment creates an obligation that the debtor must obey, independent of the original cause of action (C&Y 5.06). It comes from *Schibsby v Westenholz* (Blackburn J), and it asks whether the debtor voluntarily put itself in a position to be bound (*Merck* [30])."},{"p":"*Merck* noted two criticisms ([30], citing Harder). The theory presupposes what it is meant to explain: why should a foreign judgment create an obligation at all? And it cannot account for judgments that declare a status, such as a divorce, which impose no obligation on anyone. The Court was unenthusiastic but did **not** reject the theory. It still explains why, at common law, the creditor sues on a debt (C&Y 5.06, 5.12)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**The theory** (*Merck* [30]; *Schibsby v Westenholz*): a judgment of a competent court imposes an obligation to pay, which the forum enforces. The focus is on whether the debtor voluntarily placed itself in a position where it is bound."},{"t":"**The criticism** ([30]): it presupposes what it is meant to explain, and it cannot account for declarations of status. Prof Yeo considers the status criticism misplaced (C&Y 5.06 n 17).","b":["acad","miss"]},{"t":"**The court's attitude:** unenthusiastic, but it did **not** reject the theory, which earlier Singapore decisions support (C&Y 5.06). It still explains the common law procedure, since the creditor sues on an obligation (C&Y 5.12)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-1.3"]},{"k":"step","t":"Comity and finality"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Comity is the respect courts of different countries show each other, like neighbours who honour each other's decisions because they expect to live side by side for a long time. Finality is the idea that a dispute, once properly decided, should stay decided."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"**Comity** is the respect one country's courts give to the acts of another's. *Godard v Gray* is often read as rejecting comity as the basis for recognition. *Merck* explained that case as rejecting only a thin version of it: comity as mere discretionary politeness, a courtesy a court could give or withhold at will. In its fuller sense comity is now \"routinely invoked\" ([31]). **Finality**, the policy that litigation must end, serves the recognising state only in the broad sense of enhancing comity ([32]). So comity underlies finality too (C&Y 5.07)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Comity** ([31]): *Godard v Gray* is explained as rejecting comity only in the sense of mere discretionary politeness. Comity is now \"routinely invoked\"."},{"t":"**Finality** ([32]): this serves the recognising state only in the broad sense of enhancing comity. The Court saw comity as underlying finality (C&Y 5.07)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-1.5"]},{"k":"step","t":"The Court's answer: comity, balanced against the rule of law"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Respect has limits. Singapore's courts respect foreign courts, but they also have a job at home: protecting the rule of law in Singapore. So respect for foreign courts is always weighed against that job."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"The Court concluded that \"considerations of transnational comity and reciprocal respect among courts of independent jurisdictions\" undergird recognition at common law, including transnational **issue estoppel**, the rule that stops a party re-arguing a particular issue already decided between the same parties (*Merck* [33]; C&Y 5.07). Comity is not unconditional. It is balanced against the Singapore court's constitutional role in safeguarding the rule of law. That is why transnational issue estoppel must be applied with special care ([34]; Module 4.2)."}]},{"k":"call","c":"tip","n":[{"p":"Asked why Singapore recognises foreign judgments, lead with comity (*Merck* [33]), state the counterweight, and add that the obligation theory survives as the explanation for the common law procedure."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"***Merck* [33]:** \"transnational comity and reciprocal respect among courts\" undergird recognition, including transnational issue estoppel."},{"t":"This is **balanced against** the recognising court's constitutional role in safeguarding the rule of law, which is why transnational issue estoppel needs special care ([33]–[34])."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-1.2"]},{"k":"step","t":"The open question: reciprocity"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Reciprocity means \"I will honour your courts' decisions if your courts honour mine.\" If the reason for honouring foreign judgments is respect between courts, it is natural to ask whether that respect should run both ways. The Court of Appeal raised that question but did not answer it."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"In legal terms, **reciprocity** means that country A's court will recognise or enforce a judgment of country B only if B's courts would do the same for A (C&Y 5.60). It underlies Singapore's statutory schemes and is mandatory in most civil law countries, but it is not a requirement of the common law. So the Court's remarks in *Merck* were \"quite unexpected\" (C&Y 5.60). The Court saw reciprocity as \"entirely consonant\" with comity, and observed that the absence of reciprocity would rarely be an obstacle in practice (*Merck* [39]; C&Y 5.61, 5.64 n 174)."},{"p":"Why has the common law not required it? Countries that impose reciprocity usually treat recognition as a matter of sovereignty. The common law treats it as enforcing private rights acquired under foreign law, which is why the obligation theory dominates there (C&Y 5.62). Prof Yeo's caution, which the Court noted, makes the same point. Moving to comity goes hand in hand with talk of reciprocity."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Reciprocity** ([39]; slide 15): **open**. If adopted, it would affect recognition and enforcement alike (C&Y 5.09).","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"**For:** consistent with a comity rationale and with the CCAA, REFJA and the Maintenance Orders (Reciprocal Enforcement) Act (C&Y 5.61)."},{"t":"**Prof Yeo's caution:** the common law focuses on personal obligations and the conduct of the party to be bound, while the statutes are concerned with international relations. Strict reciprocity is also falling out of favour worldwide."},{"t":"The common law treats recognition as enforcing private rights, not as a matter of sovereignty (C&Y 5.62)."}]}],"b":["open","miss"]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-1.6"]},{"k":"step","t":"Should Singapore require it? The critique and the spectrum"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Even \"you must respect us back\" can be strict or relaxed. A strict version says: \"Show me you have actually honoured one of our judgments.\" A relaxed version says: \"It is enough that you probably would.\""}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Chong & Yip argue against imposing reciprocity. Reciprocity is on the wane worldwide, even in civil law countries. It rarely persuades other countries to recognise the forum's judgments. And it punishes private parties for government policy they cannot control, by making them litigate all over again (C&Y 5.63). If Singapore did adopt it, the version would matter. The options run from strict (a treaty, or proof of actual past recognition) to relaxed (likely recognition, a presumption, or reciprocity in name only), and C&Y read *Merck* as pointing to a liberal version (C&Y 5.64)."}]},{"k":"call","c":"example","n":[{"p":"Mei sues Bob on a Country X judgment. X's courts have never been asked to enforce a Singapore judgment, but expert evidence shows that X's law would allow it. Under de facto reciprocity Mei would fail; under de jure or presumed reciprocity she would pass. No reciprocity requirement has yet been imposed in Singapore; *Merck* left the point open."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**C&Y critique** (5.60–5.64):","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"imposing reciprocity is contrary to the global trend;"},{"t":"it rarely succeeds in persuading other countries to recognise Singapore judgments;"},{"t":"it penalises private parties for government policy;"},{"t":"if it is ever imposed, expect a *liberal* version of reciprocity (5.64)."}]}],"b":["acad"]},{"t":"**The spectrum** (C&Y 5.64): a treaty or bilateral agreement; **de facto** (evidence that the foreign court has recognised a judgment of the court addressed); **de jure** (it is likely that it would); presumed unless the contrary is shown; in name only."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-1.4"]},{"k":"take","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"*Merck* considered obligation, comity and finality, and preferred comity: \"transnational comity and reciprocal respect among courts\" ([33])."},{"t":"Comity is balanced against the Singapore court's role in safeguarding the rule of law, which is why transnational issue estoppel needs special care."},{"t":"The obligation theory was not rejected and still explains why the creditor sues on a debt."},{"t":"Reciprocity is open. C&Y oppose it and expect a liberal version if it ever comes."}]}]},{"k":"traps","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"*Merck* did **not** impose reciprocity."},{"t":"*Merck* did **not** abolish the obligation theory."},{"t":"The counterweight to comity is the Singapore court's constitutional role, not the foreign court's sovereignty."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":true,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-1.7","LAW302-Q12-1.8"]}],"ic":"star","nq":8}]},{"id":"w12.s3","n":"3","title":"The common law requirements","sub":"Name the common law requirements for giving effect to a foreign judgment, say which applies only to enforcement, and take them in the right order.","lessons":[{"id":"w12.s3","code":"Stage 3","kind":"overview","t":"Overview: the common law requirements","blocks":[{"k":"obj","t":"Name the common law requirements for giving effect to a foreign judgment, say which applies only to enforcement, and take them in the right order."},{"k":"big","t":"Requirements first, defences second. The party relying on the judgment must tick every box on the checklist before the debtor's complaints (the defences, Week 13) come into play."},{"k":"step","t":"The checklist at the door"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Think of a checklist at the door. (1) Was the foreign court allowed to judge this person? (2) Is the decision finished? (3) Did it decide who is right? (4) If you want money, is it a fixed sum? Only when every box is ticked do we move on to the other side's complaints (the defences, in Week 13)."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Mei has won a judgment against Bob in Country X, which is not covered by REFJA or the CCAA, so she must use the common law (Stage 1). Before a Singapore court gives her judgment any effect, it must pass a short checklist: the common law requirements for recognition and enforcement, which the Court of Appeal has restated from *Humpuss* (*Ong Han Nam* [48]; *India v Deutsche Telekom* [64]–[65])."},{"p":"Each box has a purpose. Singapore gives effect to foreign judgments out of comity (respect between the courts of different countries) and to bring disputes to an end (Stage 2), but only on conditions. The foreign court must have been entitled, by Singapore's standards, to call Bob before it (**international jurisdiction**). Its own legal system must treat the decision as settled (**final and conclusive**). It must have decided the dispute itself, not a point of procedure (**on the merits**). And to make Bob pay, the judgment must be for a definite sum of money, because the common law collects foreign judgments as debts."},{"p":"Notice what is **not** on the list: whether the foreign court got the answer right. The Singapore court is not an appeal court and does not re-examine the merits (C&Y 5.66)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The checklist","sub":"*Humpuss*, cited in *Ong Han Nam* [48]; *India v Deutsche Telekom* [64]–[65]; C&Y 5.20; slide 3","n":[{"ol":[{"t":"The judgment is **on the merits**."},{"t":"For enforcement only: a **fresh money judgment** that is not a tax, fine or penalty."},{"t":"The foreign court had **international jurisdiction** under Singapore conflict rules."},{"t":"The judgment is **final and conclusive**."},{"t":"(Reciprocity? This is open.)","b":["open"]},{"t":"**No defences** (Week 13)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-10.1"]},{"k":"step","t":"Sword or shield: recognition asks for less"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Mei's judgment is like a winning ticket. She can **cash it in** (make Bob pay) or **hold it up as a shield** (\"this was already decided, stop suing me\"). Holding it up needs only a genuine, finished ticket. Cashing it in also needs a fixed amount of money printed on it."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"In legal terms, the shield is **recognition**: the court treats the judgment as res judicata (a matter already decided, which the same parties cannot reopen), usually through an estoppel (a rule that stops a party re-arguing it). The sword is **enforcement**: the court helps Mei collect. Recognition is a prerequisite to enforcement, and the requirements are the same for both except the fresh money judgment requirement (item 2 on the checklist), which applies to enforcement only (C&Y 5.08–5.09, 5.20)."},{"p":"The reason lies in how the common law enforces. Mei sues Bob in Singapore on the obligation the foreign judgment created, as a debt (C&Y 5.12). A debt claim needs a sum of money; recognition needs no debt. So a declaratory judgment, or an order to do something, cannot be enforced but can still be recognised (C&Y 5.11; Module 3.3)."},{"p":"The reciprocity item (5 on the checklist) carries a question mark. Reciprocity (giving effect only to judgments from countries that give effect to Singapore's) was raised in *Merck* [39] but not decided."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Recognition is a prerequisite to enforcement. The same requirements apply to both, except the fresh money judgment requirement, which applies to enforcement only (C&Y 5.09, 5.20)."},{"t":"Recognition needs no special procedure: the normal pleading rules apply (C&Y 5.10; *Merck* [35]). Declaratory judgments cannot be enforced; their value lies in creating res judicata (C&Y 5.11)."},{"t":"More specific requirements apply when a party argues an estoppel (C&Y 5.20; Stage 4)."},{"t":"Reciprocity as an additional requirement, for recognition and enforcement alike, was flagged in *Merck* [39] and left undecided (C&Y 5.09, 5.20; Stage 2).","b":["open"]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-10.2"]},{"k":"step","t":"The jurisdiction decision tree"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Was Country X's court allowed to summon Bob at all? Broadly, yes if Bob was **there** when he was served with the case, or if he **agreed** to the court deciding it, by taking part or by promising in advance."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"International jurisdiction carries the most law, so it has its own decision tree. Its starting point matters most: Singapore asks whether, by **Singapore's** conflict of laws rules (its rules for cases with a foreign element), the foreign court was entitled to summon this defendant. What the foreign court's own law said about its jurisdiction is irrelevant (*Giant Light* [20]; *India v Deutsche Telekom* [65]). The tree then asks about presence (and, an open question, residence), and failing that about submission: by conduct, by agreement, or to related claims. If neither applies, there was no international jurisdiction. Modules 3.4 and 3.5 work through each branch and its question marks."}]},{"k":"flow","title":"International jurisdiction flowchart","g":{"root":"A","nodes":{"A":{"t":"Did the foreign court have international jurisdiction (judged by Singapore rules)?","q":false},"B":{"t":"Was the defendant present (or resident?) in the foreign country at the time of service?","q":true},"B1":{"t":"Voluntary physical presence, even temporary (Adams v Cape 519). A website is not presence (Lucasfilm)","q":false},"B2":{"t":"Fixed place of business + own business carried on there for more than a minimal period, directly or through a representative (Adams v Cape 530-531)","q":false},"C":{"t":"Did the defendant submit?","q":true},"C1":{"t":"A step that necessarily involves waiving the objection to jurisdiction (WSG Nimbus [54]): suing, counterclaiming, defending on the merits. Henry v Geoprosco? (cf IGCF v Al Jomaih, 2025)","q":false},"C2":{"t":"Prior consent, express or implied (Vizcaya). Is implied consent accepted in Singapore? Open","q":false},"C3":{"t":"Inchoate submission: same or related claims, if fair (Giant Light; Murthy)","q":false},"D":{"t":"No international jurisdiction. Categories closed? (Schibsby v Westenholz; cf Beals v Saldanha in Canada)","q":false}},"edges":[["A","B",""],["B","B1","Individual"],["B","B2","Company"],["B","C","No"],["C","C1","By conduct"],["C","C2","By agreement"],["C","C3","Related claims"],["C","D","None"]]}},{"k":"step","t":"The order of analysis"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Before a bank pays out on a cheque, the teller checks the basics: is it genuine, signed and for a stated amount? Only if it passes does the bank listen to the account holder saying \"stop, I was tricked into writing it\". The person presenting the cheque must get it past the checks; the person objecting must prove the objection."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"A problem question on a foreign judgment has a natural order."},{"p":"**First, the regime.** If Mei's judgment comes from a recognised court of a REFJA-gazetted country and falls within the Act, she must register it: REFJA s 7(1) bars other proceedings to recover the sum. The CCAA, by contrast, does not exclude the common law (s 21) (Stage 1)."},{"p":"**Second, the requirements**, which the party relying on the judgment must establish. On a summary judgment application (a request for judgment without a full trial), the burden is on the creditor (C&Y 5.13)."},{"p":"**Third, the defences**, which the judgment debtor must prove (C&Y 5.65; Week 13)."},{"p":"**Fourth, the effect:** an estoppel if Mei uses the judgment as a shield (Stage 4), or a Singapore judgment she can enforce if she sues for money (C&Y 5.12)."},{"p":"A judgment that fails a requirement never needs a defence. That is why Bob's lawyer will test the requirements, above all international jurisdiction, before alleging fraud."}]},{"k":"call","c":"tip","n":[{"p":"Use the checklist as your headings. For each item, say who must prove it and whether it is genuinely in doubt on the facts. Deal briefly with the easy items, spend your time on the one in dispute, and only then turn to the defences."}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-10.3"]},{"k":"take","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"At common law the judgment must be on the merits, from a court with international jurisdiction under Singapore's rules, final and conclusive, and free of any defence."},{"t":"Enforcement adds a fresh money judgment that is not a tax, fine or penalty. Recognition does not need it."},{"t":"Reciprocity is an open question, not an established requirement (*Merck* [39])."},{"t":"Work in order: regime, requirements (the creditor shows), defences (the debtor proves), effect."}]}]},{"k":"traps","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Do not apply the money requirement to recognition."},{"t":"Do not treat a lack of international jurisdiction as a defence for Bob to prove. It is a requirement."},{"t":"Do not judge international jurisdiction by the foreign court's own law, or ask whether the foreign court was right."}]}]}],"ic":"check","nq":3},{"id":"w12.m3.1","code":"3.1","kind":"module","t":"Judgment on the merits (including limitation)","blocks":[{"k":"obj","t":"Decide whether a foreign decision is \"on the merits\", including default judgments, interlocutory decisions and decisions based on limitation."},{"k":"big","t":"Singapore gives effect to a foreign court's answer to the dispute itself. A ruling on procedure alone answers nothing about who is right, so it fails the first item on the checklist."},{"k":"step","t":"What \"on the merits\" means"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Think of a referee's notebook. Some entries record the result: who won, and why. Others are housekeeping: kick-off was moved, a team handed in its line-up late. Singapore only gives effect to the first kind. The foreign court must have actually decided **who is right**, not just a housekeeping point such as how evidence should be filed."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"In legal terms, the foreign judgment must be a judgment **on the merits**. The merits are the substance of the dispute: what happened, what the law says about it, and so who is entitled to what. The classic description comes from Lord Brandon in the House of Lords in *The Sennar (No 2)*. A decision on the merits establishes certain facts as proved or not in dispute, states the relevant principles of law, and expresses a conclusion on applying that law to those facts. By contrast, \"a decision on procedure alone is not a decision on the merits\"."},{"p":"Why insist on this? Singapore recognises foreign judgments out of respect for another court's resolution of a dispute, and to bring that dispute to an end (Stage 2). A ruling about how evidence should be filed resolves nothing between Mei and Bob, so there is nothing for Singapore to respect or treat as settled."},{"p":"Chong & Yip's fuller formulation from the same case (below) also requires that the judgment cannot be reopened by the court that gave it. That overlaps with finality (Module 3.6), which is why the two are often stated together: \"a final and conclusive judgment (on the merits)\" (*Ong Han Nam* [48]). Keep one distinction clear, though. \"On the merits\" asks what **kind** of decision the foreign court made, not whether it was **right** (C&Y 5.66)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Definition** (C&Y 5.22; *The Sennar (No 2)*, slide 4). Lord Brandon: \"a decision on procedure alone is not a decision on the merits\". A decision on the merits \"establishes certain facts as proved or not in dispute; states what are the relevant principles of law … and expresses a conclusion …\"."},{"t":"C&Y 5.22, quoting *The Sennar (No 2)*: the court has held that it has jurisdiction to adjudicate on an issue raised in the cause of action, and its judgment on that cause of action cannot be varied, reopened or set aside by the court that delivered it or any court of co-ordinate jurisdiction, although it may be subject to appeal to a higher court."},{"t":"Whether the decision was correct is irrelevant: Singapore does not review the merits (C&Y 5.66; Week 13)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-2.7"]},{"k":"step","t":"Default and interlocutory judgments"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"If Bob is told about a match and simply does not turn up, the referee can award the game to Mei. Bob cannot later say \"but nobody saw me play\". He chose not to. A court that gives judgment because the defendant ignored the case has still decided the claim."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"A **default judgment** is one given because the defendant did not defend: he did not appear, or did not file a defence in time. It counts as a judgment on the merits, even though the defendant took no part and the court may not have applied its mind to the evidence (C&Y 5.23). The reason is the one in the analogy: the loser chose not to take part. The analogy assumes Bob was properly told of the case. If he was not, he may have a natural justice defence (a complaint that he was denied a fair hearing: Week 13), but that is a separate question from whether the judgment is on the merits."}]},{"k":"call","c":"why","n":[{"p":"Not every legal system agrees. Chong & Yip point to Indian law as a contrast, and note that Singapore courts, aware that a default judgment given without consideration of the evidence may not be enforced abroad, have been prepared to hold a full trial at the claimant's request (C&Y 5.23, n 61)."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"An **interlocutory** decision is one made along the way, before the final judgment. It can still be on the merits, provided it is not on a \"procedural\" issue (C&Y 5.23). So a ruling at an interlocutory hearing that finally decides whether a contract clause is valid can qualify; a ruling that extends the time for filing evidence cannot. The Court of Appeal in *Lakshmi* [101] accepted that issue estoppel (a bar on re-arguing a specific point already decided between the same parties) can, in principle, arise from foreign interlocutory decisions. Module 4.2 shows the limits."}]},{"k":"call","c":"tip","n":[{"p":"When a debtor says \"it was only a default judgment\", the merits point is a dead end. The live issue is often international jurisdiction. A defendant who stayed away did not submit by conduct in those proceedings, so ask whether he was present in the foreign country, had agreed in advance, or had taken part in earlier proceedings on the same or related claims (inchoate submission: the Chinese judgment in *Giant Light* was a default judgment, yet consent was imputed; Modules 3.4 and 3.5)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Default judgments** are on the merits, even though the court may not have examined the evidence (C&Y 5.23)."},{"t":"**Interlocutory judgments** can be on the merits, provided the decision is not on a \"procedural\" issue (C&Y 5.23). *Lakshmi* [101] accepts that issue estoppel can in principle arise from foreign interlocutory decisions."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-2.1"]},{"k":"step","t":"Limitation: a time-bar ruling counts (FLPA s 5)"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"A shop that refuses your refund because you came back after the 30-day return window has given you a real answer: no refund. A court that dismisses a claim because it was brought too late has likewise answered the claim. (The analogy is loose: a return window is the shop's own rule, while a limitation period is set by law. The point is only that \"too late\" is a real answer.)"}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"A **limitation** rule says a claim must be brought within a fixed time; a claim brought later is \"time-barred\". An older view treated limitation as procedural, which would make a time-bar dismissal look like a decision \"on procedure alone\". Singapore has settled the point by statute. Under **FLPA s 5**, a foreign court that decided a matter wholly or partly by reference to the law of limitation, whether its own country's, another country's or Singapore's, is *deemed* to have decided it on the merits. This fits the modern view that limitation is a matter of substance (C&Y 5.24)."}]},{"k":"call","c":"example","n":[{"p":"Bob sues Mei in Country X on a loan. The X court dismisses the claim as time-barred under X law. Bob then sues Mei on the same loan in Singapore. Mei pleads the X judgment as a cause of action estoppel (a bar on suing again on the same claim between the same parties: Stage 4). Under FLPA s 5 the X decision is on the merits, so, if the other requirements are met and no defence applies, Bob's Singapore claim fails."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Without that rule, a claimant who lost abroad for suing too late could simply try again in Singapore. Do not confuse it with a different time limit: the six years Mei has to bring a common law action on a foreign judgment (Module 3.6). That limit governs enforcement actions. It says nothing about whether the foreign decision was on the merits."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**FLPA s 5:** a foreign court that decided a matter wholly or partly by reference to the law of limitation of \"that or any other country (including Singapore)\" is *deemed* to have decided it on the merits. This fits the modern view that limitation is a matter of substance (C&Y 5.24)."},{"t":"**A separate rule:** a common law action on a foreign judgment must be brought within six years under LA s 6(1)(a), running from when the judgment became final and conclusive under the foreign law (*Sang Cheol Woo* [43]–[48]). The six-year limit does not apply to recognition (C&Y 5.98)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-2.2"]},{"k":"take","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"\"On the merits\" means the foreign court decided the substance: it found facts, stated the law and reached a conclusion (*The Sennar (No 2)*). Procedure alone does not count."},{"t":"Default judgments count, even if the court never examined the evidence (C&Y 5.23)."},{"t":"Interlocutory decisions count if they are not on a procedural issue (C&Y 5.23; *Lakshmi* [101])."},{"t":"A foreign decision made by reference to any country's limitation law, Singapore's included, is deemed to be on the merits (FLPA s 5)."}]}]},{"k":"traps","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Do not argue that a default judgment fails this requirement because no evidence was heard."},{"t":"Do not call a time-bar ruling \"procedural\" to escape it. FLPA s 5 deems it to be on the merits."},{"t":"Do not confuse FLPA s 5 with the six-year limit for suing on a foreign judgment (LA s 6(1)(a))."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":true,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-2.8","LAW302-Q12-2.9"]}],"ic":"book","nq":5},{"id":"w12.m3.2","code":"3.2","kind":"module","t":"A judgment on a judgment","blocks":[{"k":"obj","t":"Explain why Singapore is unlikely to enforce a foreign judgment that merely declares another foreign judgment enforceable, and how REFJA deals with such judgments."},{"k":"big","t":"Singapore decides for itself whether the original judgment deserves effect here. It will not let a second foreign court's stamp of approval stand in for that check."},{"k":"step","t":"What a \"judgment on a judgment\" is"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Mei wins against Bob in Country A. She takes that win to Country B, whose court says: \"Country A's judgment can be enforced here in B.\" Then Mei asks Singapore to enforce B's judgment. That is like asking a bank to cash a photocopy of a cheque that a second office has stamped \"accepted here\". The stamp shows the photocopy was accepted in B. It tells you nothing about whether the original cheque was good."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"A judgment on a judgment arises when a second court does nothing more than declare that a first court's judgment can be enforced in the second court's territory. The second court does not hear the dispute again. It only checks that the first judgment passes its own local rules for enforcing foreign judgments (C&Y 5.25, 5.27)."},{"p":"Why would a creditor bring the second judgment to Singapore rather than the first? Usually because the first one has a weakness here: perhaps the first court's jurisdiction over Bob is doubtful by Singapore's standards. The question is whether Singapore should treat the second court's declaration as a fresh judgment it can enforce. The High Court's answer in *Sang Cheol Woo* is no."},{"p":"That case shows both routes side by side. Woo held a Korean judgment against Spackman. He also obtained a New York judgment declaring the Korean judgment enforceable in New York, against assets there. In Singapore he sued on both. On the Korean judgment, the court rejected Spackman's time-bar, natural justice and public policy objections ([48], [95], [101]). On the New York judgment, it refused enforcement ([106]–[112])."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"***Sang Cheol Woo* [106]–[112]:** the New York judgment only declared the Korean judgment enforceable in New York, against assets in New York. The Singapore High Court **refused** to enforce it.","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"*Morgan Stanley v Pilot Lead* (HK), which allowed a Singapore judgment registering an English judgment to be registered in Hong Kong, was not followed: no principled basis was offered for it [109], and it has been criticised in Hong Kong itself (*Motorola v Uzan*) [111]."},{"t":"Countries' recognition requirements differ, so a judgment recognised elsewhere would not necessarily be recognised in Singapore if brought here directly [110]."}]}]},{"t":"**Contrast:** at first instance in *Desert Palace v Poh Soon Kiat*, the High Court saw no difficulty in enforcing a judgment on a judgment at common law, but did not grapple with the conceptual problems. The Court of Appeal reversed that decision on another ground (it was not a fresh money judgment: *Poh Soon Kiat*, Module 3.3) without considering this point (C&Y 5.26)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-2.11","LAW302-Q12-2.3"]},{"k":"step","t":"Why Singapore looks past the stamp"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"A second office's stamp only says what that office is willing to accept. It cannot tell another bank whether the original cheque was good. And if stamps counted, anyone holding a doubtful cheque would simply shop around for the office with the sloppiest checks."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Chong & Yip give three reasons against enforcing a judgment on a judgment: two of legal principle and one of policy (C&Y 5.27)."},{"p":"First, it is not a judgment **on the merits**. The second court never asked who was right; it only asked whether the first judgment met its own enforcement rules. Second, the second court decided only what effect the first judgment has **inside its own territory**. Each country decides that question for itself under its own conflict rules, so B's answer cannot bind Singapore. The Court of Appeal made a similar point in *Merck*: a foreign judgment that is territorially limited cannot found an estoppel (a rule that stops a party re-arguing a point already decided). Third, there is a **laundering** risk. A creditor could route a weak judgment through a country with relaxed enforcement rules and present the \"clean\" result here. Singapore would then be testing jurisdiction, finality and defences against the intermediate judgment rather than the original."},{"p":"The second reason fits one of the \"outer limits\" of transnational issue estoppel that the Court of Appeal sketched, provisionally, in *Merck*. Some issues are for the Singapore court alone, including issues that are procedural for conflict of laws purposes. The Court's example is close to ours: a ruling by Country Y's court that Country X's judgment creates an issue estoppel does not bind Singapore (*Merck* [55]; Module 4.2)."}]},{"k":"call","c":"example","n":[{"p":"Mei's Country A judgment rests on jurisdiction that Singapore does not accept: Bob was never present in Country A and never took part in the case. If Country B's rules are more relaxed and its court declares the judgment enforceable, Mei might hope Singapore will look only at B's judgment. The laundering argument is the reason Singapore should not let her."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**C&Y's three arguments against** (5.27):","c":[{"ol":[{"t":"A judgment on a judgment is not a judgment on the **merits**."},{"t":"The second court decided only the first judgment's effect **within its own territory**. Compare *Merck* [56]: a territorially limited judgment cannot create an estoppel."},{"t":"**Laundering:** Singapore would test jurisdiction, finality and defences against the intermediate judgment instead of the original one."}]}],"b":["acad"]},{"t":"*Merck* [55] (a provisional view of the \"outer limits\"; Module 4.2): an issue that is procedural for conflict of laws purposes is for the forum to decide, so a Country Y ruling that a Country X judgment creates an issue estoppel does not bind Singapore (C&Y 5.110–5.111)."},{"t":"Concern about laundering is what led the UK to bar registration of judgments on judgments under its 1933 Act (*Clarke v Fennoscandia*; C&Y 5.26)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-2.4"]},{"k":"step","t":"The statutes close the door"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"The statute simply puts up a sign at its counter: \"No stamped photocopies.\""}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"REFJA deals with the problem expressly. Registration under REFJA lets a creditor holding a judgment of a recognised court of a gazetted country (a country listed by Ministerial order) have it enforced here without a fresh action (Stage 1). Section 3(4) keeps three kinds of judgment out of that route, and the third is the judgment on a judgment. The UK prohibition prompted by laundering concerns has been carried into the amended REFJA (C&Y 5.26). That leaves the common law as the only place the question can still arise (C&Y 5.27), and *Sang Cheol Woo* has answered it there too."}]},{"k":"call","c":"why","n":[{"p":"The creditor's real route is the original judgment. Woo's own case shows it: his New York judgment failed, but his claim on the Korean judgment survived Spackman's objections. If Mei's original judgment cannot pass Singapore's checklist, routing it through Country B will not save it."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**REFJA s 3(4)** excludes three kinds of judgment of a recognised court of a gazetted country (C&Y 5.158):","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"(a) a judgment given on appeal from a court that is not a recognised court;"},{"t":"(b) a judgment or other instrument regarded, for enforcement purposes, as a judgment of that court but given or made in another foreign country;"},{"t":"(c) a judgment given in proceedings founded on a judgment of a court in another foreign country and having as their object the enforcement of that judgment. This is the judgment on a judgment (slide 21; C&Y 5.25)."}]}]},{"t":"The RECJA has been repealed. Even under the UK model it copied, the English Court of Appeal held a judgment on a judgment was not registrable, because allowing it would let in judgments of non-reciprocating countries, and the Act assumed the registering court could check the proceedings on the merits (*Strategic Technologies*; C&Y 5.135)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-2.5"]},{"k":"take","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"A judgment on a judgment only declares another judgment enforceable in the second court's territory."},{"t":"A Singapore court is unlikely to enforce it at common law: the High Court in *Sang Cheol Woo* refused to, and the Court of Appeal has not decided the point. REFJA excludes it from registration (s 3(4)(c))."},{"t":"The reasons: it is not on the merits, it speaks only for the second court's territory, and it would allow laundering (C&Y 5.27)."},{"t":"The creditor's route is to rely on the **original** judgment, which must itself meet Singapore's requirements."}]}]},{"k":"traps","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Do not treat a foreign court's declaration that another judgment is enforceable as a new judgment on the merits."},{"t":"Do not assume that a judgment recognised in one country will be recognised in Singapore."},{"t":"Do not cite *Poh Soon Kiat* as Court of Appeal approval of judgments on judgments. The point was not considered on appeal."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":true,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-2.6","LAW302-Q12-2.10"]}],"ic":"cards","nq":6},{"id":"w12.m3.3","code":"3.3","kind":"module","t":"A fresh money judgment (enforcement only)","blocks":[{"k":"obj","t":"Apply the money-judgment requirement, including to costs orders and to judgments that mix money and non-money orders, and explain what a creditor can do with a non-money judgment."},{"k":"big","t":"At common law Singapore enforces a foreign judgment by letting the creditor sue on it as a debt. A debt claim needs a definite sum of money that the judgment itself orders the debtor to pay. A non-money judgment can still be recognised, if it meets the other requirements, but it cannot be enforced."},{"k":"step","t":"Why money, and why only for enforcement"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"To cash in at common law, the ticket must show a fixed sum of money, and not a fine or a tax. An order to *do* something, such as handing over shares or stopping some conduct, cannot be cashed in this way, although Singapore can still recognise it."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"At common law Mei cannot register the foreign judgment, as she could under REFJA. She sues in Singapore on the obligation it created, as a debt (C&Y 5.12; Stage 1). Historically that action was *indebitatus assumpsit*, an old form of action for recovering a definite sum of money, and the money requirement is its legacy. A debt action can recover a sum. It cannot make Bob hand over shares or stop selling goods."},{"p":"Recognition involves no debt: it only treats the judgment as res judicata (a matter already decided, which the parties cannot reopen). So the requirement applies to enforcement only (C&Y 5.09, 5.20)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"The fresh money judgment requirement applies to enforcement, not recognition (C&Y 5.09, 5.20, 5.28)."},{"t":"The creditor sues on the obligation created by the foreign judgment. The refusal to enforce non-money judgments traces back to the action of *indebitatus assumpsit* for definite sums (C&Y 5.12, 5.31; *Giant Light* [62]–[63])."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-3.11"]},{"k":"step","t":"A fixed and ascertainable sum"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"A bill that says \"S$100, plus interest at 5% a year until you pay\" is still a bill for a fixed sum: anyone with a calculator can work out what is owed on a given day. What matters is that the judgment itself fixes the amount, or a formula for it."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"In legal terms, the judgment must be for a **fixed and ascertainable** sum, and simple arithmetic is enough. The Singapore court does not reassess the amount, because that would be reviewing the merits. The sum must also not be a tax, fine or other penalty: Singapore will not act as a foreign State's collector (Week 13 covers this rule)."},{"p":"A **costs order** (an order that one party pay the other's legal costs) can be enforced in its own right if it is final and for a definite sum, even where the main judgment ordered no money at all. So if Bob's claim against Mei in Country X is dismissed and he is ordered to pay her costs in a fixed amount, Mei can sue for those costs in Singapore."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"C&Y 5.28–5.30","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"The judgment must be for a **fixed and ascertainable** sum, and not a tax, fine or penalty. A sum that can be worked out by simple arithmetic counts as ascertained (C&Y 5.28)."},{"t":"A final **costs order** for a definite sum is enforceable, even if the main judgment did not order a sum of money (C&Y 5.30; *Giant Light* [74], citing *Russell v Smyth*)."},{"t":"In *Sang Cheol Woo* [100], the penalty principle was said to concern sums payable to the State. Punitive damages awarded to a private party raise a separate, less settled question (Week 13)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-3.1","LAW302-Q12-3.7","LAW302-Q12-3.6"]},{"k":"step","t":"\"Fresh\": the judgment itself must order payment (*Poh Soon Kiat*)"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"A letter from your bank saying \"you still owe us the balance on your loan\" is not a new loan. It only reminds you of the old one. A foreign judgment that merely repeats that an existing debt is still owed does not create a fresh obligation to pay."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"In *Poh Soon Kiat*, Desert Palace, which ran a Las Vegas casino, obtained a 1999 default judgment in Nevada against Poh for a US$2m gambling debt, and then a Californian judgment founded on it, also in 1999. In 2001 a Californian court set aside a fraudulent transfer of Poh's interest in a Californian property (a transfer that put it out of the creditor's reach), ordered the interest sold to satisfy the 1999 judgment, and said Poh \"shall remain liable\" for any shortfall. Desert Palace sued in Singapore on the 2001 judgment. The High Court enforced it; the Court of Appeal overruled the High Court."},{"p":"Two lessons follow. Look at what the judgment does in substance, not at words that echo a debt. And whether a judgment is a \"fresh\" money judgment is decided by the law of the court that gave it. (The High Court decision, reported as *Desert Palace*, reappears in Module 3.2: the Court of Appeal reversed it on this point without considering the judgment-on-a-judgment question.)"}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**\"Fresh\" money judgment, as determined by the law of the court of origin** (*Poh Soon Kiat*; slide 5; C&Y 5.29)."},{"t":"**Holding:** the 2001 Californian judgment was **not** a fresh money judgment. In substance it set aside a fraudulent transfer and made consequential orders, and \"shall remain liable\" was mere surplusage (words that add nothing), because the liability already existed under the 1999 judgment (*Poh Soon Kiat* [27], [30]–[32])."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-3.12","LAW302-Q12-3.2"]},{"k":"step","t":"Money mixed with other orders (*Giant Light*)"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Picture a court unwinding a sale: \"you hand back the goods, the seller hands back the price\". If the refund does not depend on something you have failed to do, the seller cannot refuse to pay just because the judgment also mentions the goods. The money part can still be cashed in."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"That is *Giant Light*. A Chinese company bought two generator sets from a Singapore company, found them unsatisfactory and sued in China. The Chinese court gave a default judgment rescinding the contract: the buyer was to return the sets, and the seller was to refund the price and pay damages. When the buyer sued on the judgment in Singapore, the seller argued that the \"return\" order meant this was not a judgment for a definite sum. The High Court disagreed. The buyer only had to make the sets available for collection, which it had done, so the seller's duty to pay had fallen due. Reasoning by analogy with costs orders, the court held that a definite sum ordered alongside other orders can found an action in debt. Chong & Yip call the result \"surely correct\" on those facts (C&Y 5.32)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"*Giant Light* [55]–[79]; C&Y 5.32","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"The **money order was enforceable**. The buyer's obligation to \"return\" the sets only meant making them available for collection, which it had done [68]–[71]."},{"t":"By analogy with costs orders, \"where a foreign court makes an order for payment of a definite sum of money … amongst other orders, the order … should be capable of founding an action in debt\" [75]."},{"t":"The seller's argument that the non-money order prevented the judgment being a money judgment was rejected [72]–[76]."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-3.3"]},{"k":"step","t":"Severance: the road not taken in *Giant Light*"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Severance is like cutting a bruise out of an apple and eating the rest. A court can cut out the objectionable part of a judgment and give effect to the remainder, as long as the parts can be cleanly separated."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Severance usually comes up where part of a judgment offends the rule against enforcing foreign penal, revenue or other public laws; in *Rodriguez Licea* [28], for example, the High Court severed non-compensatory damages and enforced the compensatory damages (Week 13). In *Giant Light* the claimant argued, in the alternative, that the non-money order could be severed. The court did not need severance, and added (obiter) that it would not be appropriate, because the Chinese orders did not engage the penal-law rule."},{"p":"Chong & Yip ask why the penal-law rule should be the only trigger, since severance makes more judgments enforceable. They accept that refusing it on the facts could be justified: both parties' experts said the obligations were to be performed simultaneously, so they were interdependent. But that was not the court's reason. On the present law, they suggest, a judgment mixing money and non-money orders may not be enforceable where no penal law is involved, and severance should be possible whenever the orders are not interdependent. Read this alongside the main holding, which Chong & Yip endorse (C&Y 5.32): where a definite sum is ordered amongst other orders and payment does not depend on anything the creditor has failed to do, the money order founds an action in debt without any need for severance (*Giant Light* [75]). Severance matters only where that route is not available."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"A court may sever a judgment and recognise and enforce only its unobjectionable part, if the parts can be separated and identified. The issue usually arises under the rule against enforcing foreign penal, revenue or other public laws (C&Y 5.99; *Rodriguez Licea* [28])."},{"t":"*Giant Light* [77]–[78]: severance was **not** needed, and the court doubted it was available because the non-money order did not engage the rule against enforcing foreign penal laws.","b":["obiter"]},{"t":"C&Y 5.101–5.102: severance should be possible whenever the money and non-money orders are not interdependent. Refusing it in *Giant Light* could be justified because the obligations were to be performed simultaneously [71], though that was not the court's reason.","b":["acad"]},{"t":"Under REFJA, only the parts of a judgment that meet the Act's requirements may be registered (C&Y 5.154). Under the CCAA, s 19 requires the court to recognise a severable part (Week 13)."}]}]},{"k":"step","t":"Non-money judgments: recognised, not enforced"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"A ticket that says \"Bob must hand over the painting\" cannot be cashed at the common law counter. But Mei can still show it to prove the point was decided. She can bring her original claim again in Singapore and say: \"A court has already decided this, so Bob cannot argue it again.\" That gets her to much the same place by a longer road."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"In legal terms, a non-money judgment, such as an injunction (an order to do or not do something) or an order for specific performance (an order to carry out a contract), cannot be enforced at common law, but it can be recognised. The longer road works because a foreign judgment does not merge the original claim (merger is the rule that a claim is extinguished once it becomes a judgment; on the Court of Appeal's preliminary view it does not extend to foreign judgments). The remedy is then for Singapore law as the lex fori (the law of the court hearing the case): the same relief, or a close local equivalent, is likely but not guaranteed."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Non-money judgments are not enforceable at common law** (C&Y 5.31). The rule has historical roots in the old action of *indebitatus assumpsit* (*Giant Light* [62]–[63]).","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"They **can** still be recognised."},{"t":"The creditor can sue afresh on the original cause of action and plead the judgment as res judicata (no merger: *Ocean Tankers* [95]–[96]; C&Y 5.105), which gives a similar result by a longer route. Remedies are for the lex fori, so identical relief is likely but not guaranteed (C&Y 5.31)."},{"t":"Comparisons:","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"**REFJA s 4(4):** since the 2019 amendments, a non-money judgment (for example a freezing order, which stops a party dealing with its assets, an injunction or an order for specific performance) can be registered if \"just and convenient\", having regard to the circumstances and the nature of the relief; otherwise the monetary equivalent may be registered, assessed on performance in Singapore. Factors named in Parliament: whether Singapore law has an equivalent remedy; whether an injunction is territorially limited to the foreign country; whether enforcement risks an illegality or a breach of privilege (C&Y 5.153, 5.156)."},{"t":"**CCAA:** non-money judgments are covered."},{"t":"**Canada:** *Pro-Swing v Elta* accepts enforcement of non-money judgments in principle, and a handful of other common law courts have followed (C&Y 5.31)."}]}]},{"t":"**C&Y 5.21 and 5.31:** the common law is \"distinctly lagging behind\" the statutes. Since much the same result can be reached by the longer route, it would be more efficient to enforce non-money judgments directly, and convergence (compare *Merck* [37] on defences) points the same way.","b":["acad","miss"]}]}]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-3.4"]},{"k":"take","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Enforcement at common law needs a fixed and ascertainable sum that is not a tax, fine or penalty. Simple arithmetic is enough, and final costs orders qualify."},{"t":"The judgment itself must impose the obligation to pay, judged by the law of the court of origin (*Poh Soon Kiat*)."},{"t":"A money order made alongside other orders can be enforced if payment does not depend on something the creditor has failed to do (*Giant Light*)."},{"t":"Non-money judgments can be recognised but not enforced at common law. REFJA and the CCAA go further."}]}]},{"k":"traps","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Do not say the whole judgment fails just because it contains a non-money order (*Giant Light*)."},{"t":"Whether the judgment is a \"fresh\" money judgment is a question for the **foreign** law."},{"t":"Do not treat words such as \"shall remain liable\" as creating a new debt. Look at what the judgment does in substance (*Poh Soon Kiat*)."},{"t":"Do not apply REFJA's \"just and convenient\" test in a common law action."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":true,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-3.8","LAW302-Q12-3.9","LAW302-Q12-3.10","LAW302-Q12-3.5"]}],"ic":"coins","nq":12},{"id":"w12.m3.4","code":"3.4","kind":"module","t":"International jurisdiction (1): presence and residence","blocks":[{"k":"obj","t":"Apply the presence and residence ground to individuals and to companies."},{"k":"big","t":"Singapore asks whether, by **Singapore's** conflict rules, the foreign court was entitled to summon this defendant. How the foreign court actually took jurisdiction under its own law is irrelevant (*Giant Light* [20]; *India v DT* [65])."},{"k":"step","t":"Whose rules decide? Singapore's"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"A referee from another league sends Bob off and fines him. Before Singapore helps collect the fine, it asks: by **our** rulebook, was this referee entitled to take charge of Bob at all? The other league's rulebook may say yes, but every league can write its own rules as widely as it likes."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"A foreign court can have jurisdiction under its own law and still lack **international jurisdiction**: jurisdiction that the court asked to recognise its judgment will accept. You will also see it described as being \"a court of competent jurisdiction\", or having jurisdiction \"in the international sense\" (C&Y 5.33)."},{"p":"In legal terms, Singapore tests the foreign court's jurisdiction by **Singapore's** private international law (its conflict of laws rules for cross-border cases). The foreign court's own jurisdiction rules do not decide the question, though Module 3.5 shows its procedural rules can explain what a defendant's step meant. One way to see why: if a foreign court's own say-so were enough, any country could make its judgments enforceable here simply by giving its courts wide powers."},{"p":"The four grounds below come down to two ideas. Either the defendant was **there** (presence, this lesson), or the defendant **accepted** the court, for example by suing there or by fighting the case on the merits (three forms of submission, Module 3.5)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"International jurisdiction is judged by Singapore private international law, not by the foreign court's own rules (*Giant Light* [20], citing *Buchanan v Rucker*; *India v DT* [65]; C&Y 5.33)."},{"t":"***India v Deutsche Telekom* [65]: four grounds of international jurisdiction:**","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"(a) **presence** in the foreign country;"},{"t":"(b) filing a claim or counterclaim there;"},{"t":"(c) voluntary submission by appearing;"},{"t":"(d) a prior agreement to submit."}]}]},{"t":"C&Y reduce these to two criteria: the defendant's presence or residence within the foreign court's jurisdiction at the relevant time, or the defendant's submission (C&Y 5.33)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-4.1","LAW302-Q12-4.2"]},{"k":"step","t":"Individuals: present when served"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"When you stay at a friend's home, you follow the house rules while you are there, even for a weekend. Countries work the same way. If Bob is in Country X, even on a short trip, X's courts can call him to answer a claim, provided he is served while he is there and he came of his own free will."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"The simplest ground is presence. Its rationale is a kind of temporary allegiance: someone in a country has the benefit of its laws, so must accept its courts while there. Three refinements make the rule precise."},{"p":"**When.** At the date of **service** (formal delivery of the court papers to the defendant), not the date the claimant issued the proceedings."},{"p":"**How long.** Any length. There is no minimum stay for an individual."},{"p":"**How it came about.** Voluntarily. Someone tricked or forced into a country has not chosen to put himself under its laws, so the allegiance rationale does not apply."}]},{"k":"call","c":"example","n":[{"p":"Mei issues proceedings in Country X on 1 March, while Bob is on holiday there. He flies home on 3 March and is served in Singapore on 5 March. The presence ground fails: by the date of service, Bob had left X."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Timing:** presence is tested at the time of suit, which means the date of **service**, not the date proceedings were issued (*Adams* at 518; C&Y 5.34)."},{"t":"**Voluntary presence suffices, even if temporary**, \"whether or not accompanied by residence\" (*Adams v Cape* at 519). Voluntary means no element of compulsion, fraud or duress (*Adams* at 518–519; C&Y 5.35)."},{"t":"**Rationale:** temporary allegiance. Someone present in a country has the benefit of its laws and \"must take the rough with the smooth\" (*Adams* at 519; C&Y 5.37)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-4.8"]},{"k":"step","t":"Physical presence only: websites and sales do not count"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Calling across a fence into a neighbour's garden is not the same as walking into it. A business that advertises into a country, sells into it or runs a website that people there can visit is calling across the fence. It is not standing in the garden."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Because the rationale is the allegiance of someone within the country, presence means **physical** presence (C&Y 5.35)."},{"p":"*Lucasfilm v Ainsworth* applied this to the internet. Mr Ainsworth, an English maker of film props, sold props to US customers through a website that quoted prices in US dollars first, and he emailed US customers. The English Court of Appeal held that he was not present in the US, so a US judgment against him could not rest on presence. A website is no different in principle from advertising in a country, and the \"omnipresence\" of the internet makes it a poor basis for saying its owner is present anywhere in particular ([191]–[194]). Arguing that the site targeted US customers did not help: targeting is still not presence ([190], [192])."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Presence must be **physical**. A website selling into a country, or advertising there, is not presence (*Lucasfilm* [192]–[194]; C&Y 5.35)."},{"t":"Selling goods into a country, or using travelling salesmen there, is not presence (*Littauer*; *Vogel v Kohnstamm*, both approved in *Adams*)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-4.4"]},{"k":"step","t":"Residence without presence: an open question"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Bob lives in Country X but is abroad on a long trip when Mei serves her claim. If a weekend guest is bound by the house rules, surely someone who lives in the house is too? That is the argument for residence. The difficulty is that the leading cases speak of presence, and the Court of Appeal's latest list leaves residence out."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Residence usually signals a deeper connection with a country than a visit does, so you might expect it to count. The authorities below point both ways. Singapore obiter dicta (remarks not needed for the decision, and so not binding) and Chong & Yip's *a fortiori* (all the more so) argument favour it. *Adams* left it open, English law now seems to say no, and *India v DT* [65] lists presence only."},{"p":"That is why the handout asks whether some Memoranda of Guidance (MoGs: non-binding documents stating a country's existing law on enforcing foreign judgments; Stage 1) are still right to list residence at the start of proceedings as a common law ground. The honest answer is that it is not settled. REFJA reverses the common law's emphasis: residence when the proceedings were instituted counts, and mere presence does not."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Residence without presence:**","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"*Adams* left the point open. English law now seems to say no, given the UK Supreme Court's emphasis on presence (*Rubin* [10]; C&Y 5.36).","b":["open"]},{"t":"Singapore cases have mentioned both residence and presence in obiter (*United Malayan Banking Corp v Khoo Boo Hor* [9]; C&Y 5.37).","b":["obiter"]},{"t":"C&Y argue that residence should qualify *a fortiori*, since it is usually a stronger connection than mere presence (C&Y 5.37).","b":["acad"]},{"t":"*India v DT* [65] lists **presence only** among its four grounds."},{"t":"This is the point behind the handout's question on whether the MoGs' reference to residence is still good law."}]}],"b":["open","miss"]},{"t":"**Contrast REFJA s 5(3)(a)(iv):** jurisdiction is based on **residence** when the proceedings were instituted, or for a company its **principal place of business**. Mere presence does **not** count under REFJA. Section 5(3)(a)(v) adds a ground unknown to the common law: an office or place of business in the foreign country, where the proceedings concerned a transaction effected through or at that office or place (C&Y 5.162–5.163)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-4.3"]},{"k":"step","t":"Companies: a fixed place of business"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"A company has no body, so it cannot step off a plane. For a company, \"being there\" means having a base in the country from which **its own** business is done: its own branch, or a representative working from a fixed place on the company's business. A trade-fair stand open for over a week can count. A salesman with an order book in a hotel room for a few days does not."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"The ideas of presence and residence translate \"somewhat uneasily\" to a company (C&Y 5.38). *Adams v Cape* looks for the corporate equivalent of standing in the country: a settled base from which the company's own business is carried on, whether a **branch office** of its own or a **representative's** fixed place of business."},{"p":"\"Fixed place\" means reasonably permanent, judged in context. \"More than a minimal period\" rules out the fleeting visit: unlike an individual, a company is not present just because it was briefly there. The test was built for trading companies, but a non-trading body can be present too, presumably on the same principles."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Companies: the *Adams v Cape* test** (at 530–531; C&Y 5.38–5.41; slide 8). A company is present only if one of two things is true:","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"**(i) \"Branch office\":** it has its **own fixed place of business**, maintained at its own expense (as owner or lessee), from which it has carried on **its own business** for more than a minimal period; or"},{"t":"**(ii) \"Representative\":** a representative has, for more than a minimal period, carried on **the company's** business (not the representative's own business) at or from a fixed place of business."}]}]},{"t":"**What counts as a \"fixed place of business\":** a reasonably permanent place (*Littauer*). A nine-day trade-fair stand has qualified (*Dunlop Pneumatic Tyre v Cudell*). A few days of taking orders in a hotel room has not (*Littauer*) (C&Y 5.39)."},{"t":"**Non-trading companies:** the same principles would \"presumably\" apply, adapted to their corporate activities (*Adams* at 524; C&Y 5.41)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-4.5","LAW302-Q12-4.11"]},{"k":"step","t":"Whose business? Representatives and corporate groups"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"A local shop that stocks Bob Pte Ltd's products is running its own shop, not Bob Pte Ltd's. A manager Bob Pte Ltd installs in an office abroad, who signs deals in its name, is running its business. The clearest sign of which you are looking at is whether the local person can commit the company to a contract without checking first."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"The representative case is the hard one, because the representative is often a business in its own right. Is it carrying on **its own** business or **the company's**? Of the *Adams* questions below, the court gave significant weight to authority to bind the company."},{"p":"*Adams* itself shows the test at work. Cape, an English company, faced a judgment of a US federal court in Tyler, Texas. The claimants said Cape was present through two Illinois companies: NAAC, its subsidiary, and CPC, an independently owned company. Each carried on its own business, and neither could bind Cape. Funding CPC's start-up costs did not make CPC's business Cape's (Scott J at 482), and never binding the parent is \"a powerful factor pointing against the presence of the overseas corporation\" (at 531)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Factors** for the representative case (*Adams* at 530–531; C&Y 5.40): whether the premises were acquired for the purpose of acting for the company; how the representative is paid; how much control the company has; and, given **significant weight**, whether the representative can **bind the company contractually** without needing prior approval. None is necessarily conclusive."},{"t":"A representative who merely acts as a go-between, without authority to bind the company, is not carrying on the company's business (*Vogel v Kohnstamm*; C&Y 5.40)."},{"t":"**Corporate groups:** there is no general principle that all companies in a group are treated as one. The \"single economic unit\" and veil-piercing arguments failed in *Adams*. NAAC (a Cape subsidiary) and CPC (an independently owned company) were each carrying on **their own** business, and neither had authority to bind Cape."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-4.6"]},{"k":"step","t":"Federal states: which \"country\"?"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Picture the United States as one building with a main hall and fifty rooms. Federal courts sit in the hall; each state's courts sit in their own room. On the tentative view in *Adams*, being anywhere in the building may be enough for the hall, but a room has authority only over people in it. The picture's weak spot: it assumes the hall really has authority over the whole building."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"In a federation, is \"the foreign country\" the whole federation, or the state where the court sits? In *Adams* the judgment came from a federal court in Texas, while Cape's alleged presence was in Illinois. Cape was not present anywhere in the US, so the point did not need deciding. The Court of Appeal's view, set out below, is therefore obiter, and was expressed with doubt."},{"p":"Chong & Yip doubt the federal half. It assumes that US federal courts have a national jurisdiction on federal matters, yet a judgment of a federal court in Texas is regarded as a \"foreign judgment\" in the other states."}]},{"k":"call","c":"tip","n":[{"p":"With a judgment from a federation, first identify the court. For a federal court, cite the *Adams* dicta, flag that they are obiter and doubted, and look for submission as a safer ground."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Federal states** (obiter in *Adams* at 557, \"with varying degrees of doubt\"; C&Y 5.42):","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"presence anywhere in the federation may support the jurisdiction of a **federal** court;"},{"t":"a **state** court needs presence in that state: Texas is no less a foreign court for a resident of Illinois \"than if Chicago were in France\" (*Adams* at 555)."}]}],"b":["obiter"]},{"t":"C&Y question the premise: under US law, a judgment of a federal court in Texas is regarded as a \"foreign judgment\" in the other states (C&Y 5.42).","b":["acad"]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-4.7"]},{"k":"take","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"International jurisdiction is judged by Singapore's rules, not the foreign court's."},{"t":"An individual is present if physically and voluntarily in the country when served, even briefly. Selling into the country, online or otherwise, does not count."},{"t":"Residence without presence is open at common law; REFJA uses residence, not presence."},{"t":"A company is present only through a fixed place of business where **its own** business is done for more than a minimal period, by itself or through a representative (authority to bind weighs heavily)."},{"t":"In a federation, *Adams* (obiter) says a state court needs presence in that state, and a federal court may rely on presence anywhere in the federation; the federal-court view is doubted."}]}]},{"k":"traps","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"A website or targeted sales into a country do not make a seller present there."},{"t":"Do not treat a parent company as present through its subsidiary simply because they form a group."},{"t":"Under REFJA, the test is residence or principal place of business, not presence."},{"t":"Presence is tested at the date of service, not the date of issue. \"More than a minimal period\" belongs to the company test, not to individuals."},{"t":"Presence obtained by fraud, compulsion or duress does not count."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":true,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-4.9","LAW302-Q12-4.10","LAW302-Q12-4.12","LAW302-Q12-4.13"]}],"ic":"map","nq":13},{"id":"w12.m3.5","code":"3.5","kind":"module","t":"International jurisdiction (2): submission","blocks":[{"k":"obj","t":"Decide whether a defendant submitted to the foreign court, by conduct, by agreement, or by \"inchoate\" submission."},{"k":"big","t":"Submission is about consent. A defendant who asks the foreign court to decide the dispute in its favour has accepted that court's authority. A defendant who only tells the court it has no authority has not."},{"k":"step","t":"What submission means"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Bob was never in Country X. Can X's court still bind him? Yes, if he **accepted** its authority. If you sit down at a chess match and ask the arbiter to rule on a disputed move, you cannot later say the arbiter had no say over your game. Walking up only to say \"I never agreed to play\" accepts nothing. Agreeing beforehand that this arbiter will settle any dispute also counts."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Submission is the second route to international jurisdiction. It takes two forms: the defendant's **conduct** in the foreign proceedings, and its **agreement**, given in advance, to the foreign court's jurisdiction (C&Y 5.43). It counts because it is fair to hold a party to a process it chose to join. A defendant cannot try for a win on the merits and then, having lost, deny that the court could judge him."},{"p":"For conduct, the *Nimbus* test below, and the *Rubin* formulation you will meet in the *IGCF* step, come to one question: would a defendant who still rejected the court have taken this step? If not, the step shows consent. As with presence, Singapore law decides."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**The test for submission by conduct** (*WSG Nimbus* [54]; *Giant Light* [24]): has the defendant \"taken a step in the proceedings which necessarily involved waiving their objection to the jurisdiction\"?"},{"t":"**Whose law decides?** **Singapore** law (C&Y 5.54)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-5.1"]},{"k":"step","t":"Protest is not submission: *Nimbus* and *Re Dulles*"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Telling a referee \"you have no right to referee this match\" is not agreeing to play. If staying at home and ignoring the match is not consent, turning up only to object cannot be consent either."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"In *WSG Nimbus*, the Board of Control for Cricket in Sri Lanka sued Nimbus in the Colombo High Court, although cl 19 of their broadcast-rights agreement included a reference to arbitration in Singapore. Nimbus objected under s 5 of the Sri Lankan Arbitration Act, which removed the court's jurisdiction over a matter covered by an arbitration agreement if the other party objected. The court ruled that cl 19, which allowed a choice between arbitration and litigation, was not an \"arbitration agreement\". Nimbus took no further part."},{"p":"In Singapore the Board pleaded an **issue estoppel** (a bar on re-arguing an issue already decided between the same parties) on cl 19, which needed a submission. Its theory that Nimbus had invoked the court's separate jurisdiction to decide its own jurisdiction was rejected as \"overly theoretical\". Objecting was the only way Nimbus could remove the court's jurisdiction, so it had \"obviously\" not submitted ([52], [54])."},{"p":"The court preferred Denning LJ's reasoning in *Re Dulles' Settlement (No 2)*. Letting judgment go by default is not submission, and appearing only to protest is no different in principle. Fighting the merits as well is submission: the defendant invites a decision in his favour, and cannot accept it if he wins and reject it if he loses."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"***WSG Nimbus* [31]–[54]:** Nimbus objected to the Colombo court's jurisdiction under s 5 of the Sri Lankan Arbitration Act, lost on that objection, and took no further part (it started arbitration and obtained injunctions in Singapore instead). It did **not** submit, so the Board could not rely on an issue estoppel on whether cl 19 was an arbitration agreement.","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"The \"dual jurisdiction\" argument based on *Williams & Glyn's* was rejected; the judge warned against an \"overly theoretical\" approach (C&Y 5.44–5.45)."}]}]},{"t":"***Re Dulles' Settlement (No 2)*** (Denning LJ), preferred in *Nimbus* [54]: appearing solely to protest against jurisdiction is not submission; fighting the case on the merits as well is (C&Y 5.48)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-5.2","LAW302-Q12-5.9"]},{"k":"step","t":"The clear cases, and a merits defence filed alongside a challenge"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"If Bob starts the case in X, hits back with a counterclaim, or argues that he is not liable, he has asked X's court to judge. He has taken a seat at the table. The awkward case is the defendant who says \"you have no power to hear this\" and, in the same breath, \"and anyway I am not liable\"."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Suing as claimant is submission, and it extends to related claims brought back against the claimant, such as a counterclaim, cross-action or set-off (a cross-claim that reduces what is owed). A defendant who counterclaims, cross-claims or claims a set-off, or who defends on the merits, submits too."},{"p":"The hard case is a merits defence filed together with a jurisdiction challenge. Chong & Yip's answer (below) turns on the foreign procedure, and on one idea: was pleading the merits the defendant's free choice? A defendant cannot \"have its cake and eat it\", keeping a chance of winning on the merits while reserving the right to say it never submitted."},{"p":"Submission also needs the defendant's **own** step. Being unilaterally included as a creditor in a foreign composition plan (a debt arrangement in foreign insolvency proceedings) is not, in itself, submission."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Clear examples of submission** (C&Y 5.46): suing as claimant, which extends to related claims brought against the claimant, such as a counterclaim, cross-action or set-off (*Schibsby*; *Malaysia Marine v VLK* [23]); as defendant, counterclaiming, cross-claiming or claiming a set-off; defending on the merits (*Malaysia Marine v VLK* [23])."},{"t":"**Defending on the merits alongside a jurisdiction challenge** (C&Y 5.46):","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"If the foreign rules **allowed** but did not require a merits defence at that stage, it is likely to count as submission."},{"t":"If the foreign rules **required** both together and the objection was maintained throughout, it is likely not submission.","b":["acad"]},{"t":"If the defendant could have withdrawn its merits defence after losing on jurisdiction but did not, it should not be allowed to \"have its cake and eat it\".","b":["acad"]}]}]},{"t":"**Insolvency:** the unilateral inclusion of the judgment debtor as a creditor in a foreign composition plan could not in itself be submission (*Humpuss*; C&Y 5.53)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-5.10","LAW302-Q12-5.11"]},{"k":"step","t":"Asking the court not to use its jurisdiction: *Henry v Geoprosco*"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"There is a difference, at least on paper, between telling a court \"you have no power to hear this\" and \"you have the power, but please do not use it\". *Henry v Geoprosco* treated the second as accepting the court's power. Critics reply that both requests aim at the same thing: getting out of the court, not into it."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Three terms first. **Service out** is serving a defendant outside the court's territory, which the defendant can apply to **set aside** (cancel). **Forum non conveniens** (FNC) is the argument that another country's courts are the more appropriate forum. A **stay** pauses the proceedings, for example in favour of arbitration."},{"p":"In *Henry v Geoprosco*, the defendant applied to set aside service out, arguing among other things that Canada was not the forum conveniens, and alternatively sought a stay because of an arbitration clause. The English Court of Appeal held that it had asked the court, in its discretion, not to exercise a jurisdiction it had. That was submission, even though success would have deprived the court of jurisdiction."},{"p":"*Nimbus* proceeded \"on the basis that it is the law\" without deciding the point. On that logic an FNC argument may be submission, but a \"no nexus\" argument (the claim does not fit the ground of service relied on) is not: there is no discretion, as the claim fits or it does not."},{"p":"Chong & Yip call this line unsatisfactory: it is the kind of \"overly theoretical\" approach *Nimbus* warned against, and it sits badly with *Re Dulles*. They prefer a simple rule, which would match REFJA: an unsuccessful set-aside application is never submission, whatever the argument."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"***Henry v Geoprosco*** (English Court of Appeal): applying to set aside service out on FNC grounds, or for a stay in favour of arbitration, **invited the court to exercise its discretion** and so counted as submission. Much criticised, and reversed in England by CJJA s 33 (C&Y 5.47)."},{"t":"**In Singapore:** *Nimbus* proceeded on the basis that it is the law, **without deciding** the point (*Nimbus* [50]; C&Y 5.47–5.48).","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"On that logic, an FNC argument risks being submission, but a \"no nexus\" argument does not, as no discretion is involved (C&Y 5.48)."}]}],"b":["open"]},{"t":"**Better view** (C&Y 5.49): an unsuccessful application to set aside service should never amount to submission, whatever argument was run.","b":["acad","miss"]},{"t":"**Contrast REFJA s 5(3)(a)(i)(C):** appearing to invite the foreign court, in its discretion, not to exercise its jurisdiction is **not** submission, so *Geoprosco* does not apply under REFJA (C&Y 5.49, 5.163)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-5.3"]},{"k":"step","t":"*IGCF v Al Jomaih*: the Privy Council's protest view"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"The Privy Council asked a practical question about the \"please do not use your power\" request: would a defendant who still rejected the court have any reason to make it? Yes. A defendant who asks for a stay because of an arbitration clause is saying \"this dispute belongs somewhere else\". That is a protest, however the application is labelled."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"In 2025 the Privy Council, on appeal from the Cayman Islands, refused to follow *Geoprosco*: the respondent's stay application and its Order 39 application in Pakistan (the latter in substance a challenge to jurisdiction) were not submission, so the anti-suit injunction (an order restraining a party from pursuing proceedings abroad) stood. Cayman law, as the law of the forum, decided the question; Pakistan law only explained what the steps meant."}]},{"k":"call","c":"tip","n":[{"p":"If Bob argued FNC or sought a stay for arbitration, run both views: possible submission under *Geoprosco* (assumed in *Nimbus*), a protest under *IGCF*, C&Y's better view and the REFJA analogy. Then say which you prefer and why."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"***IGCF v Al Jomaih* [2025] UKPC 54 (Lord Hamblen; Privy Council on appeal from the Cayman Islands):**","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"*Geoprosco* does not represent Cayman law."},{"t":"Distinguishing between challenging the **existence** of jurisdiction and challenging its **exercise** is \"unrealistic and irrational\". A stay sought because of an arbitration or jurisdiction clause is a **protest against** jurisdiction, not an acceptance of it."},{"t":"The *Rubin* test applies: a step counts as submission only if it is \"only necessary or only useful if\" the objection has been waived."},{"t":"On the facts, the respondent's applications in Pakistan (a stay in favour of arbitration, and an application under Order 39 to recall an injunction, relying on the shareholders' agreement) were **not** submission. The appeal was dismissed."}]}]},{"t":"**Status in Singapore:** persuasive, not binding.","b":["open"]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-5.4"]},{"k":"step","t":"Singapore's standards: foreign rules as context, no mirror image"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"A raised hand means \"I have a question\" in a classroom and \"I bid\" at an auction. To read Bob's step, Singapore looks at how X's procedure treats it, but makes the final call itself. And it judges X's court by a stricter list than it uses for its own courts."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"A procedural step only makes sense against the rules of the court where it was taken. So, although Singapore law decides, the foreign rules supply context. *Giant Light* approved Thomas J in *Akai*: the significance of a step \"can only be understood by reference to\" the foreign law, so if a counterclaim would not count as submission in the foreign court, the forum takes that into account. The context cuts both ways: a foreign rule deeming a step to be submission does not bind Singapore either."},{"p":"Nor does Singapore use a **mirror image** approach, accepting any basis of jurisdiction it would use itself. A Singapore court can take jurisdiction over a defendant served abroad, but a foreign court asserting jurisdiction over someone outside its territory does not thereby gain international jurisdiction (C&Y 5.50). The list of grounds is narrower, and closed."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Singapore** law decides. Foreign procedural rules are relevant context, showing what a step meant in that system (*Akai*, approved in *Giant Light* [26]–[27]; C&Y 5.54).","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"It would be strange to find submission where the foreign rules would not, but *Rubin* [161] leaves the possibility open: submission \"is to be inferred from all the facts\" (C&Y 5.55). *Rubin* calls for a \"broader approach\" that takes account of the international context (quoted in *IGCF*)."}]}]},{"t":"**Is this the same as submission to the Singapore court's own jurisdiction?** **Not as a mirror image**, and the grounds of international jurisdiction are narrower than Singapore's own (*Schibsby*; C&Y 5.50; slide 13). The general test is similar (*Williams & Glyn's*, adopted in *Rubin* [160]). But the ROC 2021 rule that challenging the *exercise* of Singapore's jurisdiction is not submission (O 6 r 7(6)) does not automatically carry over (C&Y 5.50).","b":["open"]},{"t":"**Are the categories closed?** *Schibsby v Westenholz* says yes. Canada's *Beals v Saldanha* adds a \"real and substantial connection\" ground. In *Giant Light*, the claimant raised *Beals* but the court did not need to decide it [21], [53]. Singapore lists the grounds as closed (*India v DT* [65])."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-5.14","LAW302-Q12-5.8"]},{"k":"step","t":"Inchoate submission: consent that carries over"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Bob defends Mei's claim in Country X. Mei ends that case to try to settle, then files the identical claim again when talks fail. Bob ignores the new case. It would be odd if a new case number wiped out Bob's acceptance of the court. The law can treat his earlier submission as carrying over, if that is fair to both sides."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"\"Inchoate\" means begun but not complete. In *Giant Light*, a Chinese company sued Aksa, a Singapore company, in China over two unsatisfactory generator sets. Aksa defended the 2005 proceedings, which the claimant discontinued while the parties tried to settle. Aksa ignored the identical 2008 proceedings, and judgment went against it in default (C&Y 5.32, 5.51). The High Court held that Aksa had submitted: had the 2005 case been stayed rather than discontinued, no one would have doubted it ([50])."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Inchoate submission** (*Giant Light* [37]–[52]; *Murthy v Sivajothi*; C&Y 5.51–5.52):","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"A submission to one claim can extend to claims concerning the **same subject matter** or **related claims**, even in later, separate proceedings, if that is **fair** to both sides. \"Technical impediments created by procedural rules under both foreign and forum law\" are disregarded ([49])."},{"t":"Two inter-related issues: the connection between the later and the original claim (\"a matter of degree\"), and fairness to both parties (C&Y 5.52)."},{"t":"***Giant Light*:** consent was imputed because \"the later proceedings were separate … only because of a technicality.\""},{"t":"**Contrast *Adams v Cape*:** Cape's limited appearance in Tyler 1 (to record a consent order, after protesting jurisdiction throughout) did **not** extend to Tyler 2, which involved different plaintiffs. Imputing consent there would have been \"grossly unfair\" (*Giant Light* [40]–[42])."}]}]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-5.5","LAW302-Q12-5.16"]},{"k":"step","t":"Submission by agreement (*Vizcaya*)"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Bob may have agreed to X's courts before any dispute arose, in a jurisdiction clause. That is the easy case. The harder question is whether agreement can be read between the lines. Agreeing that a contract follows X's rulebook (X law) is not agreeing that X's referees (X's courts) will decide disputes about it."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"A jurisdiction clause in favour of the foreign court is submission by prior agreement. The debate is about **implied** agreement. Singapore High Court decisions on the (now repealed) RECJA required an express agreement (C&Y 5.56)."},{"p":"In *Vizcaya v Picard*, the Privy Council, on appeal from Gibraltar, asked whether Vizcaya had consented in advance to the New York court that gave judgment against it. It held that consent may be implied, and that whether a contract contains such a term is for the **proper law of the contract** (the law that governs it). But choosing New York law and doing business there was not consent, even though it gave the New York court \"long arm\" jurisdiction (power over defendants outside its territory) under its own rules. As in Module 3.4, what the foreign court's own rules allow is not the test."},{"p":"*Vizcaya* is persuasive here, not binding, and the Singapore cases pre-date it. Nor does REFJA's wording (\"agreed … to submit\", s 5(3)(a)(iii)) settle the common law question."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Submission by agreement** (*Vizcaya* [56]–[72]; C&Y 5.56–5.58):","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"The real question is whether the debtor **consented in advance** to the foreign court's jurisdiction. The consent need not be contractual, and it **may be implied** [56]."},{"t":"Whether a term is implied is a question for the **proper law of the contract**, which must be proved by expert evidence on its rules of construction or its implied terms [60]–[61]."},{"t":"Consent **cannot** be inferred merely from: being a shareholder or partner; the contract being made, or deemed made, in that country; the contract being performed there; or the contract choosing that country's law [58], [71]; C&Y 5.57."},{"t":"On the facts, choosing New York law and transacting business in New York gave the New York court \"long arm\" jurisdiction, but was **not** a submission [69]–[72]."}]}]},{"t":"**Singapore:** earlier High Court decisions under RECJA required an **express** agreement. Whether *Vizcaya*'s implied consent will be adopted is open (C&Y 5.58; slide 12).","b":["open"]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-5.6","LAW302-Q12-5.17","LAW302-Q12-5.7"]},{"k":"take","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Submission by conduct is a step that necessarily waives the objection to jurisdiction (*Nimbus* [54]). Suing, counterclaiming and defending on the merits count; a protest alone does not (*Re Dulles*)."},{"t":"Asking the court not to exercise its jurisdiction is contested: *Geoprosco* says submission and *Nimbus* assumed it without deciding; *IGCF* (persuasive) treats a stay for arbitration as a protest; C&Y's better view is that an unsuccessful set-aside application is never submission, whatever the argument."},{"t":"Singapore law decides, with foreign procedural rules as context. There is no mirror image, and the grounds are closed."},{"t":"Submission can carry over to the same or related claims where fair (*Giant Light*), but not where unfair (*Adams*)."},{"t":"Prior agreement counts. *Vizcaya* accepts implied consent, though not from a choice of law alone; whether Singapore follows it is open."}]}]},{"k":"traps","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Merely contesting jurisdiction and losing is not, by itself, submission (*Nimbus*). The *Geoprosco* line on discretionary challenges (e.g. forum non conveniens) is the contested exception."},{"t":"Seeking a stay in favour of arbitration was treated as submission under *Geoprosco* and as a protest under *IGCF*. Know both views and why they differ."},{"t":"A choice of a country's law does not by itself mean consent to that country's courts (*Vizcaya*)."},{"t":"A merits defence filed voluntarily alongside a jurisdiction challenge is likely to be submission, even though the challenge is kept up."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":true,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-5.12","LAW302-Q12-5.13","LAW302-Q12-5.15","LAW302-Q12-5.18"]}],"ic":"users","nq":18},{"id":"w12.m3.6","code":"3.6","kind":"module","t":"Final and conclusive","blocks":[{"k":"obj","t":"Apply both aspects of finality to a foreign judgment, work out which law decides, and explain what finality means for limitation and for issue estoppel."},{"k":"big","t":"Singapore treats a foreign judgment as final only if it is final at home: the court that gave it cannot change it, and its own legal system treats it as settled."},{"k":"step","t":"Two aspects of finality"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Think of a referee's decision. Before Singapore treats Country X's decision as the end of the match, two things must be true. The referee can no longer change the call, and Country X's rulebook treats the call as settled. If that rulebook says a call becomes settled only after a video review, Singapore waits for the review too."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Mei has won a judgment against Bob in Country X and wants Singapore to give it effect. Singapore will do that only for a decision that is truly finished. The reason goes back to Stage 2: Singapore gives effect to foreign judgments out of comity (respect between the courts of different countries) and to bring disputes to an end. Neither aim is served by a decision the foreign court might still change, and it would be unfair to make Bob pay on a judgment that could later vanish at home."},{"p":"In legal terms, the judgment must be **final and conclusive**, and for a foreign judgment that has two aspects. First, the court that gave the judgment cannot vary, reopen or set it aside. Second, the foreign court must itself regard the judgment as final and conclusive: as res judicata, meaning a matter decided once and for all between the parties, which they cannot reopen. Lord Reid explained why the second aspect matters in *Carl Zeiss*: it would \"verge on absurdity\" to treat as conclusive something the foreign courts themselves would not."},{"p":"That tells you which law decides. Res judicata status is something the foreign legal system gives or withholds, so Singapore asks what Country X's law says, proved by expert evidence."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**The two aspects** (*Humpuss* [69]–[70], applied in *Sang Cheol Woo* [31]–[42]):","c":[{"ol":[{"t":"The judgment cannot be varied, reopened or set aside by the court that gave it (*The Bunga Melati 5* [81])."},{"t":"**For foreign judgments:** the foreign court must itself regard the judgment as final and conclusive, i.e. as res judicata between the parties. Lord Reid in *Carl Zeiss*: it would \"verge on absurdity\" to treat as conclusive something the foreign courts themselves would not."}]}]},{"t":"**Which law decides?** The **law of the foreign court**, proved by expert evidence (*Sang Cheol Woo* [42]; *Giant Light* [66]; C&Y 5.59)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-6.5","LAW302-Q12-6.1"]},{"k":"step","t":"Appeals, default judgments and interlocutory rulings"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"An exam result that has been officially released is your result, even though you could still ask for a re-mark. It stays your result unless the re-mark changes it. In the same way, a judgment can be final even while a challenge to it is possible or under way."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Three situations often cause confusion."},{"p":"**An appeal.** A judgment that is subject to appeal, or actually under appeal, can still be final and conclusive: until a higher court reverses it, it stands in its own system. For convenience, the Singapore court may stay (pause) its enforcement proceedings until the foreign appeal is heard. But if the foreign law itself withholds res judicata status until appeals are exhausted, Singapore follows the foreign law (next step)."},{"p":"**A default judgment** is one given because the defendant did not take part. Suppose Bob ignored the Country X proceedings and judgment was entered against him, which he could still apply to set aside. If it has the effect of finality unless and until it is set aside, it is final, even though the X court is unlikely to have considered Bob's arguments. Chong & Yip suggest that what counts is finality as to the merits, not whether the merits were examined."},{"p":"**An interlocutory decision**, made along the way before the final judgment, can qualify if it finally determines the parties' rights on a specific issue."},{"p":"REFJA, the registration statute for gazetted countries (Stage 1), takes the same approach to appeals, with a safety valve built in."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"A judgment that is **subject to appeal** can still be final and conclusive. The Singapore court may stay enforcement pending the appeal (*Nouvion v Freeman*; C&Y 5.59)."},{"t":"**Default judgments** can be final even though they may be set aside, if they have the effect of finality until the defendant succeeds in setting them aside. That the foreign court is unlikely to have considered the defendant's arguments is irrelevant (C&Y 5.59).","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"C&Y suggest the important criterion is that the judgment is final **as to the merits**, not that the merits were examined (5.59).","b":["acad"]}]}]},{"t":"An **interlocutory** decision that finally determines the parties' rights on a specific issue can qualify (C&Y 5.59; *Lakshmi* [101])."},{"t":"**REFJA:** unless it is interlocutory, the judgment must be final and conclusive (s 3(2)). It is final even if an appeal is pending or the time for appealing has not expired (s 3(5)). The court then has a discretion to set aside the registration, or adjourn an application to set it aside, until the appeal is disposed of or the time for appealing has expired (C&Y 5.157)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-6.3","LAW302-Q12-6.6"]},{"k":"step","t":"*Sang Cheol Woo*: when the foreign law says \"not yet\""},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"In some races the result is announced at the finish line but becomes official only after the stewards rule on any protest. You might even collect a provisional prize straight away. But the record books count the result only once it is official. Singapore asks when the result became official under the foreign country's own rules."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"*Sang Cheol Woo* shows the second aspect at work. Mr Woo obtained a judgment against Mr Spackman in the Seoul High Court in September 2011. It could be provisionally executed, that is, enforced at once. The Korean Supreme Court dismissed Mr Spackman's appeal on 31 October 2013, and Mr Woo sued on the judgment in Singapore in February 2019."},{"p":"Mr Spackman said the action was time-barred, because more than six years had passed since 2011. His argument rested on the first aspect of finality alone: after 2011 the Seoul High Court could no longer vary its judgment. The High Court rejected it as \"very strained\". Under Korean law the judgment became res judicata only when appeals were exhausted, in October 2013, and provisional execution did not change that. Until then the judgment was not final, so it could not have been enforced in Singapore at all. Time ran from 2013, and the action was in time."},{"p":"One textbook point to watch. C&Y 5.97 says time runs from the date of the foreign judgment. For this course, follow *Sang Cheol Woo*. The two dates coincide whenever the foreign law treats a judgment as res judicata as soon as it is given."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"***Sang Cheol Woo* [43]–[48]:**","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"Under Korean law, a judgment becomes res judicata only when appeals are exhausted. So the Seoul High Court judgment of 2011 became final and conclusive only when the Korean Supreme Court ruled on **31 October 2013**."},{"t":"The fact that the judgment could be **provisionally executed** did not make it final, since provisional execution can be reversed if an appeal succeeds [44]."},{"t":"Spackman's argument, based on the first aspect of finality alone, was \"very strained\" [31]."},{"t":"Consequence: the six-year limitation period under LA s 6(1)(a) ran from 2013, so the 2019 action was **not** time-barred."},{"t":"This matches the treatment of appellate judgments under **REFJA s 4(1)(b)**, and the *Merck* [37] call for convergence [46]–[47]."}]}]},{"t":"C&Y 5.97 says time runs from the date of the foreign judgment. Follow *Sang Cheol Woo*: the six years run from when the judgment became final and conclusive under the foreign law."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-6.2"]},{"k":"step","t":"Finality of a single issue"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"In some legal systems a judgment is like a sealed verdict: only the final answer binds, not the judge's reasons on the way there. Before you rely on one of those reasons later, check that the foreign law treats it as binding at all."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Finality comes back in Module 4.2 in a sharper form. Issue estoppel lets a party rely on a specific point the foreign court decided, rather than on the judgment as a whole. So it is not enough that the judgment is final: the decision on that specific issue must itself be final and conclusive under the foreign law. This matters most for judgments from civil law countries, where the operative result binds the parties but the reasoning may not. Lord Reid made the same point in *Carl Zeiss*: for issue estoppel, the court must be satisfied that the issue cannot be relitigated in the foreign country (C&Y 5.114)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**For issue estoppel**, the decision on the **specific issue**, not just the judgment as a whole, must be final and conclusive under the law of the court of origin (*Merck* [41]–[43], following *Carl Zeiss*; C&Y 5.109).","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"In some systems (e.g. civil law systems), only the result binds, not the reasoning. In *Mad Atelier*, French law had no issue estoppel, so the French court's findings were not final (*Merck* [43])."}]}]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-6.4"]},{"k":"take","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"\"Final and conclusive\" has two aspects: the court that gave the judgment cannot vary it, and the foreign law treats it as res judicata."},{"t":"The law of the foreign court decides, proved by expert evidence."},{"t":"A possible or pending appeal does not by itself prevent finality, and default judgments and some interlocutory decisions can be final. But if the foreign law withholds res judicata status until appeals end, Singapore follows that (*Sang Cheol Woo*)."},{"t":"At common law, the six years run from when the judgment became final and conclusive under the foreign law; for issue estoppel, the specific issue must be final under that law."}]}]},{"k":"traps","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Do not test finality by Singapore law, or by the first aspect alone (Spackman's \"very strained\" argument)."},{"t":"Provisional enforceability is not finality."},{"t":"Do not assume \"under appeal\" means \"not final\", or the reverse, without checking the foreign law."},{"t":"A default judgment is not unfinished just because the defendant could still apply to set it aside, as long as the foreign law gives it effect until it is set aside."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":true,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-6.7"]}],"ic":"lock","nq":7}]},{"id":"w12.s4","n":"4","title":"The effect of a recognised foreign judgment","sub":"4.1 Cause of action estoppel · 4.2 Transnational issue estoppel (Merck) · 4.3 Henderson v Henderson (the extended doctrine of res judicata)","lessons":[{"id":"w12.m4.1","code":"4.1","kind":"module","t":"Cause of action estoppel","blocks":[{"k":"obj","t":"Explain the three ways a recognised foreign judgment can stop relitigation in Singapore, and use cause of action estoppel both as a shield for a winning defendant and as a route for a winning claimant whose judgment cannot be enforced."},{"k":"big","t":"Once Singapore recognises a foreign judgment, the same claim between the same parties cannot be fought again. And because a foreign judgment does not swallow the claim, the winner can also sue again and use the judgment to avoid re-arguing the merits."},{"k":"step","t":"Three ways a judgment closes the door"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"A finished court case is like a closed file. Singapore can keep it closed in three ways. It can stop the loser bringing the **same claim** again. It can stop anyone reopening a **specific point** the file already settled, even inside a different claim. And it can stop parties raising points they **should have put in the file** the first time."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Stage 3 asked whether a foreign judgment qualifies for recognition. Stage 4 asks what recognition does: the judgment becomes res judicata in Singapore, a matter already decided which the parties cannot reopen. The law gets there through estoppel, a rule that stops a party asserting something inconsistent with what has been settled. The three routes match the ELI5: cause of action estoppel (this module), issue estoppel (Module 4.2) and the rule in *Henderson v Henderson* (Module 4.3). Cause of action estoppel and issue estoppel are uses of recognition, not enforcement, so the Stage 3 requirements apply but the fresh money judgment requirement does not (C&Y 5.09, 5.20). *Henderson* works differently, through abuse of process (Module 4.3)."},{"p":"Cause of action estoppel is the bluntest. A cause of action is the set of facts that gives someone a right to sue, such as a particular breach of a particular contract. If the same cause of action comes back between the same parties (or their privies: people whose legal position is tied to a party's, such as a successor), about the same subject matter, the judgment is an absolute bar on every point it decided, unless fraud or collusion is alleged (fraud is covered in Week 13). The policy is finality: litigation must end, and a party who has won once should not have to win again."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"A foreign judgment can be res judicata in Singapore through **cause of action estoppel**, **issue estoppel** or the rule in ***Henderson v Henderson*** (C&Y 5.103).","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"Cause of action and issue estoppel are forms of estoppel per rem judicatam, the traditional doctrine of res judicata. *Henderson* is the \"extended doctrine\" and is primarily concerned with abuse of process."}]}]},{"t":"**Cause of action estoppel** arises where the cause of action in the later proceedings is identical to that in the earlier proceedings, the earlier proceedings were between the same parties or their privies, and they involved the same subject matter. The bar is then **absolute** as to every point decided, unless fraud or collusion is alleged such as to justify setting aside the earlier judgment (C&Y 5.104, quoting *Arnold v National Westminster Bank*).","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"It can arise even if one action was brought in rem (against a thing) and the other in personam (against a person) (C&Y 5.104)."}]}]},{"t":"Estoppel is a use of **recognition**. The fresh money judgment requirement does not apply (C&Y 5.09, 5.20). Declaratory judgments cannot be enforced, and their value lies precisely in creating res judicata (C&Y 5.11)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-7.10"]},{"k":"step","t":"Shield and second bite"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"If Mei sued Bob abroad and lost, Bob can hold up the foreign judgment as a **shield** when she tries again in Singapore. If Mei won abroad but cannot cash the judgment in here, for example because it orders Bob to hand over shares rather than pay money, she can **start again** in Singapore on her original claim and hold up the judgment to show that the question has already been answered."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"**The shield.** Mei sues Bob in Country X and loses, then sues him in Singapore on the same cause of action. Bob pleads the X judgment in his defence. He is asking the court to recognise it, not enforce it, and the ordinary pleading rules are enough. Why could Mei even try again? A Singapore judgment swallows the cause of action it decides (lawyers call this merger), but on the Court of Appeal's preliminary view in *Ocean Tankers*, merger does not extend to foreign judgments. So only the estoppel stops her."},{"p":"**The second bite.** The same gap helps a winning claimant. Suppose Mei's X judgment orders Bob to transfer shares rather than pay money. It cannot be enforced at common law (Module 3.3), and if Country X is outside REFJA and the CCAA, no statute helps her. But Mei can sue Bob again in Singapore on her original cause of action and plead the X judgment as res judicata. If the judgment meets the requirements for recognition, Bob is bound and the merits are not argued afresh. It is a longer route than enforcement, but it gets her to a similar place."}]},{"k":"call","c":"example","n":[{"p":"Mei's Country X judgment orders Bob to transfer shares to her under their contract. Bob ignores it. Mei sues Bob in Singapore for breach of the same contract and pleads the X judgment. If the X court had international jurisdiction over Bob, its judgment is final and on the merits, and no defence applies, Bob cannot re-argue whether he breached the contract."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**How cause of action estoppel is used** (C&Y 5.105):","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"(i) the defendant who won abroad pleads the judgment as a defence (or counterclaim) when the losing claimant sues again in Singapore on the same cause of action;"},{"t":"(ii) the claimant who won abroad sues again in Singapore on the original cause of action and pleads the judgment as res judicata, for example where it is a non-money judgment outside REFJA and the CCAA, which cannot be enforced at common law. If the judgment meets the recognition requirements, it binds the parties and the merits need not be engaged afresh."}]}]},{"t":"Route (ii) is possible, and route (i) is needed, because there is **no merger** with foreign judgments (*Ocean Tankers* [95]–[96], a preliminary view approving *Malaysia Credit Finance*; C&Y 5.10). Prof Tham's suggestion that merger might occur once a judgment is registered under statute was **not decided** ([95]).","b":["open"]},{"t":"No special procedure is needed: the normal pleading rules apply (C&Y 5.10; *Merck* [35])."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-7.11"]},{"k":"step","t":"REFJA judgments as a shield"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"REFJA is the fast lane for enforcing judgments from listed countries: you register the judgment instead of suing on it. But you do not need a fast-lane ticket just to hold the judgment up as a shield. Pleading it in your defence is enough."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Stage 1 showed that REFJA is exclusive for collecting money: if a judgment falls within the Act, s 7(1) bars any proceedings to recover the sum other than registration. That rule is about collecting. It says nothing against using a REFJA judgment defensively."},{"p":"REFJA in fact provides for recognition directly. A judgment within its scope is recognised as conclusive between the parties in all proceedings founded on the same cause of action, and may be relied on by way of defence or counterclaim. So if Bob wins against Mei in a gazetted country and Mei sues again in Singapore, Bob can plead the judgment without registering it. The defences that would bar enforcement also bar recognition, so Mei can still raise, for example, fraud or public policy (Week 13)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"A judgment within REFJA is recognised as **conclusive** between the parties in all proceedings founded on the same cause of action, and may be relied on by way of **defence or counterclaim**. It need not be registered to have this effect, and the same defences that bar enforcement bar recognition (C&Y 5.172)."},{"t":"REFJA s 7(1) bars proceedings to **recover a sum** under such a judgment other than by registration (C&Y 5.173). It does not stop a party relying on the judgment as res judicata."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-7.12"]},{"k":"step","t":"A promise not to rely: cross-estoppel"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"If Bob tells Mei, \"I won't use that foreign judgment against you\", he may be held to what he said. The judgment gives Bob one estoppel; his own statement can give Mei another, which blocks the first."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Estoppel can cut both ways. A party who has represented that it would not rely on a foreign judgment may be prevented from raising the judgment as res judicata. Chong & Yip call this cross-estoppel: an estoppel per rem judicatam (from the judgment) met by an estoppel by representation (from what the party represented). The Court of Appeal noted the same possibility in *Merck* [36], when discussing conflicting judgments (Week 13). The policy is fairness: having represented that he will not rely on the judgment, Bob should not be able to ambush Mei with it later. This course does not go into the detailed requirements of estoppel by representation, so know the idea and its effect."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Cross-estoppel:** a party may be prevented from raising res judicata if it has represented that it would not rely on the foreign judgment. An estoppel per rem judicatam is met by an estoppel by representation (C&Y 5.103, citing *Showlag v Mansour* and *Republic of India v India Steamship*; *Merck* [36])."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-7.13"]},{"k":"take","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"A recognised foreign judgment can be res judicata through cause of action estoppel, issue estoppel or *Henderson v Henderson* (C&Y 5.103)."},{"t":"Cause of action estoppel needs the same cause of action, the same parties (or privies) and the same subject matter. It is then an absolute bar on the points decided, unless fraud or collusion is alleged."},{"t":"It works as a shield for the winning defendant and as a second route for the winning claimant, because there is no merger with foreign judgments (*Ocean Tankers*)."},{"t":"Estoppel is recognition: no money judgment, no registration and no special procedure is needed, even for a REFJA judgment. A party who represented that it would not rely on the judgment may be met by a cross-estoppel."}]}]},{"k":"traps","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Do not say a foreign judgment \"merges\" the cause of action. *Ocean Tankers* says it does not."},{"t":"Do not apply the fresh money judgment requirement to an estoppel."},{"t":"Do not read REFJA s 7(1) as barring a defensive use of a REFJA judgment. It bars only recovery proceedings other than registration."},{"t":"Where the claims differ but share a point, the tool is issue estoppel (Module 4.2), not cause of action estoppel."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":true,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-7.14"]}],"ic":"shield","nq":5},{"id":"w12.m4.2","code":"4.2","kind":"module","t":"Transnational issue estoppel (Merck)","blocks":[{"k":"obj","t":"Apply the elements, the extra cautions and the \"outer limits\" of transnational issue estoppel, and decide which kinds of foreign ruling can bind the parties in Singapore."},{"k":"big","t":"A specific point finally decided abroad between the same parties binds them in Singapore, but the court applies the doctrine with special care and keeps some questions for itself."},{"k":"step","t":"The elements, and why Singapore takes care"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Even in a case about a different claim, if a foreign court has already decided a specific point between the same parties (for example, what a contract clause means), you usually cannot re-argue that point in Singapore. Singapore is careful, though, because it is trusting a court whose procedure it may not know well."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Issue estoppel is narrower and subtler than cause of action estoppel (Module 4.1). The claims in the two cases differ, but one point overlaps: an issue that was a necessary ingredient of the first claim, litigated and decided there, comes up again in a different claim between the same parties (or their privies: people whose legal position is tied to a party's)."},{"p":"*Merck*, the Court of Appeal's leading decision, sets out three elements. They are the same as for a domestic judgment, but must be applied with special care. Why? Comity (respect between the courts of different countries) supports transnational issue estoppel, but it is balanced against the Singapore court's constitutional role in safeguarding the rule of law (Stage 2). There are practical worries too, voiced by Lord Reid in *Carl Zeiss*. A Singapore judge may not know the foreign procedure well enough to be sure what was decided, and a defendant may sensibly not fight a trivial foreign case with full force."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Issue estoppel** arises where a particular issue forming a necessary ingredient in a cause of action has been litigated and decided, and in later proceedings between the same parties involving a different cause of action, to which the same issue is relevant, one party seeks to reopen it (C&Y 5.106, quoting *Arnold v National Westminster Bank*)."},{"t":"**The elements** (*Merck* [35], [40]; C&Y 5.107):","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"(1) a foreign judgment **capable of recognition**;"},{"t":"(2) **identity of issues**;"},{"t":"(3) **identity of parties**: only common parties (or their privies) can be bound (C&Y 5.108)."},{"t":"These are the same elements as for a domestic judgment, but they must be applied with **special care** [34]. They match the requirements the House of Lords set out in *The Sennar (No 2)* (C&Y 5.107)."}]}]},{"t":"**Why the care:** comity and reciprocal respect among courts, balanced against the recognising court's constitutional role in safeguarding the rule of law (*Merck* [33]). Lord Reid's cautions in *Carl Zeiss* clearly influenced *Merck* (C&Y 5.114)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-7.1"]},{"k":"step","t":"Is it really the same issue?"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Before treating a foreign court's answer as settled, Singapore reads the judgment closely. Did the court really need to decide this point, or did it only mention it in passing? And does that country's law treat the reasons, not just the result, as binding?"}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"The heart of the doctrine is identity of issues. A remark the result did not depend on is merely collateral and binds no one. A finding that the foreign law does not treat as binding cannot bind here either (Module 3.6). And it matters whether the party had a real chance to argue the point abroad."},{"p":"There is also a limit on the kind of issue. Issue estoppel stops the parties relitigating what was decided about **their** dispute. A pure question of law, a statement of the law that does not affect the parties' rights, cannot found an estoppel. Applying the law to the parties' facts, as the English court did in *Merck*, can."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Cautions on identity of issues** (*Merck* [40]–[43]):","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"Work out exactly what the foreign court decided, and whether the issue was **necessary** to its decision, not merely collateral (C&Y 5.108)."},{"t":"The decision on the **specific issue** must be **final and conclusive under the foreign law**. In some systems (e.g. civil law systems), only the result binds, not the reasoning. In *Mad Atelier*, French law had no issue estoppel, so the French court's findings were not final [43]."},{"t":"Ask whether the party had the **occasion or opportunity** to raise the issue."}]}]},{"t":"**A pure question of law** that does not affect the parties' rights cannot found an issue estoppel. Applying law to facts, as in *Merck*, can ([51], agreeing with Prof Yeo)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-7.5"]},{"k":"step","t":"The *Merck* story"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Two companies argued in England about what one clause of their old agreement meant, and the English court answered. In Singapore, one side tried to reopen the meaning by pointing to other clauses and saying the English ruling only covered the UK. The Court of Appeal held it to the English answer."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Merck US (Merck Sharp & Dohme) and Merck KGaA were bound by a 1970 co-existence agreement made by their predecessors. The English Court of Appeal had interpreted cl 7, applying German principles of interpretation. In Singapore, Merck US said it was not bound: more clauses (cll 5 and 6) were now pleaded, and the English decision covered only the UK. Both arguments failed, and one of them also ran into *Henderson v Henderson* (Module 4.3)."},{"p":"The lesson for problem questions: separate the issue that was decided (here, interpretation) from the issues that may differ between countries (here, breach)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"***Merck* [46]–[50]:** the English Court of Appeal's **interpretation of cl 7** of the 1970 co-existence agreement (applying German principles of interpretation) bound Merck US in Singapore.","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"Pleading further clauses did not change the issue: how cl 7 should be **interpreted** was identical [47]. The argument about the relationship between cll 5 and 6 was barred by *Henderson v Henderson* [47]."},{"t":"The English references to the UK concerned **breach**, not interpretation [48]."},{"t":"Applying German law to the facts was not a \"pure\" question of law, so it could found an estoppel [51]."}]}]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-7.2"]},{"k":"step","t":"Same words, different law: *Gonzalo Gil White*"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Two courts can discuss the same rule and still answer different questions. One asks, \"Is this rule valid under our constitution?\" The other asks, \"Did you break this rule as a term of your contract?\" Same name on the label, different question inside."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"*Gonzalo Gil White* shows how identity of issues works when foreign law is involved. The Mexican courts had decided issues about Art 115A by reference to Mexican law and public policy, including its constitutionality. In Singapore, Art 115A had been incorporated into a contract, and the question was whether it had been breached, under Singapore law. There was no identity of issues, for issue estoppel or for abuse of process: matching words, or even matching consequences, are not enough. As the Court put it, \"while Art 115A featured in both … that was where the commonality ended\"."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"***Gonzalo Gil White* [83]–[93]: identity of issues where foreign law is involved.** The test has two levels (drawing on *Kabab-Ji*):","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"(1) Were the issues **framed** the same way in both courts?"},{"t":"(2) If so, did the legal issues arise from **different laws**? If they did, and the differences are of substance and not just form, there is **no identity of issues** [87]."},{"t":"The question is identity of **issues**, not of consequences [91]."},{"t":"Public policy is unique to each State, so no issue estoppel can arise on the enforcing court's public policy (*India v DT* [86], quoted at [90])."},{"t":"On the facts, the Mexican courts decided the constitutionality of Art 115A under **Mexican** law. The Singapore proceedings concerned breach of Art 115A under **Singapore** law. So there was no identity of issues [92]–[93]."}]}]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-7.7"]},{"k":"step","t":"The outer limits"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Even a properly decided foreign point has edges. Some questions belong to Singapore alone, such as whether Singapore's own protective laws apply. Some foreign rulings speak only for the foreign country's territory. And a defendant who reasonably shrugged off a small claim abroad should not lose a big case in Singapore because of it."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"*Merck* sketched, provisionally, three \"outer limits\", each protecting something different. The first protects Singapore's own legal order: some issues the forum must decide for itself, such as whether a forum mandatory law (a Singapore law that applies whatever law governs the contract) strikes down a clause. The second respects the reach of the foreign ruling: a finding on the validity of a patent speaks only for that territory. The third protects a defendant who did not choose the foreign forum. The claimant chose it; the defendant may sensibly let a small claim in a distant court go by default, and may not be estopped if it had bona fide reasons for not fighting. A further limit, public policy ([58]), is covered in Week 13."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**The \"outer limits\"** are provisional (*Merck* [54]–[57], plus [58] on public policy, covered in Week 13; C&Y 5.110)."},{"t":"**Limit 1: issues the forum must decide for itself** [55]:","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"(a) where a **mandatory law of the forum** applies;"},{"t":"(b) where the issue engages **forum public policy**;"},{"t":"(c) where the issue is **procedural** for conflict of laws purposes. Example: a ruling by Country Y's court that Country X's judgment creates an issue estoppel does not bind Singapore."}]}]},{"t":"**Limit 2: territorially limited judgments** [56], such as findings on the validity of a patent, or on a particular country's public policy."},{"t":"**Limit 3: extra caution against a foreign defendant** [57], drawing on *Carl Zeiss* and Briggs. A defendant may sensibly let a small claim abroad go by default (\"it may have been a claim too small to be worth the expense of fighting\"). If it had **bona fide reasons** for not contesting, or not fully contesting, an issue, it may not be estopped. Estoppel may therefore apply *asymmetrically*."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-7.3","LAW302-Q12-7.4"]},{"k":"step","t":"Procedural issues: an unsettled boundary"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Some foreign rulings are about the rules of the game rather than who won: whether papers were properly served, or whether the court may hear the case at all. English courts say such rulings can sometimes bind. Singapore's Court of Appeal has hinted they cannot. Chong & Yip suggest a better question: is this a point Singapore must decide for itself?"}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Limit 1(c) opens a wider debate. *Merck* described Country Y's ruling as procedural and not on the merits, which Chong & Yip read as suggesting that procedural rulings cannot found an estoppel in Singapore. English law is more generous, as *The Sennar (No 2)* and *Desert Sun Loan v Hill* show. And Singapore itself accepts that issue estoppel can in principle arise from interlocutory decisions (ones made along the way, before the final judgment), which is where procedural points are usually decided. So the boundary is unsettled."},{"p":"Chong & Yip suggest dropping the procedure/merits label and asking whether the issue is one the forum keeps the prerogative to decide. International jurisdiction is the clearest example. Whether Bob submitted to Country X's court is for Singapore's own conflict rules (Module 3.5). If X's ruling that he submitted could estop him, the question would be \"sub-contracted out\" to the very court whose jurisdiction is in doubt."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"*Merck* [55] called a Country Y ruling on the estoppel effect of a Country X judgment \"a decision on a procedural matter … and not … a decision on the merits\". C&Y read this as suggesting that a procedural decision cannot found an issue estoppel (5.111)."},{"t":"**English law** allows issue estoppel on procedural as well as substantive issues (C&Y 5.112–5.113):","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"*The Sennar (No 2)*: a Dutch ruling that the court had no jurisdiction because of a Sudanese exclusive jurisdiction clause (a term sending disputes only to the Sudanese courts) was substantive, because it determined the parties' rights and obligations under the clause."},{"t":"*Desert Sun Loan v Hill*: a procedural issue decided in foreign interlocutory proceedings can found an estoppel if (1) the procedural or jurisdictional issue was expressly submitted to the foreign court, (2) the specific issue of fact was raised before and decided by that court, and (3) the *Carl Zeiss* caution is carefully borne in mind. On the facts there was no estoppel: the Arizona ruling that it had jurisdiction over Mr Hill did not cover the narrower issue of whether he had authorised another partner to instruct the US attorney to act for him personally."}]}]},{"t":"**Singapore:** \"on the merits\" does not appear to extend to \"procedural\" issues, yet issue estoppel can arise from interlocutory decisions (*Lakshmi* [101]), where procedural issues are typically decided. What counts as \"procedural\" needs unpacking (C&Y 5.115).","b":["open"]},{"t":"**C&Y's better approach:** ask whether the issue is one the **forum retains the prerogative to decide for itself**. Such issues generally relate to the proper administration of justice before the court or raise sovereignty issues (5.116).","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"**International jurisdiction**, including submission, is a prime example. Roch LJ in *Desert Sun*: the enforcing court decides submission under its own conflict rules, so no estoppel arises from the foreign court's ruling that the defendant submitted. Otherwise the question could be \"sub-contracted out\" to the foreign court (C&Y 5.117, 5.120).","b":["acad"]}]}],"b":["acad"]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-7.15"]},{"k":"step","t":"Forum conveniens rulings"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"When a case could be heard in two countries, each court asks, \"Am I the right place for this?\" Each answers that for itself. If the British Virgin Islands court says, \"I am the right place\", Singapore can still ask its own version of the question."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Two terms first. Forum non conveniens (FNC) is the principle that a court may stay (pause) its proceedings because another country's court is the more appropriate place for the trial. An anti-suit injunction (ASI) is an order restraining a party from pursuing proceedings in a foreign court."},{"p":"In *Lakshmi*, a court in the British Virgin Islands (BVI) refused to stay its proceedings in favour of Singapore on FNC grounds. The Court of Appeal held that this did not stop the Singapore court looking at the natural forum afresh when an ASI was sought. An ASI asks a different question, and otherwise whichever court heard its application second would always be shut out, so outcomes would turn on court scheduling."},{"p":"Chong & Yip take the point further. Even where both courts face parallel stay applications, a foreign ruling on forum conveniens should not bind Singapore in either direction, because FNC is a balancing exercise each court performs for itself."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"***Lakshmi* [99]–[105]:**","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"Issue estoppel **can in principle** arise from foreign interlocutory decisions [101]."},{"t":"But a foreign court's **refusal to stay** its proceedings on FNC grounds does **not** estop the losing party from seeking an **anti-suit injunction** in Singapore. The tests differ, since an ASI also requires vexation or oppression [99]. The contrary view would make outcomes depend on court scheduling, which would be arbitrary [103]."},{"t":"The foreign decision may still be relevant to **comity** [105]."}]}]},{"t":"**Parallel stay applications:** a foreign ruling on forum (non) conveniens does not oblige Singapore to hear the case, or to stay it. FNC is a balancing exercise in which each court decides for itself, and it is each court's prerogative to decide whether to assume jurisdiction (C&Y 5.119–5.120).","b":["acad"]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-7.6"]},{"k":"step","t":"Governing law, and the slide 21 drill"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Some foreign answers do travel. If, after a full trial, a foreign court decides which country's law governs a contract, that answer can bind the parties here, even if Singapore would have reasoned differently. A quick early view at a preliminary hearing cannot, because the foreign court could still change its mind at trial."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"The governing law of a contract is the contrast case. Unlike jurisdiction, it is not a question Singapore must keep for itself, so an estoppel can arise, but only from a ruling at the final trial, not from a provisional view on a \"good arguable case\" at an early stage. Nor does it matter that the foreign court's choice of law rules (the rules a court uses to identify the governing law) differ from Singapore's. The slide 21 drill then pulls the module together."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Governing law of a contract** (C&Y 5.120–5.121):","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"A foreign decision on the governing law can found an estoppel **if it was decided at trial**. A ruling at the interlocutory stage on a \"good arguable case\" standard is not final, because it can be reopened at trial."},{"t":"It does not matter that the foreign court used different choice of law rules (*First Laser*). C&Y: \"This must be correct\", since estoppel has never required that the later court would have reached the same answer (5.121).","b":["acad"]}]}]},{"t":"**Slide 21 drill:**","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"A foreign ruling that the foreign court is forum conveniens: **no** estoppel. Each court decides its own jurisdiction (*Lakshmi*; C&Y 5.119–5.120)."},{"t":"A judgment on a judgment: **no** (Module 3.2)."},{"t":"A foreign ruling that an exemption clause is valid, where the clause would be void under UCTA: **probably no**, *if* UCTA applies as a forum mandatory rule (*Merck* [55(a)])."},{"t":"A foreign ruling that the governing law is country X's law: **yes, potentially**, if decided at trial."}]}]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-7.8"]},{"k":"take","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Three elements: a foreign judgment capable of recognition, identity of issues and identity of parties, applied with special care (*Merck* [34]–[35], [40])."},{"t":"Identity of issues means the same issue, necessarily decided and final under the foreign law. The same words under different laws are not the same issue (*Gonzalo Gil White*)."},{"t":"The outer limits: issues the forum decides for itself, territorially limited judgments, and caution against foreign defendants. Public policy is in Week 13."},{"t":"A foreign stay ruling does not stop Singapore deciding the natural forum for itself (*Lakshmi*; slide 21). On C&Y's view, the same goes for international jurisdiction, including submission. A governing-law ruling made at trial can bind."}]}]},{"k":"traps","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"\"The same words appear in both cases\" does not mean the issues are identical (*Gonzalo Gil White*)."},{"t":"A stay ruling does not decide the natural forum for all purposes (*Lakshmi*)."},{"t":"A pure question of law cannot found an issue estoppel (*Merck* [51])."},{"t":"Reciprocity is not one of the outer limits. It was discussed separately and left open (*Merck* [39])."},{"t":"An interlocutory \"good arguable case\" ruling on governing law is not final."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":true,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-7.16","LAW302-Q12-7.17","LAW302-Q12-7.18","LAW302-Q12-7.19"]}],"ic":"target","nq":13},{"id":"w12.m4.3","code":"4.3","kind":"module","t":"Henderson v Henderson (the extended doctrine of res judicata)","blocks":[{"k":"obj","t":"Explain how the rule in *Henderson v Henderson* bars points that could and should have been raised abroad, apply the broad approach to abuse of process, and set it beside the open question of the *Arnold* exception."},{"k":"big","t":"Estoppel stops you contradicting what a foreign court decided; *Henderson* stops you holding back what you should have put to it in the first place."},{"k":"step","t":"Bring your whole case"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"You are expected to bring your whole case the first time. If you held back arguments you could have made abroad, raising them now in Singapore may be an abuse of process. It is like an exam: the time to answer a question is during the exam, not after the results are out. The analogy has a limit, though. Unlike an exam rule, the court will listen to a good reason for not raising the point earlier."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Cause of action estoppel and issue estoppel both depend on something the foreign court decided. The rule in *Henderson v Henderson* deals with what it never decided, because a party never raised it. It is called the extended doctrine of res judicata (res judicata being a matter decided once and for all). It was developed by the courts of Chancery and rests on abuse of process: using the court's procedures unfairly, for example by litigating a dispute in instalments."},{"p":"Notice how differently the argument runs. A party resisting an estoppel is trying to contradict what the foreign court decided. A party resisting *Henderson* says something else: the foreign court never concluded the whole dispute, so the new point is still open. A successful *Henderson* plea therefore shuts out matters that the foreign judgment did **not** make res judicata. The rule applies to foreign judgments as it does to local ones."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"A foreign judgment can be res judicata through the rule in ***Henderson v Henderson***, the extended doctrine of res judicata. It covers points that **could and should** have been raised before the foreign court but were not, and it is grounded in **abuse of process** (C&Y 5.103, 5.124; *Merck* [47], [64])."},{"t":"Wigram VC: the court requires the parties \"to bring forward their whole case\", and the plea applies \"to every point which properly belonged to the subject of litigation, and which the parties, exercising reasonable diligence, might have brought forward at the time\" (C&Y 5.124)."},{"t":"**How it differs from estoppel:** the party resisting it is not contradicting a foreign decision; it argues that the foreign court did not conclude the whole claim. A successful plea shuts out matters that the foreign judgment did not make res judicata (C&Y 5.125)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-7.20"]},{"k":"step","t":"A broad look at all the circumstances"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"The rule is not a trapdoor that opens whenever someone forgot an argument. The court asks whether, all things considered, raising the point now is unfair to the other side."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Read literally, Wigram VC's words could bar every point that belonged to the earlier case. The courts no longer apply them so mechanically. In *Humpuss*, the High Court held that the court looks at all the circumstances of the case: whether the later proceedings are a collateral attack on the earlier decision (an indirect attempt to undermine it), whether there is fresh evidence, whether there are bona fide reasons why the point was not raised earlier, and any other special circumstances. Two consequences follow. The parties need not be the same in the foreign and the Singapore proceedings. And failing to raise a point that belonged to the earlier case does not automatically make raising it now an abuse."}]},{"k":"call","c":"example","n":[{"p":"Bob sued Mei in Country X for the price of goods and won after a full trial. Mei had complained about the goods' quality all along but chose not to counterclaim in X. She now sues Bob in Singapore for damages for defective goods, and Bob says this is an abuse of process. The court will ask whether her claim is in substance a collateral attack on the X judgment, whether she has fresh evidence, and whether she had a bona fide reason for holding the claim back."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"The court decides whether there is an abuse by looking at **all the circumstances** of the case, considering in particular (*Humpuss* [63]; C&Y 5.126):","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"whether the later proceedings are a **collateral attack** on the earlier decision;"},{"t":"whether there is **fresh evidence** that might warrant relitigation;"},{"t":"whether there are **bona fide reasons** why an issue that ought to have been raised earlier was not;"},{"t":"whether other special circumstances justify allowing the case to proceed."}]}]},{"t":"The parties **need not be the same** in the foreign and Singapore proceedings (*Humpuss* [61]; C&Y 5.126)."},{"t":"Not raising a point that belonged to the earlier litigation does not **automatically** make raising it later an abuse (*Humpuss* [60], [63]; C&Y 5.126)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-7.21"]},{"k":"step","t":"*Henderson* and *Arnold* across borders"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Two doctrines sit at the edges of res judicata and pull in opposite directions. One (*Arnold*) can, in rare cases, reopen a point that was decided wrongly. The other (*Henderson*) closes off points that were never raised. For foreign judgments, the first is uncertain; the second clearly applies."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"*Merck* used *Henderson* against Merck US: its argument about the relationship between cll 5 and 6 could have been raised in England, so it could not be raised for the first time in Singapore (Module 4.2). *Henderson* may do even more work across borders than at home. Because transnational issue estoppel is applied with special care, fewer points are caught by estoppel, and abuse of process acts as a safety net."},{"p":"The *Arnold* exception points the other way. For local judgments, it allows an issue estoppel to be reopened in narrow, special circumstances, notably where the earlier decision was clearly wrong because of a point that could not reasonably have been argued at the time. Whether it applies to foreign judgments is open. Prof Yeo set out four possible approaches; the Court of Appeal found difficulties with each and declined to decide."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"*Henderson* applies to foreign judgments. In *Merck*, the argument about the relationship between cll 5 and 6, which could have been raised in England, was barred [47]. *Henderson* may do more work across borders, because issue estoppel is applied more narrowly there (*Merck* [62]–[65])."},{"t":"**The *Arnold* exception** allows a local issue estoppel to be reopened in narrow circumstances, including that the decision was **clearly wrong** because of a point that was not, and could not reasonably have been, taken or argued, and that great injustice would otherwise result (C&Y 5.122)."},{"t":"Whether and how *Arnold* applies to foreign judgments is **open** (*Merck* [62]–[65]).","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"The four possible approaches (*Merck* [63]; C&Y 5.123): (1) *Arnold* does not apply to foreign judgments; (2) it applies to issue estoppel but not to cause of action estoppel; (3) it has a wider scope than for local judgments, with working justice as the overriding consideration; (4) the conclusiveness of the specific issue is tested by the law of the court of origin."},{"t":"Applying the \"clearly wrong\" requirement to a foreign judgment sits uneasily with the rule against reviewing the merits ([63])."}]}],"b":["open"]},{"t":"C&Y prefer the fourth approach, which matches the general principle that finality is tested by the law of the court of origin (5.123; Module 3.6).","b":["acad"]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-7.9"]},{"k":"take","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"*Henderson* bars points that could and should have been raised abroad. It rests on abuse of process, not on anything the foreign court decided."},{"t":"The court looks at all the circumstances: collateral attack, fresh evidence, bona fide reasons and special circumstances. The parties need not be the same."},{"t":"*Merck* applied *Henderson* to the English decisions, and it may do more work across borders than at home."},{"t":"Whether the *Arnold* exception applies to foreign judgments is open; C&Y prefer testing conclusiveness by the law of the court of origin."}]}]},{"k":"traps","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Do not require the point to have been decided abroad. That is estoppel, not *Henderson*."},{"t":"Do not require the same parties (*Humpuss* [61])."},{"t":"Do not treat *Henderson* as automatic: fresh evidence and bona fide reasons can save a point."},{"t":"Do not say *Merck* decided the *Arnold* question either way."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":true,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-7.22"]}],"ic":"refresh","nq":4}]},{"id":"w12.s5","n":"5","title":"Requirements compared across the three regimes","sub":"Compare how the common law, REFJA and the CCAA deal with each requirement for giving effect to a foreign judgment, explain why they differ, and use the differences to pick the right route and argue it.","lessons":[{"id":"w12.s5","code":"Stage 5","kind":"stage","t":"Requirements compared across the three regimes","blocks":[{"k":"obj","t":"Compare how the common law, REFJA and the CCAA deal with each requirement for giving effect to a foreign judgment, explain why they differ, and use the differences to pick the right route and argue it."},{"k":"big","t":"All three regimes ask the same basic questions: was the foreign court entitled to judge, is the judgment final, and is there a defence? The statutes fix more of the answers in advance (REFJA through reciprocity and its own list of jurisdiction grounds, the CCAA through the parties' choice of court), so procedure is simpler and, under the CCAA, Singapore asks least about jurisdiction."},{"k":"step","t":"Three doors, one framework"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"There are three doors into the same room. The **CCAA** door (chosen courts in Hague Convention countries) asks the fewest questions about the foreign court. The **REFJA** door (listed countries) works by registration and has its own list of jurisdiction grounds. The **common law** door (everyone else) needs a fresh case in Singapore and the full common law checklist."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"The room is the same whichever door Mei uses: Bob's assets in Singapore, or a defence to a claim Bob brings here. What changes is how closely Singapore inspects the judgment on the way in."},{"p":"The common law is the default. It deals with judgments from anywhere in the world, so it trusts least and checks most. Each statute rests on an arrangement with other countries. REFJA covers the recognised courts of countries **gazetted** (listed by Ministerial order) because they give \"substantial reciprocity\" to Singapore judgments (s 3(1)). The CCAA enacts the Hague Convention on Choice of Court Agreements. It covers the court chosen in an **exclusive choice of court agreement** (an agreement that disputes go only to that court) in a **Contracting State** (a country that has joined the Convention) (s 2(1))."},{"p":"Underneath, the skeleton is shared. REFJA, based on the UK Foreign Judgments (Reciprocal Enforcement) Act 1933, is modelled on the common law: the foreign court must have had **international jurisdiction** (the right, judged by Singapore's standards, to decide a case against the defendant), the judgment must be final and conclusive, and no defence must apply (C&Y 5.147–5.148). So master the common law checklist (Stage 3), then learn what each statute changes."},{"p":"**Reciprocity** (giving effect to a country's judgments only if it does the same for yours) shows the pattern. At common law, whether Singapore should require it is open (*Merck* [39]). The statutes settle it once, for a whole country, through gazetting or membership of the Convention, so no creditor has to prove it case by case."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Reciprocity at common law:** a possible requirement that *Merck* [39] left undecided.","b":["open"]},{"t":"**Which door:** the CCAA prevails over REFJA; REFJA bars a common law action on a judgment within its scope (s 7(1)); the CCAA does not exclude the common law (s 21; C&Y 5.173, 5.200–5.201; Stage 1)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-11.1"]},{"k":"step","t":"What kinds of judgment can come through"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Think about what each door lets Mei carry through. The common law door cashes only a cheque for a fixed sum; anything else she may show at the door, but not cash. The REFJA door, widened in 2019, also takes orders to do things and some temporary orders, though non-money orders get a closer look. The CCAA door takes finished decisions of every kind, money or not, but no temporary orders."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Why is the common law the narrowest door? Mei does not enforce the foreign judgment itself. She sues Bob on the debt that it creates (C&Y 5.12), in an action descended from the old action of *indebitatus assumpsit*, a claim for a sum owed (*Giant Light* [62]–[63]). An order to hand over shares, or to stop selling goods, cannot be turned into a debt, so it can be recognised but not enforced (C&Y 5.31)."},{"p":"The statutes do not depend on an action in debt: they give the foreign judgment the force of a local one. So the 2019 amendments could open REFJA to non-money judgments and to **interlocutory** judgments (orders made before the final decision, for example to preserve assets), and the CCAA covers non-money judgments too. REFJA keeps two brakes: a \"just and convenient\" test for non-money judgments, and gazette orders that may cover less than the Act allows (C&Y 5.151)."},{"p":"Only REFJA can register an interim order such as a freezing order: it is not final, so the common law cannot enforce it, and the CCAA excludes interim measures. At common law, though, an interlocutory decision that finally decides a specific, non-procedural issue can still be recognised, but not enforced unless it is a final money judgment. A mere stamp of approval (a judgment that only declares another country's judgment enforceable) is excluded from REFJA and is unlikely to be enforced at common law: the High Court refused one in *Sang Cheol Woo*."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Common law:** enforcement needs a fresh money judgment for a fixed sum that is not a tax, fine or penalty; recognition is available for any final judgment on the merits (C&Y 5.09, 5.20).","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"That includes an interlocutory decision that finally decides a specific, non-procedural issue (C&Y 5.23, 5.59), which can in principle found an issue estoppel (*Lakshmi* [101])."}]}]},{"t":"**REFJA:** money and non-money judgments, lower-court and interlocutory judgments, consent judgments and orders, and judicial settlements (s 2(1); C&Y 5.152–5.153). A non-money judgment is registered only if \"just and convenient\", otherwise its monetary equivalent may be (s 4(4); C&Y 5.156). Judgments for taxes, fines or penalties are excluded (C&Y 5.154)."},{"t":"**CCAA:** final decisions on the merits, consent and default judgments, and costs determinations, including non-money judgments (s 2(1)); interim measures (s 10) and procedural rulings are excluded, as they are not decisions on the merits (C&Y 5.179). Judicial settlements can be enforced (s 20)."},{"t":"**Judgment on a judgment:** unlikely to be enforced at common law (*Sang Cheol Woo* [106]–[112]; Module 3.2); excluded from REFJA (s 3(4)(c); C&Y 5.158)."},{"t":"C&Y argue that the common law should follow the statutes and enforce non-money judgments too (C&Y 5.21, 5.31).","b":["acad","miss"]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-11.2"]},{"k":"step","t":"Is it final? Home law, statute, or home effect"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"The common law asks how the judgment stands back home: is it settled there? The CCAA asks whether it still works back home. REFJA writes its own rule: a judgment counts as final even while an appeal is pending, and temporary orders need not be final at all. Under both statutes, a judgment that can no longer be enforced at home cannot be registered or enforced here."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Finality protects Bob from paying on a decision that the foreign court could still change. At common law it also respects the foreign system's own view of its judgment: as Lord Reid said in *Carl Zeiss*, it would \"verge on absurdity\" to treat as conclusive what the foreign courts themselves would not (Module 3.6)."},{"p":"So at common law the court that gave the judgment must be unable to vary, reopen or set it aside, and the foreign law must treat it as **res judicata** (a matter finally decided between the parties). A pending appeal is not automatically fatal, unless the foreign law withholds finality until appeals end, as Korean law did in *Sang Cheol Woo*."},{"p":"REFJA sets its own rule. Unless the judgment is interlocutory, it must be final and conclusive, and the Act deems it so even while an appeal is pending or still possible; the court can instead pause matters until the appeal is decided. A judgment that can no longer be enforced in its country of origin cannot be registered."},{"p":"The CCAA states the \"home effect\" idea most directly: a judgment gets no more effect in Singapore than it has in the State of origin."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Common law:** both aspects of finality, judged by the law of the foreign court and proved by expert evidence (*Humpuss* [69]–[70]; *Sang Cheol Woo* [31]–[42]; C&Y 5.59). A judgment under appeal can still be final, though enforcement may be stayed pending the appeal (C&Y 5.59); but if the foreign law withholds res judicata status until appeals end, Singapore follows it (*Sang Cheol Woo* [43]–[48])."},{"t":"**REFJA:** final and conclusive between the parties unless it is an interlocutory judgment (s 3(2)(b)); deemed final even if an appeal is pending or still possible (s 3(5)). The court may set aside the registration, or adjourn the application to set it aside, until the appeal is disposed of (s 6(1); C&Y 5.157). Not registrable if it could not be enforced in the country of the foreign court (s 4(3); C&Y 5.160)."},{"t":"**CCAA:** recognised only if it has effect, and enforced only if it is enforceable, in the State of origin (s 13(2); C&Y 5.183)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-11.3"]},{"k":"step","t":"Was the foreign court entitled to judge? The biggest difference"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Picture three door staff checking whether the foreign court had the right to judge Bob. The common law guard checks Bob's ID: was he there when served, or did he agree to take part? The REFJA guard works from a printed list that mostly matches. The CCAA guard asks only: is this the court Bob and Mei chose? If it is, nobody asks how that court took jurisdiction. (The analogy stops there: Bob can still argue, as a ground for refusal, that the agreement itself was invalid. That is Week 13.)"}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"This is where the regimes differ most, and where problem questions most often turn."},{"p":"At common law Singapore applies its own rules: Bob's presence in the country when he was served, or his **submission**, meaning that he accepted the court's authority by conduct, by agreement, or by inchoate submission (Modules 3.4–3.5). REFJA swaps these rules for a statutory list of grounds on which the foreign court is **deemed** (treated by law) to have had jurisdiction. The list \"largely replicate[s]\" the common law, with four differences (C&Y 5.163)."},{"p":"The CCAA asks least. Being the chosen court, or a court to which it transferred the case, is enough, whatever ground that court relied on (C&Y 5.178). The parties' exclusive choice is the foundation of the Convention, under which the chosen court's judgment must be recognised in the other Contracting States (C&Y 5.177)."}]},{"k":"call","c":"example","n":[{"p":"Bob asks Country Y's court to stay Mei's case on grounds of **forum non conveniens** (another country is clearly more appropriate), loses, and takes no further part. Under REFJA that was not a submission (s 5(3)(a)(i)(C)). At common law, *Henry v Geoprosco* would treat it as one, but that case is undecided in Singapore and was rejected by the Privy Council in *IGCF v Al Jomaih* (Module 3.5)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Common law:** presence when served, or submission by conduct, by agreement or by inchoate submission, judged by Singapore's rules (*India v Deutsche Telekom* [65]; Modules 3.4–3.5).","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"Whether residence without presence is enough is unsettled (C&Y 5.36–5.37).","b":["open","miss"]}]}]},{"t":"**REFJA deemed grounds** for judgments in personam (s 5(3)(a); C&Y 5.162): (i) a voluntary appearance, other than to protect seized property, to contest jurisdiction, or to invite the court in its discretion not to exercise jurisdiction; (ii) being a claimant or counterclaimant; (iii) an agreement, before the proceedings and in respect of their subject-matter, to submit to that court or the courts of that country; (iv) residence, or for a company its principal place of business, in the country when the proceedings were instituted; (v) an office or place of business there, where the proceedings concern a transaction effected through or at that office."},{"t":"**Differences from the common law** (C&Y 5.163): *Henry v Geoprosco* is excluded (s 5(3)(a)(i)(C)); mere presence is not enough; the corporate test differs from *Adams v Cape*; s 5(3)(a)(v) has no common law equivalent."},{"t":"If the foreign court had no jurisdiction, the registration **must** be set aside (s 5(1)(a)(ii); C&Y 5.162)."},{"t":"**CCAA:** a judgment of the chosen court qualifies whatever ground of jurisdiction that court relied on (s 2(1); C&Y 5.178)."}]}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"The direction of REFJA's differences is telling: it favours lasting connections, such as residence or a principal place of business, over a fleeting visit, and it adds a ground that links a local office to the deal sued on."}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-11.4"]},{"k":"step","t":"How Mei gets through, and how long she has"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"At the common law door, Mei must start a whole new case, and she has six years. At the REFJA door, she registers the judgment, again within six years. At the CCAA door, she makes an application, and the Act sets no deadline, though the judgment must still work back home."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"The procedure follows the trust: a fresh action at common law, usually by **summary judgment** (judgment without a full trial, because Bob has no real defence), which C&Y call more cumbersome than registration; registration under REFJA; a simple application under the CCAA."},{"p":"The time limits follow the procedure. The common law action is on an **implied debt** (the obligation to pay that the judgment creates), so the six-year limit for debts applies, not the twelve years for Singapore judgments. Time runs from when the judgment became final and conclusive under the foreign law; C&Y 5.97 dates it from the judgment itself, but the course follows *Sang Cheol Woo*. REFJA gives six years to register, with a special rule for appellate judgments. Neither the CCAA nor O 37 sets a limitation period, and C&Y conclude that none applies, as there is no implied debt; but a judgment whose enforcement is time-barred at home cannot be enforced here either."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Procedure:** at common law, a fresh action, usually by summary judgment (C&Y 5.12–5.13); under REFJA, registration, after which the judgment is enforced as if it were a local judgment (C&Y 5.149); under the CCAA, an application under s 13 (O 37 ROC 2021), with no fresh action (C&Y 5.181)."},{"t":"**Common law time limit:** 6 years under LA s 6(1)(a), because the action is on an implied debt (*Poh Soon Kiat* [41]–[54]), running from when the judgment became final and conclusive under the foreign law (*Sang Cheol Woo* [43]–[48])."},{"t":"**REFJA time limit:** 6 years from the judgment (s 4(1)(a)); an appellate judgment may be registered at any time after the last appellate judgment (s 4(1)(b); C&Y 5.150).","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"The High Court has said that the 6-year limit should also apply to appellate judgments (C&Y 5.150).","b":["obiter"]}]}]},{"t":"**CCAA:** no limitation period in the Act or in O 37; the judgment must remain enforceable in the State of origin (s 13(2); C&Y 5.181).","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"C&Y conclude, following Prof Yeo, that no limitation period applies, because the implied-debt reasoning does not apply (C&Y 5.181).","b":["acad","miss"]}]}]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-11.5"]},{"k":"step","t":"The full comparison"},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Each row below compares the three regimes on one issue: **CL** (common law) | **REFJA** | **CCAA**. Use it to revise, and to check a problem answer: once you know the door, run down its column."}]},{"k":"cmp","cols":[["CL","Common law"],["REFJA","REFJA"],["CCAA","CCAA"]],"rows":[{"h":"Courts covered","cells":{"CL":"any court.","REFJA":"recognised courts of gazetted countries (s 3).","CCAA":"the chosen court of a Contracting State, or a court it transferred the case to (s 2(1))."},"note":[]},{"h":"Kinds of judgment","cells":{"CL":"enforcement requires a fresh money judgment; recognition is available for any final judgment on the merits.","REFJA":"money and non-money judgments, interlocutory judgments, consent judgments and orders, judicial settlements. Non-money judgments only if \"just and convenient\" (s 4(4)).","CCAA":"final decisions on the merits, consent and default judgments, costs determinations; judicial settlements can be enforced (s 20)."},"note":[]},{"h":"Finality","cells":{"CL":"both aspects, judged by the foreign law.","REFJA":"final and conclusive, unless interlocutory; a pending appeal does not prevent finality (s 3(2), (5)).","CCAA":"the judgment must have effect or be enforceable in the State of origin (s 13(2))."},"note":[]},{"h":"International jurisdiction","cells":{"CL":"presence (residence is open) or submission (by conduct, agreement or inchoate submission).","REFJA":"deemed grounds in s 5(3): submission (excluding the protective appearances in s 5(3)(a)(i)(A)–(C)), being a claimant or counterclaimant, prior agreement, residence or principal place of business, or an office in the foreign country through which the transaction was done. Mere presence is **not** enough.","CCAA":"the chosen court; the basis on which it took jurisdiction is irrelevant."},"note":[]},{"h":"Judgment on a judgment","cells":{"CL":"unlikely to be enforced (*Sang Cheol Woo*).","REFJA":"excluded (s 3(4)(c)).","CCAA":"n/a."},"note":[]},{"h":"Procedure","cells":{"CL":"fresh action, usually summary judgment.","REFJA":"registration, then enforcement as if a local judgment.","CCAA":"application under s 13 (O 37)."},"note":[]},{"h":"Time limit","cells":{"CL":"6 years under LA s 6(1)(a), running from when the judgment became final and conclusive (*Sang Cheol Woo*).","REFJA":"6 years to register, or any time after the last appellate judgment (s 4(1)).","CCAA":"none in the Act."},"note":[]},{"h":"Effect on the common law","cells":{"CL":"n/a.","REFJA":"bars a common law recovery action (s 7(1)).","CCAA":"the common law is not excluded (s 21)."},"note":[]},{"h":"Reciprocity","cells":{"CL":"open (*Merck* [39]).","REFJA":"built in through gazetting.","CCAA":"built in, since the regime operates between Contracting States."},"note":[]}]},{"k":"take","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"One framework (international jurisdiction, finality, no defences); the regimes differ in how much Singapore checks for itself."},{"t":"The common law enforces only fresh money judgments; REFJA and the CCAA reach non-money judgments, and only REFJA can register interim orders such as freezing orders."},{"t":"International jurisdiction differs most: Singapore's own rules at common law, deemed grounds under REFJA, and only \"is this the chosen court?\" under the CCAA."},{"t":"Finality: the foreign law decides at common law; REFJA has its own rule (final despite a pending or possible appeal; interlocutory judgments exempt); the CCAA asks about effect and enforceability in the State of origin. Time: 6 years at common law (from finality) and for REFJA registration; none in the CCAA."}]}]},{"k":"traps","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Do not carry the common law presence rule into REFJA. Mere presence is not a deemed ground, and a company is tested by its principal place of business."},{"t":"Do not ask whether a CCAA chosen court had jurisdiction under Singapore's rules. Being the chosen court is enough for jurisdiction; the CCAA's own grounds of refusal (Week 13) come later."},{"t":"Do not apply the common law six-year limit to a CCAA application, or bring a common law action on a judgment within REFJA (s 7(1))."},{"t":"Do not treat REFJA's wider scope as automatic: non-money judgments must pass the \"just and convenient\" test, and a gazette order may cover less than the Act."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":true,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-11.6"]}],"ic":"layers","nq":6}]},{"id":"w12.s6","n":"6","title":"Wrap-up: the whole topic as one story","sub":"Pull Week 12 together into one order of analysis, and use it on problem questions that mix several issues.","lessons":[{"id":"w12.s6","code":"Stage 6","kind":"stage","t":"Wrap-up: the whole topic as one story","blocks":[{"k":"obj","t":"Pull Week 12 together into one order of analysis, and use it on problem questions that mix several issues."},{"k":"big","t":"Every foreign judgment problem runs in the same order: what the client wants, which regime applies, whether the judgment meets the requirements, and what it can do once recognised."},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Mei sues Bob in Country X and wins."},{"ol":[{"t":"**What does Mei want?** To make Bob pay (enforcement), or to stop Bob suing her again on the same thing (recognition, used as a shield). Stage 0."},{"t":"**Which door?** CCAA, REFJA or the common law. Stage 1 and Stage 5."},{"t":"**Was X's court allowed to judge Bob?** Bob was physically there when served, or Bob agreed to take part (by fighting the case or by an earlier agreement). Module 3.4 and Module 3.5."},{"t":"**Is the decision finished and about who is right?** Finished under X's own law, and on the merits. Module 3.1 and Module 3.6."},{"t":"**If Mei wants money:** is it a fixed sum, and not a fine or a tax? Module 3.3."},{"t":"**What can the judgment do in Singapore?** Block the same claim (cause of action estoppel), block re-arguing the same point (issue estoppel), or block arguments Bob should have raised before (*Henderson*). Stage 4."}]},{"p":"Week 13 then asks whether Bob has a defence: fraud, natural justice, public policy, and so on. The practice round at the end of this lesson lets you check yourself."}]},{"k":"step","t":"Start with the door"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Before checking Mei's ticket, find out which door she is standing at. Each door has its own procedure, its own jurisdiction rules and its own deadline, and one door locks another."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"The regime decides almost everything that follows, so identify it first. Is the judgment from the court chosen in an exclusive choice of court agreement, in a Contracting State to the Hague Convention? Then the CCAA applies. If not, is it from a recognised court of a country gazetted under REFJA, and within the Act? Then, to enforce it, Mei must register it: a common law action to recover the sum is barred (s 7(1)). To use it only as a shield, she needs no registration (s 11). Everything else goes through the common law."},{"p":"Getting the door wrong is costly, because the tests differ too: a short visit during which Bob is served is enough for jurisdiction at common law, but not under REFJA, and under the CCAA nobody asks how the chosen court took jurisdiction (Stage 5)."}]},{"k":"call","c":"tip","n":[{"p":"State the regime, and your reason for it, in your first two sentences. If the facts leave it unclear (for example, whether a jurisdiction clause was exclusive, or whether the country is gazetted), answer in the alternative."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**CCAA Part 3:** judgments of the chosen court of a Contracting State under an exclusive choice of court agreement (s 2(1), s 13). It prevails over REFJA (C&Y 5.200) and leaves the common law open (s 21; C&Y 5.201)."},{"t":"**REFJA:** judgments of recognised courts of gazetted countries (s 3). Proceedings to recover the sum other than by registration are barred (s 7(1)); recognition needs no registration (s 11; C&Y 5.172)."},{"t":"**Common law:** all other judgments."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-9.5"]},{"k":"step","t":"Then the requirements, in order"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Now check the ticket itself. Was the court allowed to judge Bob? Is the decision finished, and did it decide who was right? If Mei wants money, is it for a fixed sum? Mei has to answer every one of these questions. Bob's complaints come later."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"At common law the party relying on the judgment must show that it meets the requirements (on a summary judgment application the burden is the creditor's: C&Y 5.13); defences are for the judgment debtor to prove (C&Y 5.65; Week 13). Follow the Stage 3 checklist. Start with international jurisdiction, which is often decisive in problem questions. Then ask whether the judgment is on the merits, and final and conclusive under the foreign law. Last, and for enforcement only, ask whether it is a fresh money judgment for a fixed sum that is not a tax, fine or penalty."},{"p":"Deal briefly with every requirement, even when one looks decisive. Problem questions often plant facts that sound fatal but are not: a default judgment is still on the merits and can be final, and a judgment that can still be appealed may well be final under the foreign law."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**The requirements** (*Humpuss*, cited in *Ong Han Nam* [48]; *India v Deutsche Telekom* [64]–[65]; C&Y 5.20): on the merits; for enforcement, a fresh money judgment that is not a tax, fine or penalty; international jurisdiction under Singapore's rules; final and conclusive; no defences (Week 13)."},{"t":"**Reciprocity:** not imposed by *Merck* [39].","b":["open"]},{"t":"**Default judgments** are on the merits and can be final (C&Y 5.23, 5.59). A judgment under appeal can still be final, though enforcement may be stayed (C&Y 5.59)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-9.6"]},{"k":"step","t":"Finally, what the judgment can do"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Once the ticket passes the checks, Mei can cash it (if it is for money) or hold it up as a shield. Even a ticket that cannot be cashed can stop Bob re-arguing what was decided."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"A recognised money judgment can be enforced. Any recognised judgment can also be used as **res judicata** (a matter already decided). Cause of action estoppel blocks the same claim; issue estoppel blocks re-arguing a point already decided between the same parties, subject to the *Merck* elements and outer limits; and *Henderson v Henderson* blocks points that could and should have been raised abroad (Stage 4). On the Court of Appeal's preliminary view, a cause of action does not merge into a foreign judgment, so Mei can also sue again on her original claim and plead the judgment as res judicata, which helps where it cannot be enforced (*Ocean Tankers* [95]–[96]; C&Y 5.105). A shield needs no special procedure: at common law the judgment is simply pleaded (*Merck* [35]), and under REFJA it needs no registration (s 11; C&Y 5.172)."}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-9.3"]},{"k":"take","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Start with the client's goal and the regime. The regime decides the procedure and the tests."},{"t":"At common law, Mei must show the requirements: international jurisdiction, a judgment on the merits, finality and, for enforcement, a fixed sum."},{"t":"International jurisdiction is judged by Singapore's rules at common law, by the deemed grounds under REFJA, and by the parties' choice under the CCAA."},{"t":"A recognised judgment works as a sword (enforcement) or a shield (estoppel), and needs no registration to work as a shield."}]}]},{"k":"traps","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Do not run the common law checklist on a judgment within REFJA: the common law action is barred (s 7(1)). A CCAA application is tested under the CCAA. If Mei chooses the common law route for a CCAA judgment instead (s 21), the full common law checklist applies."},{"t":"Do not stop at the first requirement that fails; deal briefly with the rest."},{"t":"Do not assume that a default judgment, or a judgment open to appeal, fails the merits or finality requirements."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":true,"ids":["LAW302-Q12-9.1","LAW302-Q12-9.2","LAW302-Q12-9.4","LAW302-Q12-9.7","LAW302-Q12-9.8"]}],"ic":"trophy","nq":8}]}]},{"id":"w13","num":"13","label":"Week 13","t":"Foreign Judgments II","full":"Defences, anti-enforcement injunctions and the CCAA","sub":"The common law defences, anti-enforcement injunctions, Part 3 of the Choice of Court Agreements Act 2016, and the three regimes side by side.","color":["#7B4FA0","#5A3778","#F1E9F7"],"about":{"built":"Week 13 handout (AY2026-27), FJ Part 2 slides (Dr Adeline Chong), Chong & Yip Ch 5 (B.4.f, E, G), and every case and statute on the Week 13 reading list. Week 12 materials are used only for the prerequisite recap in Stage 0.","abbr":"C&Y = Chong & Yip, *Singapore Private International Law: Commercial Issues and Practice*; CCAA = Choice of Court Agreements Act 2016; REFJA = Reciprocal Enforcement of Foreign Judgments Act 1959; RECJA = Reciprocal Enforcement of Commonwealth Judgments Act (now repealed); CLA = Civil Law Act 1909; LA = Limitation Act 1959; AEI = anti-enforcement injunction; ASI = anti-suit injunction; PP = public policy; SG = Singapore. Paragraph pinpoints are in [square brackets].","secs":[{"title":"Week 13: complete (every required reading is in the project)","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"CCAA ss 2(1), 13, 14, 15, 16, 19, 20, 21 ✓"},{"t":"LA s 6(1)(a) and s 6(3) ✓"},{"t":"*Merck Sharp & Dohme Corp v Merck KGaA* [2021] 1 SLR 1102 (CA) [36]–[38], [58]–[61] ✓"},{"t":"*Ong Han Nam v Borneo Ventures Pte Ltd* [2021] 1 SLR 1248 (CA) [48]–[56] ✓"},{"t":"*Ee Hoong Liang v Panirvelcan s/o Kaliannan* [2022] SGHC(A) 40 [10]–[19] ✓"},{"t":"*Israel Discount Bank of New York v Hadjipateras* [1984] 1 WLR 137: the case itself is not in the project, but the handout says the video or textbook explanation is enough. It is covered by C&Y 5.83 and slide 4 ✓"},{"t":"*Poh Soon Kiat v Desert Palace Inc* [2010] 1 SLR 1129 (CA) [36]–[54], [120]–[121] ✓"},{"t":"*Venetian Macau v Hu* [2026] SGHC 180 [26]–[56], [59]–[60] ✓"},{"t":"*Paulus Tannos v Heince Tombak Simanjuntak* [2020] 2 SLR 1061 (CA) ✓"},{"t":"*Sang Cheol Woo v Spackman, Charles Choi* [2022] SGHC 298 [68]–[101] ✓"},{"t":"*Alberto Justo Rodriguez Licea v Curacao Drydock Co, Inc* [2015] 4 SLR 172 [28]–[30] ✓"},{"t":"*The Republic of the Philippines v Maler Foundation* [2014] 1 SLR 1389 [68] ✓"},{"t":"*Sun Travels & Tours Pvt Ltd v Hilton International Manage (Maldives) Pvt Ltd* [2019] 1 SLR 732 (CA) [88]–[114] ✓"},{"t":"C&Y Ch 5, Sections B.4.f (found inside the \"A, B, D\" file), E and G ✓"},{"t":"FJ Part 2 slides ✓"},{"t":"Optional readings not in the project (not needed): C&Y (2019) 15 JPIL 97 at 123–128; Yeo, \"Foreign Judgments and Contracts: The Anti-Enforcement Injunction\"."}]}]},{"title":"Week 12: not complete","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Two required secondary readings are missing: Yeo, \"Common Law Developments Relating to Foreign Judgments\" (9th YPH Lecture, 2016) and Yeo, \"The Changing Global Landscape for Foreign Judgments\" (YPH Lecture, 2021)."},{"t":"The Week 12 cases, statutes, C&Y Ch 5 A/B/D and slides are all present. Stage 0 below relies on these. The companion Week 12 file covers that week in full, and marks where the two missing lectures would most likely add commentary."}]}]},{"title":"Note on the handout","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"The Week 13 seminar outline cites REFJA \"s 5(3)(b)\" for proceedings brought in breach of a dispute-resolution agreement. In the current Act (the version in the project) and on slide 6, the provision is **s 5(4)(b)**. This file uses s 5(4)(b)."}]}]},{"title":"Textbook enrichment (this edition)","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"The lessons were expanded with in-scope material from **C&Y Ch 5**: Sections A (introduction, corrected as in B3), B.1–B.6 (the common law rules, defences, severance and estoppel), D (REFJA), E (CCAA Part 3) and G (anti-enforcement injunctions)."},{"t":"Mentioned only, not tested: B.7 (judgments in rem beyond the in personam / in rem distinction), C (the repealed RECJA, where the cases still refer to it) and F (the Hague Judgments Convention 2019)."},{"t":"Left out as outside the course: H (the Singapore Convention on Mediation)."},{"t":"Where the textbook (2023) and the course materials differ, the lessons follow the handout, the slides and the later cases."}]}]}]},"stages":[{"id":"w13.s0","n":"0","title":"Prerequisite recap (Week 12 in one page)","sub":"Know what must already be true before any \"defence\" becomes relevant.","lessons":[{"id":"w13.s0","code":"Stage 0","kind":"stage","t":"Prerequisite recap (Week 12 in one page)","blocks":[{"k":"obj","t":"Know what must already be true before any \"defence\" becomes relevant."},{"k":"big","t":"Check the requirements first and the defences second. A judgment that fails a requirement never needs a defence."},{"k":"step","t":"Requirements first, defences second"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Imagine a friend won a prize from a school in another town and wants your school to honour it. Before anyone argues \"they cheated\", your school checks the basics: was that other school allowed to judge your friend at all, and is its decision finished, not a draft? Only after those boxes are ticked do complaints come in. So the order is always: requirements first, defences second."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"This week is about the complaints, which lawyers call **defences**. They matter only once a judgment has passed the basic checks from Week 12. Here is that week in one page, told through Mei, who has won a judgment against Bob in Country X and wants a Singapore court to give it effect."},{"p":"At common law the test has three limbs: two **requirements** about the judgment, then the absence of any **defence**. \"Final and conclusive\" means the court that gave the judgment treats it as settled and cannot reopen it. To **recognise** a judgment is to accept what it decided as binding; to **enforce** it is to make the debtor pay."},{"p":"The **burden of proof** (the job of proving a point) follows the same split: Mei proves the requirements, Bob proves any defence. If Mei fails on a requirement, Bob never needs a defence."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**The regimes.** There are three today: the common law, REFJA (registration) and CCAA Part 3. RECJA has been repealed (FJ Part 1 slide 2; *Venetian Macau* [29])."},{"t":"**Common law recognition** (*Ong Han Nam* [48], citing *Humpuss*). The judgment must be:","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"(a) a final and conclusive judgment (on the merits),"},{"t":"(b) of a court with international jurisdiction under Singapore's private international law,"},{"t":"(c) with no defence available."}]}]},{"t":"**Reciprocity** (giving effect only to judgments of countries that would do the same for Singapore) as a requirement was left open in *Merck* [39] (C&Y 5.20).","b":["open"]},{"t":"**Burden of proof:** the party relying on the judgment proves the requirements. The **judgment debtor** proves any defence (C&Y 5.65; in *Sang Cheol Woo* [69], counsel accepted this for natural justice)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-0.1","LAW302-Q13-0.4"]},{"k":"step","t":"Was Country X's court entitled to judge Bob?"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"A club's referee can send off anyone playing on the club's pitch, and anyone who signed up to the club's rules. He cannot fine a passer-by who never came in. Country X's court is in the same position: Bob is bound if he was in X when the case was served on him, or if he accepted the court."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"This requirement, **international jurisdiction**, is measured by **Singapore's** private international law (its rules for cases with a foreign element), not by Country X's own rules. For a judgment *in personam* (one that binds the parties, such as an order that Bob pay Mei), the grounds fall into two families: Bob was **there**, or Bob **accepted** the court, by conduct (such as defending the case on its merits) or by agreement."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**International jurisdiction** at common law is judged by Singapore's rules, not by the foreign court's own rules:","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Presence or residence.** Natural persons: presence. Corporations: the \"fixed place of business\" test in *Adams v Cape*.","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"Whether residence without presence is enough is unsettled (C&Y 5.36–5.37).","b":["open"]}]}]},{"t":"**Submission by conduct** (*WSG Nimbus*; contrast *Henry v Geoprosco*)."},{"t":"**Submission by agreement or consent**, which may be implied (*Vizcaya v Picard*).","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"Whether Singapore will accept an implied agreement remains to be seen (C&Y 5.58).","b":["open"]}]}]},{"t":"**Are the categories closed?** *Schibsby v Westenholz* treats them as closed. Contrast Canada's \"real and substantial connection\" test in *Beals v Saldanha*."}]}]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-0.3"]},{"k":"step","t":"Enforcing means suing on a debt"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Mei's foreign judgment works like an IOU written abroad. Singapore's bailiffs will not act on a foreign IOU. Mei has to sue Bob on it here, and it is the Singapore judgment she obtains that the bailiffs then act on."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"At common law, the foreign judgment is treated as creating a debt that Bob owes under Singapore law. Mei sues on that debt in a **new action**, and it is the Singapore judgment she obtains that is enforced. Strictly, then, \"enforcing a foreign judgment\" is a misnomer (C&Y 5.12). Because only a debt can be sued on this way, the judgment must be a **fresh money judgment for a fixed sum**."},{"p":"The action is usually decided by **summary judgment**, a quick judgment without a full trial where the defendant has no real defence. This is where Week 13 comes in: Bob uses the defences to show he has one."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Common law enforcement adds two things:**","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"the judgment must be a fresh money judgment for a fixed sum that is not a tax, fine or penalty (*Poh Soon Kiat* [25]–[34]; slides 5–6);"},{"t":"enforcement is a **new action** on the implied debt the judgment creates, usually decided by summary judgment (*Poh Soon Kiat* [41]–[42])."}]}]},{"t":"On summary judgment, the creditor must show that it should be granted. The debtor obtains leave to defend by showing a fair or reasonable chance of a real or bona fide defence (C&Y 5.13)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-0.2"]},{"k":"step","t":"Recognition: the judgment as a shield"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"A judgment can be a shield as well as a sword. Suppose Bob sued Mei in Country X and lost, and now sues her again in Singapore on the same claim. Mei does not want money. She holds up the X judgment and says: \"This has already been decided.\""}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Mei simply pleads the X judgment in her defence. Because nobody is suing on a debt, neither the money-judgment requirement nor the six-year limitation period applies. A recognised judgment is **res judicata** (\"a matter already judged\") and works through **estoppels**, rules that stop a party reopening what has been decided: on the same claim (cause of action estoppel) or on the same point (issue estoppel). Separately, the *Henderson v Henderson* doctrine of **abuse of process** stops a party raising points that could and should have been raised before."},{"p":"The defences still apply. Limb (c) governs recognition as much as enforcement. *Ong Han Nam*, one of this week's fraud cases, was exactly that: a Malaysian judgment relied on as res judicata and met with an allegation of fraud (*Ong Han Nam* [48])."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Effects of recognition:** cause of action estoppel, issue estoppel (with the *Merck* requirements), and *Henderson v Henderson* abuse of process."},{"t":"A non-money judgment can be recognised, for example to found an estoppel (C&Y 5.09, 5.20). Recognition needs no special procedure (C&Y 5.10) and is not time-barred (C&Y 5.98)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-0.5"]},{"k":"take","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Three regimes: the common law, REFJA and CCAA Part 3. The RECJA has been repealed."},{"t":"Common law: final and conclusive on the merits, international jurisdiction by Singapore's rules, and no defence. Enforcement adds a fixed-sum money judgment, sued on as a debt."},{"t":"The creditor proves the requirements; the debtor proves the defences, which apply to recognition too."}]}]},{"k":"traps","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"The money-judgment requirement applies to *enforcement* only. A non-money judgment can still be *recognised*, for example to found an estoppel."},{"t":"International jurisdiction is judged by **Singapore's** rules, not by the foreign court's own rules."},{"t":"Lack of international jurisdiction is not a \"defence\" for Bob to prove. It is a requirement for the party relying on the judgment."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":true,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-0.6"]}],"ic":"refresh","nq":6}]},{"id":"w13.s1","n":"1","title":"Map of the defences","sub":"Locate every defence before learning the detail of each.","lessons":[{"id":"w13.s1","code":"Stage 1","kind":"stage","t":"Map of the defences","blocks":[{"k":"obj","t":"Locate every defence before learning the detail of each."},{"k":"big","t":"The enforcing court is **not an appeal court** for the foreign judgment. It will not re-examine the merits. The defences are narrow exceptions that protect three things: Singapore's procedural standards, Singapore's public policy, and Singapore's own judgments."},{"k":"step","t":"Not a second appeal"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Singapore's court is like a referee shown a result from another stadium. It does not replay the match. It only checks a short list of serious problems. Was the result rigged (fraud)? Was one side never told the game was on (natural justice)? Would accepting it break one of our own deepest rules (public policy)? Do we already have our own result for the same match (conflicting judgments)? If none of these apply, the result stands."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"The Singapore court does not review the **merits**: who was right on the facts and the law. If it did, every foreign judgment could be argued twice and litigation would never end. Two values sit behind this. **Comity** is the respect that courts of different countries owe one another's decisions; *Hong Pian Tee* warned against \"judicial chauvinism\", the assumption that the home court always knows better. **Finality** means a case fought and decided should stay decided (C&Y 5.66)."},{"p":"So every defence this week is a narrow exception. Each identifies something worse than a wrong answer: deception, a hearing Bob never fairly had, a result Singapore cannot accept on principle, or a clash with Singapore's own decisions."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**No review of the merits.** *Hong Pian Tee* [28], [31] (quoted in *Sang Cheol Woo* [94]); C&Y 5.66; for the CCAA, s 13(3)(a)."},{"t":"\"The enforcement forum is not an appellate tribunal vis-à-vis the foreign judgment\" (*Hong Pian Tee* [28]; C&Y 5.73)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-1.1"]},{"k":"step","t":"Nine headings, three things protected"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"The defences are the short list of objections a referee will actually listen to. \"The other referee made a bad call\" is not on the list. \"The other team bribed the referee\" is."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Most defences protect one of the three things in the big idea. Natural justice protects Singapore's **procedural standards**: Bob must have been told of the case and given a chance to argue it. Public policy protects Singapore's **public policy**. The rule against enforcing foreign penal, revenue and other public laws is a close relative: Singapore will not act as another State's enforcer (Module 2.7). The conflicting-judgments rule protects Singapore's **own judgments**. Fraud is special: the foreign court was misled rather than mistaken (Module 2.2). Satisfaction and limitation are practical: nothing is left to enforce, or Mei waited too long."},{"p":"Two headings are not established defences. Mistake is not a defence at all, subject to an open question about errors of Singapore law. Breach of an agreement to resolve disputes elsewhere is undecided at common law."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**The common law defences** (C&Y 5.65–5.102; slides 2–7):","c":[{"ol":[{"t":"Mistake: **not** a defence (but see the open question about errors of Singapore law).","b":["open"]},{"t":"Fraud: the distinction between extrinsic and intrinsic fraud."},{"t":"Public policy."},{"t":"Natural justice."},{"t":"Res judicata and conflicting judgments."},{"t":"Breach of an agreement to settle disputes elsewhere: **open** at common law.","b":["open"]},{"t":"Foreign penal, revenue and other public laws."},{"t":"Satisfaction (the judgment has been paid)."},{"t":"Limitation (affects enforcement only)."}]}]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-1.6"]},{"k":"step","t":"Developing in step with the statutes"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Singapore's common law and its two statutes are three rulebooks for the same game. The Court of Appeal wants the common law rulebook to stay broadly in line with the other two, especially the one that many countries signed up to."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"In *Merck*, the Court of Appeal said the common law defences should develop broadly consistently with REFJA and the CCAA, especially the CCAA. The CCAA enacts the Hague Convention on Choice of Court Agreements, so it reflects a transnational consensus. Notice the word \"broadly\": this is a direction of travel, not a wholesale copy. It is a leading argument wherever the common law is still open, such as proceedings brought in breach of a jurisdiction agreement (Module 2.6)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Convergence.** *Merck* [37]: common law defences should develop \"broadly\" consistently with the CCAA and REFJA, especially the CCAA, which reflects a transnational consensus."},{"t":"The common law \"should … generally be developed in a manner that is compatible and consistent with legislation which covers a broadly similar area\" (*Merck* [37], quoted in C&Y 5.65)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-1.2"]},{"k":"step","t":"Three doors, and two tools that are not defences"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"There are three doors into the same room, and each door has its own checklist. Which door Mei uses decides which complaints Bob can raise."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Before asking \"which defence?\", ask \"which regime?\". The CCAA comes first: it covers judgments of the **chosen court** of a Contracting State under an exclusive choice of court agreement (an agreement that only that court will hear the dispute). REFJA comes next, for judgments from countries gazetted under the Act. Everything else goes to the common law. The lists differ. Under REFJA, Bob applies to set aside the registration on the grounds in s 5. Under the CCAA, the grounds are an **exhaustive** list, such as recognition being \"manifestly\" incompatible with Singapore public policy (s 14(c)). Only at common law does Stage 2's list apply in full."},{"p":"Two tools are easily mistaken for defences. **Severance** lets the court cut out an objectionable part of a judgment, such as a penalty, and give effect to the rest. An **anti-enforcement injunction** (AEI) is an order addressed to Mei personally, forbidding her from enforcing the judgment abroad as well, in named countries or even worldwide. A defence stops the judgment working only in Singapore; an AEI reaches much further, which is why it is kept for exceptional cases (Stage 3)."}]},{"k":"flow","title":"Analysis flowchart","g":{"root":"A","nodes":{"A":{"t":"Foreign judgment relied on in Singapore","q":false},"B":{"t":"Which regime applies?","q":true},"C":{"t":"CCAA Part 3","q":false},"D":{"t":"REFJA registration","q":false},"E":{"t":"Common law","q":false},"C1":{"t":"s 14 mandatory or s 15/16 discretionary ground?","q":true},"X":{"t":"Refused or set aside","q":false},"C2":{"t":"Recognised/enforced as if a General Division judgment","q":false},"D1":{"t":"s 5 set-aside ground?","q":true},"D2":{"t":"Registered judgment enforced","q":false},"E1":{"t":"Requirements met? Final and conclusive, on the merits, international jurisdiction","q":true},"E2":{"t":"Any defence? Fraud, public policy, natural justice, conflicting judgment, penal law","q":true},"E3":{"t":"Is enforcement (not just recognition) sought?","q":true},"Y":{"t":"Recognised: estoppel effect","q":false},"E4":{"t":"Fixed sum, not a tax or penalty, sued within 6 years?","q":true},"Z":{"t":"Enforced through a fresh action (summary judgment)","q":false}},"edges":[["A","B",""],["B","C","Judgment of the chosen court of a Contracting State under an exclusive choice of court agreement"],["B","D","Judgment of a superior court of a REFJA country (not within CCAA)"],["B","E","Everything else"],["C","C1",""],["C1","X","Yes"],["C1","C2","No"],["D","D1",""],["D1","X","Yes"],["D1","D2","No"],["E","E1",""],["E1","X","No"],["E1","E2","Yes"],["E2","X","Yes"],["E2","E3","No"],["E3","Y","No"],["E3","E4","Yes"],["E4","Z","Yes"],["E4","X","No"]]}},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Related tools:**","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Severance** lets the court enforce the unobjectionable part of a judgment."},{"t":"An **anti-enforcement injunction** is a remedy, not a defence. It restrains the creditor from enforcing abroad, in named countries or worldwide (C&Y 5.207)."}]}]},{"t":"**The CCAA's grounds** for refusal (ss 14–16) are exhaustive (C&Y 5.186)."},{"t":"**Relationship between the regimes.** REFJA bars a common law enforcement action for judgments within its scope, and the CCAA prevails over REFJA. But the CCAA does **not** bar the common law route, unless pursuing it would be an abuse of process (C&Y 5.173, 5.200–5.201)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-1.7"]},{"k":"take","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"The Singapore court does not re-examine the merits; the defences are narrow exceptions."},{"t":"Nine common law headings, of which mistake and breach of a dispute-resolution agreement are not established defences."},{"t":"*Merck* [37]: the common law should develop broadly in line with REFJA and especially the CCAA."},{"t":"Identify the regime first. Severance and AEIs are tools, not defences."}]}]},{"k":"traps","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"An AEI is not a defence. It is an injunction against the creditor personally (Stage 3)."},{"t":"Under the CCAA, the grounds for refusal are an **exhaustive** list (C&Y 5.186). You cannot add common law defences to it."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":true,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-1.8"]}],"ic":"map","nq":5}]},{"id":"w13.s2","n":"2","title":"Common law defences","sub":"2.1 Mistake and errors of Singapore law · 2.2 Fraud · 2.3 Public policy · 2.4 Natural justice · 2.5 Res judicata and conflicting judgments · 2.6 Proceedings brought in breach of a dispute-resolution agreement · 2.7 Foreign penal, revenue and other public laws; severance · 2.8 Satisfaction and limitation","lessons":[{"id":"w13.m2.1","code":"2.1","kind":"module","t":"Mistake and errors of Singapore law","blocks":[{"k":"obj","t":"Explain why a foreign court's mistake is not a defence, and state accurately what *Merck* left open."},{"k":"big","t":"Finality matters more than correctness. Two competent courts can reach different answers, and the enforcing court does not decide which one is \"right\"."},{"k":"step","t":"Being wrong is not a defence"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"If a teacher at another school marks a right answer as wrong, your school will not re-mark the test. Otherwise every test could be re-marked forever. Being wrong is not enough."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Suppose the Country X court misread a contract, believed the wrong witness or misunderstood its own law. Bob may well be right that the judgment is wrong. In Singapore, that does not matter: a mistake of fact or law by the foreign court is irrelevant (*Godard v Gray*; *Ralli v Anguilla*)."},{"p":"The reason is the rule from Stage 1: the Singapore court does not review the merits. If the loser could reopen every foreign judgment by arguing that it was wrong, one of the main aims of recognising foreign judgments, the **finality** of litigation, would be lost (C&Y 5.66). Correcting the foreign court's errors is a job for Country X's own appeal courts, not for Singapore."},{"p":"The traditional rule went further than you might expect. It applied even where the mistake concerned the law of the **enforcing** court itself: for an English court asked to enforce a foreign judgment, a mistake about English law (C&Y 5.66). That is the part of the rule *Merck* has unsettled, at least for Singapore law."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**The general rule.** A mistake of fact or law by the foreign court is irrelevant (*Godard v Gray*; *Ralli v Anguilla*). Traditionally this held even where the mistake concerned the law of the enforcing court (C&Y 5.66; slide 2)."},{"t":"**Why:** reviewing the merits would defeat the finality of litigation, one of the underlying objectives of recognising and enforcing foreign judgments (C&Y 5.66)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-1.9","LAW302-Q13-1.3"]},{"k":"step","t":"*Merck*: a door left ajar for errors of Singapore law"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Now the open question: what if the other teacher got *our* rulebook badly wrong? Our school is the keeper of its own rulebook, and letting a misreading stand could quietly change the rules. But re-marking other schools' tests is exactly what we said we would not do. The Court of Appeal saw both sides and did not choose."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"*Merck* was an issue estoppel case: did a foreign court's decision on a particular point bind the parties in Singapore? While mapping the \"outer limits\" of that doctrine, the Court of Appeal made two sets of remarks that matter here."},{"p":"First, at [58], it accepted that issue estoppel may be denied where giving effect to the foreign judgment would conflict with Singapore public policy. It said this could \"conceivably\" extend to judgments that are perverse, or that contain a sufficiently serious and material error, but it left that open."},{"p":"Second, at [59]–[61], it asked whether a judgment resting on a wrong application of **Singapore** law deserves special treatment. Two values pull against each other. Comity says Singapore should not second-guess a foreign court. But the Singapore court is also the \"guardian or custodian\" of Singapore law, which suggests it should not condone patent errors about that law (C&Y 5.67). The court rejected both easy answers, that a foreign ruling on Singapore law can **never** found an estoppel and that it **always** can, and left the question for a future case."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"***Merck* [58]: public policy and issue estoppel.** Where a foreign judgment conflicts with Singapore public policy, issue estoppel may be denied. The court said this could \"conceivably\" extend to judgments that are perverse or contain a sufficiently serious and material error, but left that open.","b":["open"]},{"t":"***Merck* [59]–[61]: errors of Singapore law.** The court left open whether a judgment resting on an erroneous application of Singapore law deserves special treatment, whether framed as public policy or as a standalone limit on issue estoppel.","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"It rejected both extremes: (i) that a foreign ruling on Singapore law can **never** found an estoppel, and (ii) that it **always** can."},{"t":"It described the balance as comity on one side, and the Singapore court's role as \"guardian or custodian\" of Singapore law on the other."}]}],"b":["open"]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-1.10"]},{"k":"step","t":"Existent and retrospective errors"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"There are two kinds of wrong answer. In one, the other teacher ignored a rule printed clearly in our rulebook. In the other, our rulebook said nothing on the point, the teacher made a careful and sensible guess, and only later did we add a page saying something different. The first looks like carelessness. The second is bad luck."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"*Merck* [61] floated a way of separating the cases that might matter from those that should not. An **existent error** is one where the foreign court clearly erred in applying Singapore law as it stood at the time. A **retrospective error** arises where a point of Singapore law was uncertain, with no clear Singapore decision, and the foreign court took a considered position (for example, after expert evidence on Singapore law) that a later Singapore decision contradicted. The foreign court was not wrong about the law as it then stood; it simply anticipated wrongly how that law would develop."},{"p":"The court's language suggests that, if errors of Singapore law ever matter, existent errors are the candidates and retrospective errors should be left alone. But it did not decide even that. The test is about timing: ask what Singapore law was **when the foreign court decided**, not what it is today."}]},{"k":"call","c":"example","n":[{"p":"Mei relies on a Country X judgment to estop Bob on a point of Singapore contract law. When the X court decided, the point was unsettled; it weighed expert evidence and took a reasonable view. Two years later, Singapore's Court of Appeal goes the other way. That is a retrospective error, the kind least likely to stop the estoppel."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"*Merck* [61] floated a distinction between two kinds of error:","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Existent errors:** the foreign court clearly erred on Singapore law as it stood at the time."},{"t":"**Retrospective errors:** the foreign court took a defensible view of an unsettled point, which a later Singapore decision then contradicted."}]}]},{"t":"The court did not decide whether either kind justifies refusing recognition or an estoppel.","b":["open"]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-1.4","LAW302-Q13-1.5"]},{"k":"step","t":"What Chong & Yip suggest, and the *Arnold* question"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Even if our school decides that misreadings of its own rulebook do matter, it should step in only for howlers, not close calls. Otherwise it would be back to re-marking everyone's tests."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Chong & Yip think a ground of refusal for errors of Singapore law could be justified: enforcing such a judgment could upset the coherence of Singapore law and sit uneasily with the court's role as its guardian. But they would limit it to **patent** errors, meaning obvious ones, not merely arguable ones. The public policy threshold should stay high, and a narrow ground avoids the impression of the Singapore court acting as an appeal court over the foreign one (C&Y 5.84). That is an academic view, though it matches the court's apparent preference."},{"p":"The slides add a related question: is there an \"*Arnold* exception\" for foreign judgments? Domestically, that exception lets an issue estoppel be reopened in special circumstances. Whether and how it applies to foreign judgments was left open in *Merck*, as you saw in Week 12."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Academic view (C&Y 5.84):** if this ground is ever accepted, it should be limited to **patent** errors, to keep the public policy threshold high.","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"The ground could be justified because enforcing such a judgment would upset the coherence of Singapore law and conflict with the court's role as guardian of Singapore law.","b":["acad"]},{"t":"Limiting it to patent errors avoids the impression of the court acting as an appellate court vis-à-vis the foreign court (C&Y 5.84)."}]}],"b":["acad"]},{"t":"**Slide 2 prompt (\"Arnold exception?\"):** a discussion point on whether something like the domestic special-circumstances exception to issue estoppel could apply to foreign judgments. *Merck* left it open (*Merck* [62]–[65]; C&Y 5.123).","b":["open"]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-1.12"]},{"k":"take","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"A foreign court's mistake of fact or law is not a defence (*Godard v Gray*; *Ralli v Anguilla*). The policy is finality."},{"t":"*Merck* [58]–[61] left open whether an error of **Singapore** law can defeat recognition or an issue estoppel, through public policy or as a standalone limit."},{"t":"It rejected both extremes and floated a distinction between existent and retrospective errors."},{"t":"Chong & Yip suggest that any such ground be limited to patent errors."}]}]},{"k":"traps","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Do not write \"mistake is a defence\"."},{"t":"Do not write that *Merck* **decided** that an error of Singapore law defeats recognition. It left the point open."},{"t":"Do not call an error \"existent\" just because a later Singapore decision shows the foreign court was wrong. Ask what Singapore law was when the foreign court decided."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":true,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-1.11"]}],"ic":"x","nq":7},{"id":"w13.m2.2","code":"2.2","kind":"module","t":"Fraud","blocks":[{"k":"obj","t":"Classify alleged fraud as extrinsic or intrinsic, apply the correct test to each, and recognise when fraud cannot be raised at all."},{"k":"big","t":"Fraud is an exception to the rule against reviewing the merits because the foreign court was **misled, not mistaken** (*Abouloff v Oppenheimer*, in C&Y 5.68). Singapore limits the exception for fraud that goes to the merits, out of comity."},{"k":"step","t":"Misled, not mistaken"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Module 2.1 said Singapore will not re-mark another school's test. So why listen to \"they cheated\"? If a student smuggled in the answers, the teacher marked honestly but was fooled. Looking into that is not re-marking; it asks a question the teacher never had the chance to answer."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"A fraud defence looks awkward next to the rule against reviewing the merits: to decide whether Mei deceived the Country X court, Singapore must look at evidence. The English Court of Appeal resolved this in *Abouloff v Oppenheimer*. Where the foreign decision was caused by fraud, the foreign court was **misled, not mistaken**, so the enforcing court is not reviewing anything the foreign court actually decided (C&Y 5.68)."},{"p":"English law takes that reasoning a long way: Bob needs no new evidence, and it does not matter that the foreign court rejected the allegation or that he never raised it there. Courts have voiced doubts, and the Privy Council has hinted at a more nuanced approach, but *Takhar*, a later UK Supreme Court decision on domestic judgments, may suggest that the rule is becoming further entrenched in English law (C&Y 5.68)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**The English *Abouloff* rule** (C&Y 5.68). Fraud can be raised at the enforcement stage:","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"without new evidence;"},{"t":"even if fraud was alleged before, and rejected by, the foreign court;"},{"t":"even if the debtor could have raised it abroad but did not."}]}]},{"t":"The Privy Council: \"a nuanced approach might be required, depending on the reliability of the foreign legal system, the scope for challenge in the foreign court and the type of fraud alleged\" (*Altimo Holdings v Kyrgyz Mobil Tel* [116]; C&Y 5.68)."},{"t":"*Takhar v Gracefield Developments* (UKSC): a party setting aside a **domestic** judgment for fraud generally need not show that the fraud could not have been uncovered with reasonable diligence. This may further entrench *Abouloff* in English law (C&Y 5.68)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-2.1"]},{"k":"step","t":"Singapore's line: *Hong Pian Tee*"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Singapore splits cheating into two kinds. Cheating *outside* the game (hiding the match invitation, bribing the referee) can simply be complained about. Cheating *inside* the game, a lie the other side argued against and the referee ruled on, will be re-examined only on brand-new proof that would probably have changed the result. England is more generous to the complaining side."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"In *Hong Pian Tee* (2002), the Court of Appeal rejected the generous English approach and adopted the Canadian and Australian distinction between **extrinsic fraud** (outside the trial) and **intrinsic fraud** (within the trial, going to the merits). Its reasons were comity and finality: the enforcing court is not an appeal court, and *Abouloff* would invite endless litigation."},{"p":"The result is a compromise: Singapore keeps the *Abouloff* approach for extrinsic fraud but adds a fresh-evidence hurdle for intrinsic fraud. English courts draw no such distinction."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Singapore: *Hong Pian Tee* (CA, 2002)** adopted the Canadian and Australian distinction instead (C&Y 5.69–5.71)."},{"t":"**Reasons:** *Abouloff* would encourage \"endless litigation\" (*Hong Pian Tee* [27]) and \"judicial chauvinism\"; \"the enforcement forum is not an appellate tribunal vis-à-vis the foreign judgment\" ([28]). \"No one court should sit in judgment over the final decision of a competent court of another jurisdiction\" ([31]) (C&Y 5.73–5.74)."},{"t":"The Court of Appeal has since confirmed the *Hong Pian Tee* approach for foreign judgments (*Ong Han Nam*; C&Y 5.74)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-2.8"]},{"k":"step","t":"Extrinsic fraud: outside the trial"},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Extrinsic fraud happens **outside the trial**, or goes to the foreign court's jurisdiction. The common thread is that Bob was deprived of a fair contest, or the process was corrupted from outside, sometimes by the court itself (which overlaps with natural justice, Module 2.4)."},{"p":"Because Bob never had a fair chance to meet this kind of fraud in Country X, Singapore keeps the *Abouloff* approach: he can simply plead it, without fresh evidence."}]},{"k":"call","c":"example","n":[{"p":"Mei secretly pays Bob's own lawyer to abandon his defence, and the X court enters judgment against Bob. Bob never had a real contest. That is extrinsic fraud, and he can raise it in Singapore without any fresh-evidence hurdle."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Extrinsic fraud** happens outside the trial, or goes to the court's jurisdiction (C&Y 5.69–5.70). Examples:","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"the defendant was never served;"},{"t":"the defendant was fraudulently persuaded to let judgment go by default (*Hong Pian Tee* [21]);"},{"t":"bribery of a solicitor, counsel or witness; collusion (*Ong Han Nam* [49]);"},{"t":"perjury during discovery, which the defendant had no chance to test (*Ong Han Nam* [49]; *Ee Hoong Liang* [16]);"},{"t":"fraud by the court itself (C&Y 5.69). This overlaps with natural justice: slide 5 links bias of the foreign court with fraud by the court."}]}]},{"t":"**The *Abouloff* approach still applies**, so extrinsic fraud can simply be pleaded (*Ong Han Nam* [49]; C&Y 5.71)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-2.3"]},{"k":"step","t":"Intrinsic fraud: the fresh-evidence test"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"If Bob already had his chance to call Mei's witness a liar in Country X, and the judge heard both sides and decided, Singapore will not run the argument again. It will listen only if Bob turns up something genuinely new: evidence he could not have found with reasonable effort, and strong enough that it would probably have changed the result."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"In legal terms, *Hong Pian Tee* [30] allows a challenge for intrinsic fraud only on fresh evidence that meets two **cumulative** requirements. The first is about diligence: could Bob have found the evidence in time? The second is about materiality: would it have mattered? Materiality means the fraud must be shown to have **produced** the judgment (C&Y 5.71)."},{"p":"The test applies **even to default judgments**, where Bob took no part abroad and so never raised fraud there. Simply failing to defend bars a fraud argument at the enforcement stage, unless there is new evidence that reasonable diligence could not have found at the time (C&Y 5.72). Bob had his chance to contest the claim and chose not to take it."}]},{"k":"call","c":"example","n":[{"p":"After a full trial in Country X, at which Bob argued that Mei's key witness was lying, the judge believed the witness. In Singapore, Bob repeats the allegation on the same evidence. That is intrinsic fraud with no fresh evidence, so it fails."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Intrinsic fraud** happens within the trial and goes to the merits. Example: false statements at trial, \"met by counter-statements\", that the other side contested and the court adjudicated on (*Hong Pian Tee* [20]; *Ong Han Nam* [49])."},{"t":"It can be raised **only if** there is fresh evidence that (i) reasonable diligence would not have uncovered **and** (ii) would likely have made a difference to the result (*Hong Pian Tee* [30]; *Ong Han Nam* [49], [55]). The two requirements are cumulative (C&Y 5.71)."},{"t":"The intrinsic-fraud test applies **even to default judgments** (C&Y 5.72)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-2.2"]},{"k":"step","t":"Where the line falls: a chance to contest"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"The line is not \"was there a full trial?\" but \"did Bob get a fair chance to argue the point?\". A written test with a box for Bob's answer is still a contest, even if Bob leaves the box empty."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"*Ee Hoong Liang* shows how the line is drawn. A US court entered judgment against Ee on a summary judgment motion. He asked for more time to respond but never filed anything. Resisting enforcement in Singapore, he said the creditors had hidden settlement payouts that should have reduced the award, and that this was extrinsic fraud because there had been no trial."},{"p":"The Appellate Division disagreed. Domestic cases on setting aside judgments for fraud did not help him, because comity applies to foreign judgments. And the deciding question was whether he had an **opportunity to contest** the point. He could have contested the motion, so the alleged non-disclosure, which went to quantum (the amount awarded), was intrinsic fraud, and he had to meet the reasonable-diligence requirement."},{"p":"Contrast *Boxsentry*, which the court distinguished. There the non-disclosure was made in an uncontested default-judgment application itself, not in a claim the debtor could have defended, so it was extrinsic (*Ee Hoong Liang* [17]). The question is whether the debtor could contest the point the fraud concerns, not whether there was a trial."},{"p":"Singapore's line has its critics: it can be hard to draw, and it has been said to favour the fraudster over his careless opponent (C&Y 5.73)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"***Ee Hoong Liang* [10]–[19]:**","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"Domestic setting-aside cases (*Façade*; *Takhar*) are irrelevant to foreign judgments, because comity and \"not an appellate tribunal\" considerations apply ([11]–[13])."},{"t":"The deciding question is whether the debtor had an **opportunity to contest** the point. A summary judgment motion that the debtor could have contested counts as a contest. So hidden settlement payouts that went to quantum were **intrinsic** fraud ([16]–[18]), and the reasonable-diligence requirement applied ([19])."},{"t":"Contrast *Boxsentry*: non-disclosure in an **uncontested default-judgment application** was treated as extrinsic fraud ([17])."}]}]},{"t":"**Critique** (C&Y 5.73):","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"*Hong Pian Tee* \"takes the side of the fraudster against his negligent opponent\" (Briggs).","b":["acad"]},{"t":"The line between extrinsic and intrinsic fraud is hard to draw."},{"t":"The Supreme Court of Canada has advocated discontinuing the distinction."}]}],"b":["acad"]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-2.4"]},{"k":"step","t":"The fraud must have produced the judgment"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Suppose the winning team did cheat, by faking one throw-in, but won 10–0 with goals that had nothing to do with it. The cheating was real, but it did not produce the result."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Whatever its type, the fraud must have **caused** the judgment. That holds even for extrinsic fraud, where no fresh evidence is needed."},{"p":"*Ong Han Nam* is the leading illustration. The Singapore High Court refused to recognise a Malaysian judgment after listing facts that the winning party had not disclosed to the Malaysian court. The Court of Appeal reversed. The Malaysian court had appreciated the key facts, so the alleged fraud had not produced its judgment. The Singapore judge had simply taken a different view of those facts, which is \"a difference of opinion\", not fraud."},{"p":"There is also a threshold for getting started. Bob cannot just assert fraud; he needs plausible evidence disclosing a **prima facie** case, meaning enough, on its face, to call for an answer."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Causation.** The fraud must have **produced** the judgment (*Ong Han Nam* [50]; slide 3). A Singapore judge simply taking a different view of the evidence is not fraud (*Ong Han Nam* [51]). On intrinsic fraud, the evidence in *Ong Han Nam* was discoverable and immaterial ([55])."},{"t":"**Threshold.** There must be plausible evidence disclosing a prima facie case of fraud (*Owens Bank v Bracco*; slide 3). The judgment debtor bears the burden of proving the defence (C&Y 5.65)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-2.14","LAW302-Q13-2.5"]},{"k":"step","t":"When fraud cannot be raised at all"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"For cheating outside the game, Bob may normally repeat his complaint in Singapore even if the foreign referee brushed it aside during the match. But suppose that after the match he took a separate complaint about the cheating to the home league's own tribunal, had a hearing and lost. Then he has had his day on that very question, and he cannot bring the same complaint again in Singapore."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Singapore will not entertain an allegation of fraud at all where pleading it is an **abuse of process** (a misuse of the court's procedure), or where an **issue estoppel on fraud** has arisen."},{"p":"The second comes from *House of Spring Gardens v Waite*. An Irish court gave judgment against three defendants. Two of them brought a separate action in Ireland to set it aside for the plaintiffs' fraud, and lost. When the plaintiffs sought to enforce in England, the defendants raised the fraud again. *Abouloff* seemed to allow that, but the English Court of Appeal distinguished it: in none of the earlier cases had fraud been litigated in a **separate, second action** in the court of origin. All three defendants were estopped, the third as a privy (someone so closely connected with a party that he is bound too) (C&Y 5.75)."},{"p":"In Singapore, such an estoppel would realistically arise only for **extrinsic** fraud, because intrinsic fraud already needs fresh evidence before it can be raised at all (C&Y 5.75)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**When fraud cannot be raised at all** (C&Y 5.75):","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Abuse of process.**"},{"t":"**Issue estoppel on fraud** (*House of Spring Gardens v Waite*). The fraud had already been litigated, and rejected, in a **separate second action** in the court of origin. The third defendant was bound as a privy. In Singapore this will realistically arise only for extrinsic fraud (C&Y 5.75)."}]}]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-2.6"]},{"k":"step","t":"Fraud under REFJA and the CCAA"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"The two statutes have their own checklists. REFJA's just says \"fraud\", leaving it unclear whether Singapore's two-kinds-of-cheating line applies. The CCAA's mandatory fraud ground covers only cheating with the process itself, roughly the outside-the-game kind."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Under REFJA, registration **must** be set aside if the judgment was obtained by fraud, but because the Act says only \"fraud\", it is unclear whether the common law split applies. Chong & Yip note a twist: *Merck* described the common law taking its cue from the statutes, so reading REFJA in line with *Hong Pian Tee* would run the other way, but would still serve the aim of convergence."},{"p":"Under the CCAA, the mandatory fraud ground covers only fraud \"in connection with a matter of procedure\": deliberately serving at the wrong address, giving the defendant false hearing details, bribing a judge, juror or witness, or deliberately concealing key evidence. That roughly equals extrinsic fraud. Fraud on the substance (intrinsic fraud, in common law terms) could potentially be argued under s 14(c), public policy, where recognition must be \"manifestly\" incompatible with it, a deliberately high bar (C&Y 5.191)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Statutory regimes:**","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"**REFJA s 5(1)(a)(iv)** says only \"obtained by fraud\". It is unclear whether the extrinsic/intrinsic distinction applies (C&Y 5.168).","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"Adopting the common law approach under REFJA would reverse the direction described in *Merck* [37], but would be in line with its desire for convergence (C&Y 5.168).","b":["acad"]}]}],"b":["open"]},{"t":"**CCAA s 14(b)** covers only \"fraud in connection with a matter of procedure\", which is roughly extrinsic fraud (C&Y 5.190). Substantive fraud might be argued under s 14(c), public policy (C&Y 5.190–5.191)."}]}]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-2.13"]},{"k":"take","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Fraud is allowed despite the no-review rule because the foreign court was misled, not mistaken."},{"t":"Extrinsic fraud can simply be pleaded. Intrinsic fraud needs fresh, undiscoverable and material evidence (*Hong Pian Tee* [30]), even against a default judgment."},{"t":"The dividing line is the opportunity to contest (*Ee Hoong Liang*), and any fraud must have produced the judgment (*Ong Han Nam* [50])."},{"t":"Abuse of process and issue estoppel (*House of Spring Gardens*) can bar fraud altogether."}]}]},{"k":"traps","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Do not apply *Abouloff* to intrinsic fraud in Singapore."},{"t":"Do not treat \"there was no full trial\" as automatically meaning extrinsic fraud. The question is whether there was an opportunity to contest (*Ee Hoong Liang*)."},{"t":"Do not drop causation for extrinsic fraud. No fresh evidence is needed, but the fraud must still have produced the judgment."},{"t":"Do not say CCAA s 14(b) covers all fraud. It covers procedural fraud only."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":true,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-2.7","LAW302-Q13-2.9","LAW302-Q13-2.10","LAW302-Q13-2.11","LAW302-Q13-2.12"]}],"ic":"flame","nq":14},{"id":"w13.m2.3","code":"2.3","kind":"module","t":"Public policy","blocks":[{"k":"obj","t":"Apply the higher threshold of public policy, identify what must offend it, and trace the gambling cases in order from *Star City* to *Venetian Macau* (2026)."},{"k":"big","t":"Only Singapore's most fundamental public policy can block a foreign judgment: statutory policy, which *Poh Soon Kiat* puts at the top, or the common law's **\"higher\" (international)** policy, never mere domestic policy. At common law and under the REFJA, what must offend is the **effect of enforcing the judgment** here, not the underlying claim as such."},{"k":"step","t":"A narrow door: the higher threshold"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Every household has everyday rules (shoes off at the door) and a few deep ones (no violence, ever). A guest who keeps their shoes on is just different, and nobody throws them out. A guest who breaks a deep rule is shown the door. Singapore treats foreign judgments the same way: different is fine, deeply offensive is not."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"**Public policy** is the set of values a legal system treats as fundamental. As a defence, it lets Singapore refuse to give effect to a foreign judgment that would offend those values. But almost every foreign judgment applies some rule that Singapore law does not share. If \"different from our law\" were enough, the defence would swallow the rule against reviewing the merits, and **comity** (the respect courts of different countries show each other's decisions) would mean little."},{"p":"So the bar is high: a narrower, \"higher\" standard applies than in a domestic case, measured by Singapore's values, not the foreign country's. The indicators below describe something that shocks, not something that merely surprises, and in practice the defence rarely succeeds."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"A **narrower, \"higher\" standard** of public policy applies than in a domestic case (C&Y 5.80)."},{"t":"The relevant public policy is that of the **enforcing forum**. A difference between foreign and Singapore public policy is not itself a breach (C&Y 5.82)."},{"t":"**Indicators that public policy is engaged** (*Poh Soon Kiat* [89], [111], applied in *Sang Cheol Woo* [97]). Enforcement would:","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"offend a fundamental principle of justice or a deep-rooted tradition;"},{"t":"offend the general community;"},{"t":"offend fundamental ideas of morality, decency, human liberty or justice; or"},{"t":"go against a rule whose purpose is to protect the public interest."}]}]},{"t":"**CCAA s 14(c)** requires that recognition be **manifestly** incompatible with Singapore public policy. \"Manifestly\" signals a high threshold, matching the common law, where the defence rarely succeeds (C&Y 5.191)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-3.1","LAW302-Q13-3.9"]},{"k":"step","t":"Where public policy comes from: the *Poh Soon Kiat* hierarchy"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Picture a stack of rulebooks. Parliament's written rules sit on top, because a statute overrides judge-made law. The judge-made book has two chapters: a short one of deep values that hold even across borders (\"higher\" or international policy), and a long one of everyday preferences that apply only at home (domestic policy)."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Public policy can come from **statute** or from the **common law**, meaning judge-made law. *Poh Soon Kiat* ranked the sources: statute first, because statute takes precedence over the common law; then common law policy, split into \"higher\" international and domestic policy, of which only the higher kind counts against a foreign judgment. *Venetian Macau* [54] applied this hierarchy to the gambling statute you will meet below."},{"p":"Chong & Yip accept the split within the common law, but find statute's place at the top potentially misleading, since a statute can express a merely domestic policy too (C&Y 5.81)."}]},{"k":"call","c":"why","n":[{"p":"The hierarchy decides the gambling cases. If statutory policy automatically outranks international policy, a Singapore statute barring the collection of gambling debts can block a foreign casino's judgment. On Chong & Yip's approach, you would first ask whether that statute's policy is of the \"higher\" kind and meant to reach the situation."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**The *Poh Soon Kiat* hierarchy** (C&Y 5.80; *Poh Soon Kiat* [112]–[113]):","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"common law public policy divides into \"higher\" international policy and domestic policy;"},{"t":"statutory public policy **prevails** over \"higher\" international policy (*Poh Soon Kiat* [113], said obiter, that is, in passing; applied in *Venetian Macau* [54]).","b":["obiter"]}]}]},{"t":"**C&Y critique** (5.81): putting statute at the top is misleading. Only statutory policy of an equivalent \"higher\" level, and intended to reach the situation, should count. Whether a statute is meant to apply to foreign situations is a matter of statutory interpretation.","b":["acad"]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-3.3"]},{"k":"step","t":"What must offend: the judgment's effect, not the claim"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Singapore usually asks: \"What would *we* be doing if we made Bob pay on this judgment?\" It does not usually ask: \"Would we have allowed Mei's claim if she had started it here?\" The next steps show where the second question creeps back in: egregious claims and gambling debts."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"At common law, the question is whether the **effect of enforcing the foreign judgment** would offend Singapore's public policy. This keeps the enquiry on Singapore's own act of giving the judgment force, and stops it turning into a retrial of the claim."},{"p":"The now-repealed RECJA (the old scheme for registering Commonwealth judgments) asked a different question. Its public policy ground looked at the **cause of action**, meaning the legal claim the foreign court decided. The REFJA follows the common law instead: its ground is about **enforcement of the judgment**. Keep this contrast in mind. It is the key to *Venetian Macau*."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Common law:** the **effect of enforcing the judgment** must offend (C&Y 5.83; *Merck* [58], quoting the Hague report)."},{"t":"**Former RECJA s 3(2)(f):** the test looked at the **cause of action** instead (*Venetian Macau* [29]; C&Y 5.83)."},{"t":"**REFJA s 5(1)(a)(v):** registration shall be set aside if **enforcement of the judgment** would be contrary to Singapore public policy. In line with the common law, and in contrast with the RECJA, the defence operates against the foreign judgment (C&Y 5.169).","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"The reference ought to be to Singapore's **international** public policy, as at common law (C&Y 5.169).","b":["acad"]}]}]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-3.10"]},{"k":"step","t":"When the claim infects the judgment"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Usually Singapore looks at the judgment, not the deal behind it. But some deals are so rotten that the smell carries into any judgment built on them. A contract signed at gunpoint is the classic example."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"The \"effect, not cause\" rule has a limit. A cause of action can be so egregious that it **infects** the judgment based on it. Chong & Yip's example is a contract concluded under **extreme physical duress** (citing *Royal Boskalis*). The English Court of Appeal has also said it would go behind a foreign judgment enforcing a **drug trafficking** contract (*ED & F Man v Yani Haryanto (No 2)*) (C&Y 5.83). Enforcing such a judgment would make Singapore's courts an instrument of the wrong."},{"p":"*Israel Discount Bank v Hadjipateras* went further. It suggested that a judgment based on an agreement obtained by **undue influence**, an equitable doctrine, may be refused. Chong & Yip's two criticisms, below, come straight from the two ideas you have just learned: what must offend, and at what level."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"A **particularly egregious cause of action** can \"infect\" the judgment, e.g. an action on a contract concluded under extreme physical duress (C&Y 5.83)."},{"t":"***Israel Discount Bank*** (slide 4, quoting p 143): a judgment based on an agreement obtained by undue influence may be refused as contrary to \"the distinctive public policy of this country\".","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"**C&Y critique** (5.83): normally it is the judgment, not the cause of action, that must offend; and undue influence may engage only domestic public policy.","b":["acad"]}]}]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-3.2","LAW302-Q13-3.7"]},{"k":"step","t":"Other examples, and how estoppel fits in"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"If Singapore's court has told Mei not to sue Bob abroad and she does it anyway, Singapore will not reward her by enforcing her win. And Bob cannot keep a point up his sleeve abroad, then pull it out for the first time in Singapore."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"An **anti-suit injunction** (ASI) is a Singapore court order forbidding a party to bring or continue proceedings abroad. A foreign judgment obtained in breach of one is an established example of a judgment contrary to public policy: enforcing it would reward defiance of the Singapore court."},{"p":"Public policy also meets **estoppel**, a rule that stops a party reopening a matter already decided. A foreign court's finding on **its own** public policy creates no estoppel on Singapore's, because public policy is unique to each State. But a fact that **should have been** raised abroad cannot be held back and raised for the first time here: the defence is not a second chance for a debtor who stayed silent."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Breach of a Singapore ASI:** an established example (C&Y 5.85)."},{"t":"**An error of Singapore law:** possibly; *Merck* [59]–[61] left the point open (Module 2.1).","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"If it is accepted, it should be limited to **patent** errors (C&Y 5.84).","b":["acad"]}]}],"b":["open"]},{"t":"**Estoppel and public policy** (C&Y 5.82):","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"A foreign court's finding about **its own** public policy creates no estoppel on Singapore public policy (compare *India v DT* [86], quoted in *Gonzalo Gil White* [90], from Week 12)."},{"t":"But a fact that **should have been raised** abroad, and was not, cannot be raised at the enforcement stage."}]}]},{"t":"**Not contrary to public policy:** a 20% Korean statutory interest rate payable to the private creditor and aimed at deterring delay (*Sang Cheol Woo* [99]–[101])."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-3.8"]},{"k":"step","t":"The gambling cases (1): *Star City* and *Burswood*"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Singapore's courts will not help anyone collect a gambling win. The puzzle is what happens when a foreign casino first wins a judgment abroad, then brings the judgment, not the bet, to Singapore. Four cases answer the puzzle in turn."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"The starting point is s 5(2) of the Civil Law Act 1909 (CLA): \"No action shall be brought or maintained in the court for recovering any sum of money or valuable thing alleged to be won upon any wager or which has been deposited in the hands of any person to abide the event on which any wager has been made\" (C&Y 5.85). A **wager** is a bet. The section targets the **action** to recover the winnings."},{"p":"In ***Star City***, a foreign casino sued in Singapore directly on the gambling debt, and s 5(2) barred the claim. ***Burswood*** moved from the bet to a **judgment**: a Western Australian judgment on a gambling debt, registered under the RECJA. The Court of Appeal classed s 5(2) as merely **domestic** policy, which cannot block a foreign judgment, so registration was allowed."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**CLA s 5(2)** bars any action to recover money won on a wager (C&Y 5.85)."},{"t":"***Star City*** (direct claim in Singapore): s 5(2) applies. The courts will not act as \"gambling debt collectors for foreign casinos\". The section stops enforcement but does not make the contract invalid (*Venetian Macau* [37]–[38]; C&Y 5.86)."},{"t":"***Burswood*** (registration under the RECJA): s 5(2) reflects only domestic public policy, so registration was allowed (*Venetian Macau* [39]–[40]; C&Y 5.86).","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"Later High Court cases under the RECJA still treated *Burswood* as binding (*Venetian Macau* [45])."}]}]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-3.11","LAW302-Q13-3.12"]},{"k":"step","t":"The gambling cases (2): *Poh Soon Kiat*'s obiter"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Sometimes a court decides a case on one simple point, then adds a long \"and by the way\" about something else. The \"by the way\" is worth hearing, but it does not bind later courts. *Poh Soon Kiat* is the gambling cases' big \"by the way\"."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"The **ratio** of a case is the reasoning necessary to its decision, and it binds later courts. **Obiter** remarks go beyond what was necessary; they can persuade but do not bind."},{"p":"In *Poh Soon Kiat*, the claimant sued at common law on a Californian judgment based on a Nevadan gambling debt. The ratio was narrow: the Californian judgment was not a **fresh money judgment**. Everything the Court of Appeal said about gambling, listed below, was obiter, though emphatic."},{"p":"Two remarks need unpacking. At [120], the court tentatively said that a judgment that would not have been registrable should also be unenforceable at common law. RECJA policy targeted the cause of action, while common law policy targets the judgment, so this implies that the gambling cause of action **infects** the judgment (C&Y 5.87). At [121], the court left open whether an action on the judgment could be **re-characterised** as a direct claim for the gambling debt. Traditionally, a foreign judgment creates a new and independent obligation to pay, so the common law action is on that obligation, not on the bet. If the court may look through it, s 5(2) clearly applies (C&Y 5.88)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"***Poh Soon Kiat*** (common law action on a Californian judgment):","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Ratio:** the Californian judgment was not a fresh money judgment (*Venetian Macau* [41]; C&Y 5.85)."},{"t":"**Obiter:**","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"*Burswood* is \"unsound\";"},{"t":"gambling in general, especially unregulated gambling and gambling on credit, is contrary to Singapore public policy, as the retention of s 5 of the CLA shows; the court pointed to the extensive regulation of legalised gambling and to surveys of public views ([97]; C&Y 5.86);"},{"t":"statutory public policy prevails over \"higher\" international policy [113];"},{"t":"tentatively, a judgment that would not have been registrable should also be unenforceable at common law [120], implying that the s 5(2) policy infects the judgment (C&Y 5.87);"},{"t":"possibly, the action could be **re-characterised** as a direct action to recover a gambling debt; left open [121] (C&Y 5.88).","b":["open"]}]}],"b":["obiter"]}]}]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-3.6"]},{"k":"step","t":"The gambling cases (3): *Venetian Macau* (2026)"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"The casino's argument ran: \"Singapore bars suing on the *bet*, but we are only registering a *judgment*.\" The High Court's answer: a gambling debt dressed up as a judgment is still a gambling debt, and Singapore will not collect it."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"In *Venetian Macau v Hu* [2026] SGHC 180, a casino operator registered a Hong Kong judgment for a gambling debt under the REFJA. The High Court held that enforcing it was contrary to Singapore public policy under REFJA s 5(1)(a)(v). *Burswood* was no obstacle: it concerned a provision that tested the cause of action and had a different legislative origin (the UK Acts of 1920 and 1933). And counsel could not explain why a direct claim on the debt should be barred but a judgment on the same debt enforced."}]},{"k":"call","c":"tip","n":[{"p":"Identify the regime first. Under the REFJA, apply *Venetian Macau*. For a common law action on a foreign gambling judgment, build on *Poh Soon Kiat*'s obiter at [120]–[121] and support it with *Venetian Macau*'s reasoning that s 5(2) draws no distinction between direct claims and judgments [59]. Rely on *Burswood* for neither."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"***Venetian Macau v Hu* [2026] SGHC 180** (REFJA registration of a Hong Kong judgment on a gambling debt):","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"*Burswood* is **not binding**, because its ratio concerned RECJA s 3(2)(f), a different provision from REFJA s 5(1)(a)(v), with a different legislative origin [27]–[33];"},{"t":"enforcing a foreign judgment founded on a gambling debt **is contrary to Singapore public policy**, which is embodied in CLA s 5(2) [56], applying the *Poh Soon Kiat* hierarchy [54];"},{"t":"s 5(2) makes no distinction between direct claims and the enforcement of foreign judgments [59];"},{"t":"registration counts as an \"action\": ROC 2021 defines \"action\" broadly, and REFJA registration is commenced by originating application [60]."}]}]},{"t":"C&Y 5.86 predates this decision and treats *Burswood* as live. Follow *Venetian Macau*, which also confirms that the RECJA has been repealed [29]."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-3.4","LAW302-Q13-3.5"]},{"k":"take","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Merely domestic policy, or mere difference from Singapore law, is never enough; the policy must be of the \"higher\" kind or statutory."},{"t":"At common law and under the REFJA, the effect of enforcing the judgment must offend; the RECJA looked at the cause of action. A particularly egregious cause of action can infect the judgment."},{"t":"Statute sits at the top of the *Poh Soon Kiat* hierarchy, though Chong & Yip say only \"higher\" statutory policy should count."},{"t":"After *Venetian Macau*, enforcing a foreign gambling judgment under the REFJA is contrary to the public policy embodied in CLA s 5(2), and *Burswood* is not binding."}]}]},{"k":"traps","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Do not cite *Burswood* as settled law for a REFJA or common law case."},{"t":"Do not describe *Poh Soon Kiat*'s comments on gambling as its ratio. The ratio was that the Californian judgment was not a fresh money judgment."},{"t":"Do not apply domestic public policy as the test."},{"t":"Do not read \"no estoppel on Singapore public policy\" as permission to raise facts the debtor held back abroad."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":true,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-3.13","LAW302-Q13-3.14","LAW302-Q13-3.15"]}],"ic":"shield","nq":15},{"id":"w13.m2.4","code":"2.4","kind":"module","t":"Natural justice","blocks":[{"k":"obj","t":"Apply the two core limbs of the defence, know who decides whether it is made out, and handle the \"substantial justice\" argument."},{"k":"big","t":"The defendant must have had **notice** of the proceedings and an **opportunity to present its case**. A defendant who knew and chose to stay away has no complaint."},{"k":"step","t":"The two limbs: notice and a chance to be heard"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Fair play has two simple parts. You must be **told** the game is on, and you must get a **turn to speak**. If you were told and chose to stay home, you cannot later complain that you missed your turn."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"**Natural justice** here means basic procedural fairness in the foreign proceedings. Traditionally it has two limbs: the foreign court must have given the defendant **notice** of the proceedings and, having done so, an **opportunity** to defend itself (C&Y 5.76, citing *Jacobson v Frachon*). Why does Singapore insist on this, when it otherwise refuses to look behind foreign judgments? Because a judgment against someone who was never heard is not really a decision between two sides, and enforcing it would put Singapore's authority behind a one-sided process."},{"p":"The limbs protect the *chance* to be heard, not its use. A **default judgment** (one given because the defendant did not respond) is no breach if the documents were properly served: the defendant chose to let judgment go by default. And **due notice** means notice of the proceedings, not of the steps needed to defend them."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**The core test:** notice and an opportunity to substantially present one's case (*Tannos* [28], [42]; quoted in *Sang Cheol Woo* [70])."},{"t":"A default judgment given after proper service is **not** a breach (C&Y 5.76)."},{"t":"**Due notice** means notice of the proceedings, not of the steps needed to defend them. A defendant who knew of the proceedings cannot rely on lack of due notice (*Sang Cheol Woo* [70], citing Cheshire)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-4.1"]},{"k":"step","t":"Whose standards, and when to wait"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Singapore uses its own measuring stick for fairness. But if the other country's appeal court is already looking at the same complaint, Singapore may wait to see whether that court fixes the problem itself."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Whether there was a breach is for the Singapore court alone, by its own standards. Otherwise a foreign court could certify its own fairness. Comity points the other way on timing. If an appeal against the alleged breach is pending abroad, the foreign court may correct the violation itself, so Singapore should not rush in. In *Tannos*, the Court of Appeal first checked whether any recourse in Indonesia was actually being pursued, and found that none was."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Whether there was a breach is for the **Singapore court alone**, by its own standards. It is not bound by the foreign court's view (C&Y 5.77; *Tannos* [58])."},{"t":"If an appeal on the point is **pending** abroad, it may be premature for Singapore to decide (C&Y 5.77; *Tannos* holding (2), [30], [40]–[41])."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-4.2"]},{"k":"step","t":"*Tannos*: late participation does not cure missing notice"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"You are never told about the meeting where the key decision is made. You turn up to later meetings and protest, but by then the rules say that decision cannot be challenged. Turning up later did not give you a real say."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"In *Paulus Tannos*, Indonesian guarantors missed the key **PKPU** hearing because they were never properly served. (PKPU is a debt restructuring process in which an order temporarily suspends the debtor's repayment obligations: C&Y 5.78.) Turning up later, and protesting throughout, did not help: by then Indonesian procedure no longer let them raise their objections. The opportunity to be heard must be real."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"***Tannos*** (majority: Menon CJ and Tay JA; Woo Bih Li J dissenting; C&Y 5.78):","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Facts:** Indonesian guarantors were not properly served with the PKPU (debt restructuring) application and missed the 9 January hearing."},{"t":"**Holding:** their later attendance at creditors' meetings and the bankruptcy hearing, protesting about service throughout, did **not** cure the defect, because Indonesian procedure no longer let them raise their objections. The respondents could not prove valid service, and the majority declined to infer that the guarantors had actual knowledge. The Indonesian orders were **not recognised**."},{"t":"**Dissent:** Woo Bih Li J would have inferred knowledge, because the guarantors signed powers of attorney on 11 January and their accounts of how they learned of the proceedings were inconsistent."}]}]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-4.3"]},{"k":"step","t":"*Sang Cheol Woo*: the defendant who chose to stay away"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Bob gets the invitation to the match, reads it, decides the other side's case is silly, and stays home. He loses. That is not unfair. It was his choice."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"*Sang Cheol Woo* is the mirror image of *Tannos*. Spackman was personally served with the Korean appeal documents and a notice of the hearing date, but stayed away because he thought the claim frivolous. He had notice and an opportunity to be heard, and \"simply chose not to\" attend. His complaint that nobody warned him Korea's \"deemed confession\" rule would be applied failed too: due notice concerns the proceedings, not the steps needed to defend them."},{"p":"The case also shows what happens when a complaint about the merits is **repackaged** as natural justice: it runs straight into the rule that the enforcing court does not review the merits."}]},{"k":"call","c":"example","n":[{"p":"Mei sues Bob in Country X. Bob is served, receives the hearing date, and ignores both. Judgment goes against him. In Singapore he says he did not realise what he needed to file. That is a complaint about the steps to defend, not about notice of the proceedings, so it fails (*Sang Cheol Woo* [70])."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"***Sang Cheol Woo* [68]–[95]:**","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"Spackman was personally served and received notice of the hearing, but **chose** not to attend. There was no breach of natural justice [70]–[72]."},{"t":"Korean law did not require advance notice of the deemed confession rule [83]."},{"t":"Repackaging a merits complaint as \"natural justice\" runs into *Hong Pian Tee* [28], [31] (*Sang Cheol Woo* [94])."}]}]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-4.4"]},{"k":"step","t":"Beyond notice: \"substantial justice\" and foreign procedural slips"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Some people say fair play means more than being told and being heard: the referee must also do the job properly. English courts accepted a version of this; Singapore has not. And a referee who slips up on his own league's rulebook has not, for that reason alone, played unfairly."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"In *Adams v Cape*, a US judge did not objectively assess the plaintiffs' damages. He adopted counsel's proposal of an average award for all of them. The English Court of Appeal held that natural justice is not limited to notice and opportunity. It also covers a breach of English views of **\"substantial justice\"**, and these facts fell within it (C&Y 5.79). Two limits came with it. Mere procedural irregularity under the foreign court's own rules is not a defence. And where a defect could be corrected within the foreign system, the defendant is generally expected to seek recourse there first. (The *Adams* defendants had not, but that did not count against them, because they had no knowledge of any basis for seeking relief from the US court: C&Y 5.79.)"},{"p":"Singapore has not adopted the extension. *Tannos* left it open, and in *Sang Cheol Woo* no authority or principled basis was offered for it, and there was no breach on the facts anyway. A related point from the slides: **bias** of the foreign court overlaps with the idea of fraud by the court, which Module 2.2 treats as extrinsic fraud."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"***Adams v Cape***: natural justice also covers a breach of English views of \"substantial justice\" (C&Y 5.79). But:","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"mere procedural irregularity under the foreign court's own rules is not a defence (567G–568A);"},{"t":"the possibility of correcting the error **within the foreign system** must be considered (570C; *Sang Cheol Woo* [92])."}]}]},{"t":"**Singapore:** the \"substantial justice\" extension is **not established**. *Tannos* [29] left it open, and in *Sang Cheol Woo* no authority or principled basis was offered for it (C&Y 5.79; *Sang Cheol Woo* [73]–[95]).","b":["open"]},{"t":"**Bias** of the foreign court overlaps with the idea of fraud by the court (slide 5)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-4.7","LAW302-Q13-4.5"]},{"k":"step","t":"The statutory versions: REFJA and CCAA"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"The statutes add a rule about turning up. Under the REFJA, if Bob appeared in the foreign court at all, he cannot use the notice ground in Singapore. Under the CCAA, if Bob appeared, could have complained about late notice there, but argued the case instead, he cannot save the complaint for Singapore."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"**REFJA s 5(1)(a)(iii)** applies where the debtor \"did not (notwithstanding that process may have been duly served on him in accordance with the law of the country of the original court) receive notice of those proceedings in sufficient time to enable him to defend the proceedings and did not appear\". So valid service under the foreign law is not the end of the matter: what counts is whether the debtor actually received notice in time. But the ground is narrower than the common law, because the debtor must also not have appeared (C&Y 5.167)."},{"p":"The **CCAA** is more forgiving. Appearing does not by itself defeat s 14(a). The ground is lost only if the law of the State of origin allowed notification to be challenged and the defendant appeared and presented its case without challenging it: it was expected to raise the point before the chosen court (C&Y 5.188–5.189). The discretionary service ground in s 15(1)(c) comes from a Hague provision written for States that treat service of a foreign writ on their territory as a sovereign act. Singapore does not, so it will rarely matter here (C&Y 5.194)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**REFJA s 5(1)(a)(iii)** is **narrower** than the common law: the debtor must not have received notice in time **and** must **not have appeared**. Registration shall then be set aside, even if process was duly served under the foreign law (C&Y 5.167)."},{"t":"**CCAA s 14(a)** is a mandatory ground (no notification of the document instituting the proceedings, including the essential elements of the claim, in time to defend). It is lost if the law of the State of origin allowed a challenge to notification and the defendant appeared and presented its case without making that challenge (C&Y 5.188–5.189)."},{"t":"**CCAA s 14(c)** includes proceedings incompatible with fundamental principles of procedural fairness, and overlaps with s 14(a) (C&Y 5.191)."},{"t":"**CCAA s 15(1)(c)** (service incompatible with Singapore's fundamental principles on service) is discretionary and, for the sovereignty reason above, rarely relevant in Singapore (C&Y 5.194)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-4.8","LAW302-Q13-4.6"]},{"k":"take","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Natural justice means notice of the proceedings and a real opportunity to present one's case."},{"t":"Singapore decides the question by its own standards, but may wait for a pending foreign appeal."},{"t":"A defendant who knew and stayed away (*Sang Cheol Woo*) has no complaint; one who never had a real chance (*Tannos*) does."},{"t":"\"Substantial justice\" is not established in Singapore, and mere foreign procedural irregularity is never enough."},{"t":"Under the REFJA, a debtor who appeared abroad cannot use the notice ground at all; under the CCAA, one who appeared and argued without challenging notification, where the law of origin allowed a challenge, loses s 14(a)."}]}]},{"k":"traps","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"\"Default judgment\" does not equal \"breach of natural justice\"."},{"t":"\"The foreign court broke its own procedural rules\" does not equal \"breach of natural justice\"."},{"t":"Valid service under the foreign law does not by itself defeat REFJA s 5(1)(a)(iii); actual notice in time is what counts."},{"t":"Do not present Woo Bih Li J's dissent in *Tannos* as the holding."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":true,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-4.9","LAW302-Q13-4.10"]}],"ic":"users","nq":10},{"id":"w13.m2.5","code":"2.5","kind":"module","t":"Res judicata and conflicting judgments","blocks":[{"k":"obj","t":"Decide which judgment wins when judgments conflict, at common law and under the REFJA and the CCAA."},{"k":"big","t":"At common law, between two foreign judgments the **first in time** wins, and against a **Singapore** judgment the foreign judgment loses, whenever it was given. The REFJA and the CCAA give the court a discretion instead."},{"k":"step","t":"Two foreign judgments: first in time wins"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"If two foreign courts give opposite answers to the same dispute, Singapore takes the one that came first. Otherwise the loser could keep trying new courts until one said yes, and nothing would ever be final."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"**Res judicata** (\"a matter already judged\") is the principle that a final decision between the same parties should not be reopened. Its tools are **cause of action estoppel** (the same claim cannot be brought again), **issue estoppel** (an issue already decided cannot be re-argued) and the wider rule in *Henderson v Henderson* against abuse of process (C&Y 5.103). A conflict arises when two judgments, each capable of founding such an estoppel, point in opposite directions."},{"p":"When both are foreign, Singapore recognises the earlier one. This promotes **finality** and discourages **satellite litigation**: fresh proceedings elsewhere aimed at undermining a judgment already given."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"***Merck* [36(a)]:** of two competing foreign judgments, the **first in time** is recognised. This promotes finality, discourages satellite litigation, and matches CCAA s 15(1)(e) and REFJA s 5(1)(b) (C&Y 5.94)."},{"t":"Slide 6 also cites *First Global Funds v PT Bank JTrust* on conflicts between two foreign judgments (see C&Y 5.94)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-5.1"]},{"k":"step","t":"A Singapore judgment always wins"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Singapore's own answer beats any foreign answer, whether ours came first or second. A legal system that enforced two opposite answers to the same question would contradict itself."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"If the foreign judgment is inconsistent with a **Singapore** judgment, the foreign judgment is not recognised, whether the Singapore judgment came before or after it. The reasons differ with timing. Preferring an **earlier** Singapore judgment protects finality, the policy behind res judicata. That reason self-evidently cannot explain preferring a **later** one. Two other reasons do: the party relying on the foreign judgment should have raised it in the Singapore proceedings, and preferring the local judgment keeps Singapore's legal system internally coherent (C&Y 5.93)."}]},{"k":"call","c":"example","n":[{"p":"Mei wins against Bob in Country X in 2024. Bob then sues Mei in Singapore on the same dispute. Mei never mentions the Country X judgment, and Bob wins in 2025. Mei cannot now ask Singapore to recognise her 2024 judgment. She should have raised it in the Singapore case."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"***Merck* [36(b)]:** an inconsistent **Singapore** judgment prevails, whether it came before or after the foreign one. This matches CCAA s 15(1)(d).","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"Why even a **later** Singapore judgment prevails: the party relying on the foreign judgment should have raised it in the Singapore proceedings, and preferring the local judgment keeps the legal system internally coherent (C&Y 5.93)."}]}]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-5.8"]},{"k":"step","t":"A foreign judgment that arrives mid-race"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Two races on the same course are running, one here and one abroad, and the foreign one finishes first. Should its result count? The Court of Appeal has not said."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Suppose Singapore proceedings are under way when a foreign court gives judgment on the same matter. The worry is **strategic, pre-emptive litigation**: a party races to a quick judgment abroad to head off the Singapore case. The Court of Appeal in *Merck* left the question open, but suggested that this worry could be handled by looking at all the circumstances. For comparison only, the Hague Judgments Convention 2019 (Art 7(2)) lets the court addressed postpone or refuse recognition where it was seised before the court of origin and the dispute is closely connected with its own country (C&Y 5.95)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"***Merck* [38]:** whether a foreign judgment handed down while Singapore proceedings on the same or substantially the same matter are **pending** will be recognised was left open.","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"Prof Yeo suggested it may still be recognised."},{"t":"The worry about strategic, pre-emptive litigation can be handled by having regard to all the circumstances, such as undue haste or a deliberate attempt to pre-empt recognition."}]}],"b":["open"]},{"t":"Compare Hague Judgments Convention Art 7(2) (C&Y 5.95)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-5.3"]},{"k":"step","t":"Cross-estoppel: a promise not to rely on the judgment"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Bob wins abroad, then tells Mei: \"Forget that judgment. Let's fight it out in Singapore.\" Mei takes him at his word. Bob cannot later wave the judgment at her."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"A foreign judgment can be met by an estoppel of a different kind. An **estoppel by representation** holds a party to what it represented. So a party who has represented that it would not rely on a foreign judgment may be prevented from raising res judicata at all. This meeting of an estoppel arising from a judgment (estoppel per rem judicatam) with an estoppel by representation is called **cross-estoppel**."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Cross-estoppel:** an estoppel by representation can prevent a party from relying on a foreign judgment, for example where it represented that it would not rely on it (*Merck* [36]; C&Y 5.103)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-5.6"]},{"k":"step","t":"The statutes: a discretion, not a fixed rule"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"The statutes write down versions of the same \"who wins\" rules, but they let the judge choose rather than dictating the answer."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Both statutes give the court a discretion where the common law gives a fixed answer, and the REFJA's ground is narrower: it catches only a judgment given **before** the foreign judgment. Under the REFJA, an **earlier** final and conclusive judgment on the same matter, whether from Singapore or abroad, lets the court set aside registration of the later foreign judgment. Contrast grounds such as fraud and public policy, where registration \"shall\" be set aside (C&Y 5.168–5.169). Under the CCAA, a Singapore judgment gets more protection than another foreign one: it need not be earlier or on the same cause of action."},{"p":"How could a Singapore judgment and a chosen court's judgment collide? The Hague Choice of Court Convention lets a non-chosen court hear a case on certain grounds, so parallel proceedings are possible (C&Y 5.196). Suppose the Singapore court takes jurisdiction because it finds the agreement for Country X's courts null and void under Country X law. If the chosen Country X court later gives an inconsistent judgment, Singapore may refuse it under s 15(1)(d), even if the Country X court held the agreement valid."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**REFJA s 5(1)(b):** a **discretion** to set aside registration where the matter in dispute had, before the date of the foreign judgment, been the subject of a final and conclusive judgment by a court having jurisdiction in the matter. It covers an earlier Singapore judgment or an earlier foreign judgment (C&Y 5.170)."},{"t":"**CCAA s 15(1)(d):** an inconsistent Singapore judgment between the same parties; timing and cause of action are irrelevant (C&Y 5.195)."},{"t":"**CCAA s 15(1)(e):** an inconsistent foreign judgment that is earlier, between the same parties, on the same cause of action, and recognisable in Singapore (C&Y 5.195)."},{"t":"Under s 15(1)(d), it is immaterial that the chosen court held the choice of court agreement valid (C&Y 5.196)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-5.7"]},{"k":"take","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Two foreign judgments: the earlier one wins (*Merck* [36(a)])."},{"t":"A Singapore judgment beats an inconsistent foreign judgment, whatever the timing (*Merck* [36(b)])."},{"t":"A foreign judgment given while Singapore proceedings are pending is an open question (*Merck* [38])."},{"t":"A party's own representation can stop it relying on a foreign judgment (cross-estoppel)."},{"t":"REFJA s 5(1)(b) and CCAA s 15(1)(d)–(e) give the court a discretion rather than a fixed rule."}]}]},{"k":"traps","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"\"First in time\" decides between two **foreign** judgments, not between a foreign judgment and a Singapore one."},{"t":"Finality explains preferring an earlier Singapore judgment, but not a later one."},{"t":"Under the CCAA, the conflicting-judgment grounds are discretionary (\"may\"), unlike the common law rule in *Merck* [36(b)]."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":true,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-5.2","LAW302-Q13-5.9"]}],"ic":"merge","nq":7},{"id":"w13.m2.6","code":"2.6","kind":"module","t":"Proceedings brought in breach of a dispute-resolution agreement","blocks":[{"k":"obj","t":"Explain why the common law position is open, how REFJA deals with the issue, and where the CCAA and anti-enforcement injunctions fit."},{"k":"big","t":"Under REFJA, a foreign court that heard a case in breach of an agreement to resolve the dispute elsewhere is treated as having no jurisdiction, unless the debtor chose to submit to it. At common law, no court has yet decided whether the breach is a defence."},{"k":"step","t":"A broken promise about where to sue"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Two flatmates agree that if they ever argue about the rent, their landlord will decide, and nobody else. When an argument comes, one of them asks a different person instead, gets a ruling in her favour, and waves it at the other. Should everyone treat that ruling as final, even though she broke the promise to go to the landlord?"}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"A **dispute-resolution agreement** is a contract term about where disputes will be decided. The two common forms are an **exclusive jurisdiction clause** (the parties choose one country's courts, and only those) and an **arbitration agreement** (they choose private arbitrators instead of any court). Suppose Mei and Bob agreed that the Singapore courts would hear all their disputes, but Mei sues Bob in Country X, wins, and asks Singapore to enforce X's judgment."},{"p":"Two values pull against each other. **Comity** (the respect that courts of different countries show each other's decisions) and finality point towards giving X's judgment effect. **Party autonomy** (the idea that commercial parties may choose their forum and should be held to that choice) points towards refusing it."},{"p":"In legal terms, the common law has no answer yet: no Singapore court has considered the point (C&Y 5.96). The course slides call it \"unclear\", but direct you to *Merck* [37], where the Court of Appeal said the common law defences should develop broadly in step with the CCAA and REFJA."},{"p":"Keep the order of analysis in mind. A defence matters only once the requirements are met. If Bob was never in X, never took part in the case and never agreed to X's courts, X's court lacked **international jurisdiction** (jurisdiction that Singapore's own rules accept, such as presence or submission), and the judgment fails before any defence is needed. The breach question bites when X's court otherwise had jurisdiction, for example because Bob was served while visiting X."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Common law:** there has been no judicial consideration of whether a foreign judgment procured in breach of an agreement to settle the dispute elsewhere can be refused for that breach (C&Y 5.96). Slide 6 calls it \"unclear\", but note *Merck* [37] on convergence.","b":["open"]},{"t":"Requirements come before defences. If the foreign court lacked international jurisdiction, the judgment fails on that ground and the breach point never arises (Stage 0)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-5.4"]},{"k":"step","t":"Four arguments for a defence"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Picture Bob paying Mei $100 because of X's ruling, and Mei then paying Bob $100 in damages for breaking her promise about where to sue. The money goes out and comes straight back. It would be simpler to say at the start: \"You broke the promise, so you cannot collect.\""}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Chong & Yip put forward three reasons why the common law should accept a defence (C&Y 5.96). The course splits the second into two, giving four arguments. They are arguments about how the law should develop, not current law, but they are what you would use in an exam answer."},{"p":"The first is the circularity the ELI5 describes. In Week 9 you saw that a party who sues in breach of an exclusive jurisdiction clause can be made to pay damages for the breach (*Tradewaves*; *Starlight*). So if there is no defence, Singapore enforces X's judgment for Mei, and Bob then sues Mei in Singapore for damages for breaking the clause. Depending on how the damages are calculated, they could cancel out the judgment sum. A defence would avoid enforcing a judgment only to unwind it."},{"p":"The second and third arguments (C&Y's second reason) are about consistency. REFJA already deems the foreign court to lack jurisdiction in this situation (s 5(4)(b), next step). A common law defence would bring the two regimes into line, which is what *Merck* [37] asked for. The fourth argument (C&Y's third reason) is the Hague Convention on Choice of Court Agreements (HCCCA), which Singapore has enacted through the CCAA. The Convention does not forbid a country from recognising a judgment of a court that was not chosen, but refusing one would fit the Convention's underlying principle of respect for party autonomy."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Arguments for a common law defence** (C&Y 5.96):","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"Damages for breach of a jurisdiction clause can be pursued in Singapore (see Week 9: *Tradewaves*; *Starlight*). Without a defence, the creditor enforces the judgment and the debtor then sues for damages, which may cancel the judgment sum. The result is circular."},{"t":"**REFJA s 5(4)(b)** already deems the foreign court to lack jurisdiction in this situation."},{"t":"*Merck* [37] calls for convergence between the common law and the statutes."},{"t":"The HCCCA (implemented by the CCAA) reflects respect for party autonomy. It does not itself prohibit recognising a non-chosen court's judgment, but refusing to recognise one would align with that principle."}]}],"b":["acad"]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-5.10"]},{"k":"step","t":"REFJA: no deemed jurisdiction, unless the debtor submitted"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"REFJA works like a checklist. One box asks: \"Did the foreign court have the right to hear this case?\" A promise to take disputes elsewhere puts a cross in that box. But if Bob later walked onto X's pitch and played the match anyway, he cannot fall back on the promise."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Under REFJA, Mei **registers** the judgment (has it entered in the Singapore court's records so that it can be enforced like a local judgment), and Bob may apply to set the registration aside. One mandatory ground is that the foreign court had **no jurisdiction** (s 5(1)(a)(ii); C&Y 5.162). Section 5(3) lists when the foreign court is **deemed** (treated by law) to have had jurisdiction over Bob: voluntary appearance, being the claimant or counterclaiming, prior agreement to submit, residence (for a company, its principal place of business), or an office in X through which the transaction was done."},{"p":"Section 5(4) cuts back those grounds in three situations: immovable property outside the foreign country (s 5(4)(a)), sovereign immunity (s 5(4)(c)) and, for us, s 5(4)(b). Under s 5(4)(b) the foreign court is **not** deemed to have had jurisdiction if bringing the proceedings there was contrary to an agreement to settle the dispute elsewhere (C&Y 5.166)."},{"p":"The exception: s 5(4)(b) does not apply where Bob **submitted** under s 5(3)(a)(i)–(iii), or where s 5(3)(c) applies (jurisdiction recognised by Singapore law, a ground C&Y call \"rather obscure\": C&Y 5.165). The submission grounds share one feature, Bob's own choice of X's court: he fought there, sued there, or agreed to it in advance. Residence or a place of business in X is not on the list, so a clause choosing Singapore still defeats jurisdiction that rests only on those links."},{"p":"Appearing only to protect property seized or threatened with seizure, to contest the court's jurisdiction, or to invite the court not to exercise its jurisdiction, is **not** a voluntary appearance (s 5(3)(a)(i)(A)–(C); C&Y 5.162). So Bob can go to X to argue the clause without losing its protection."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**REFJA s 5(4)(b)** (C&Y 5.166): the foreign court is not deemed to have jurisdiction if bringing the proceedings there was contrary to an agreement under which the dispute was to be settled otherwise than by proceedings in that court. Registration must then be set aside (s 5(1)(a)(ii); C&Y 5.162).","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Exceptions:** the rule does not apply where the debtor **submitted** under s 5(3)(a)(i)–(iii) (voluntary appearance, being a claimant or counterclaimant, or prior agreement to submit), or where s 5(3)(c) applies."},{"t":"Appearing only to protect seized property, to contest jurisdiction, or to invite the court to decline jurisdiction is not a voluntary appearance (s 5(3)(a)(i)(A)–(C); C&Y 5.162)."}]}]},{"t":"The Week 13 seminar outline cites \"s 5(3)(b)\" for this rule. In the current Act, and on slide 6, the provision is **s 5(4)(b)**."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-5.11"]},{"k":"step","t":"The CCAA and AEIs: what the breach does not do"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"The CCAA is a fast lane reserved for the court the parties picked. A judgment from a court they did not pick cannot use the fast lane. It is not banned from Singapore, though: it can still join the ordinary queue, which is the common law."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"CCAA Part 3 covers only judgments of the **chosen** court of a Hague Convention State, or of a court to which the chosen court transferred the case. A judgment that Mei obtains from a court the parties did not choose is outside Part 3. But the CCAA does not stop Singapore from recognising that judgment at common law, if it meets the common law requirements (C&Y 5.186; slide 10; s 21). So, unless the non-chosen court is in a REFJA country (where s 5(4)(b) applies), Mei's route is the common law, where Bob can run the open breach argument."},{"p":"Bob may want more than a defence in Singapore. If Mei is enforcing in several countries, he might ask the Singapore court for an **anti-enforcement injunction** (AEI): an order forbidding Mei personally from enforcing the judgment. The Court of Appeal has made clear that breach of the clause **alone** does not justify an AEI (*Sun Travels* [98], [104]). Stage 3 explains why. Bob's other option is the claim for damages for breach of the clause."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**CCAA:** a judgment of a **non-chosen** court is outside Part 3. But the CCAA does **not prohibit** Singapore from recognising such a judgment at common law (C&Y 5.186; slide 10; s 21)."},{"t":"**Contrast with AEIs:** breach of the clause **alone** does not justify an AEI (*Sun Travels* [98], [104])."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-5.13"]},{"k":"take","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"At common law, whether breach of a dispute-resolution agreement is a defence is open (C&Y 5.96). The arguments for it (C&Y 5.96): circular damages, REFJA s 5(4)(b), *Merck* [37] convergence and party autonomy."},{"t":"Under REFJA s 5(4)(b), the foreign court is not deemed to have jurisdiction, so registration is set aside, unless the debtor submitted under s 5(3)(a)(i)–(iii) or s 5(3)(c) applies."},{"t":"A non-chosen court's judgment falls outside CCAA Part 3, but may still be recognised at common law."},{"t":"Breach of the clause alone does not justify an AEI."}]}]},{"k":"traps","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Do not call the breach a settled common law defence, or say it has been rejected. It is open."},{"t":"Do not forget the submission exception: a debtor who fought the case on the merits abroad loses the s 5(4)(b) protection. An appearance only to contest jurisdiction does not count as submission."},{"t":"Residence in the foreign country does not save the foreign court's jurisdiction under s 5(4)(b); only submission (or s 5(3)(c)) does."},{"t":"Do not cite \"s 5(3)(b)\" for this rule. In the current REFJA it is s 5(4)(b)."},{"t":"Do not reach for the breach argument before checking international jurisdiction."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":true,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-5.5","LAW302-Q13-5.12","LAW302-Q13-5.14"]}],"ic":"branch","nq":7},{"id":"w13.m2.7","code":"2.7","kind":"module","t":"Foreign penal, revenue and other public laws; severance","blocks":[{"k":"obj","t":"Identify a \"penal\" judgment, explain why sums owed to a private claimant are treated differently, and decide whether the objectionable part of a judgment can be cut out."},{"k":"big","t":"Singapore will not help a foreign State enforce its penal, revenue or other public laws, even indirectly through a judgment. Money owed to a private claimant is generally different, and where only part of a judgment is objectionable, Singapore can cut that part off and give effect to the rest."},{"k":"step","t":"Singapore will not collect for a foreign State"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"If another country's tax office or police want money from Bob, they have to collect it at home. Singapore's courts will not act as their debt collector. Mei, a private person who won money from Bob in an ordinary lawsuit, is in a different position: Singapore may well help her."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Courts are used in two different ways. Sometimes a court decides a private dispute: Mei says Bob owes her money. Sometimes the State uses its courts to exercise governmental power: to punish (a **penal** law, such as a fine or forfeiture), to tax (a **revenue** law), or to vindicate some other **public law**. *Huntington v Attrill* drew the line between \"a suit for penalty by a private individual in his own interest, and a suit brought by the government or people of a state for the vindication of public law\" (C&Y 5.89)."},{"p":"In legal terms, Singapore will not enforce a foreign penal, revenue or other public law, **directly or indirectly** (*Maler* [68(a)]; C&Y 5.89). \"Indirectly\" is the key word here. A foreign State cannot get round the rule by obtaining a judgment at home and asking Singapore to enforce or recognise it. The question is whether giving effect to the judgment would involve **executing** the foreign penal law (*Maler* [68(d)])."},{"p":"You met the same idea in Week 12's requirements. A foreign judgment can be enforced at common law only if it is for a fixed sum that is **not a tax, fine or other penalty** (C&Y 5.28). REFJA likewise excludes money judgments for taxes or similar charges, or for a fine or other penalty (C&Y 5.154)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"***Maler* [68(a)]:** Singapore will not enforce, directly or indirectly, a penal, revenue or public law of a foreign state (C&Y 5.89)."},{"t":"***Maler* [68(d)]:** the question is whether recognising the judgment would involve **executing** the penal law."},{"t":"**For enforcement:** the judgment must be for a sum that is not a tax, fine or other penalty (C&Y 5.28; Week 12). REFJA excludes money judgments for taxes or charges of a similar nature, or for a fine or other penalty (C&Y 5.154)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-6.7"]},{"k":"step","t":"Who decides what is \"penal\", and how"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"A parcel's label does not decide what is inside. Singapore opens the parcel and looks for itself. A foreign law may be called \"civil\", or may sit in a criminal code, but what matters is who is chasing the money and for whose benefit."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Whether a foreign law is penal is decided by the **lex fori**, the law of the forum, which here means Singapore law (*Maler* [68(b)]). Singapore does not simply adopt the foreign system's label. The broad test, taken from *Huntington v Attrill*, is whether the proceedings are in the nature of a suit **by the State for the recovery of penalties**."},{"p":"So a law in a foreign criminal code is not necessarily penal (*Maler* [68(c)]), and a law applied in civil proceedings may still be penal, as *Maler* itself shows. Substance beats labels."},{"p":"The English courts apply the test as a simple distinction (C&Y 5.89). If the sum, being one for the recovery of a penalty, is to be recovered at the instance of the foreign State, it is unenforceable. If it is recovered by a private litigant in its own interest, it is enforceable."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"***Maler* [68(b)]:** whether a law is \"penal\" is decided by the **lex fori**. The broad test is whether the proceedings are in the nature of a suit **by the State for the recovery of penalties** (*Huntington v Attrill*)."},{"t":"***Maler* [68(c)]:** being in the foreign criminal code is **not conclusive**."},{"t":"**The English distinction** (C&Y 5.89): a penalty recoverable at the instance of the foreign State is unenforceable; a sum recoverable by a private litigant in its own interest is enforceable."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-6.1"]},{"k":"step","t":"*Maler*: forfeiture to the State"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Calling a confiscation a \"civil claim\" does not change what it is. If the government takes property from someone because the law presumes it was ill-gotten, that is the State punishing, whatever the court papers say."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"*The Republic of the Philippines v Maler Foundation* is the leading Singapore example. The Supreme Court of the Philippines ordered that monies held in Switzerland be forfeited to the State, under a law on the forfeiture of property of public officials that is presumed to have been unlawfully acquired. The Republic asked the Singapore court to recognise that judgment."},{"p":"The Philippine proceedings were civil in form, but that did not save them. The State pursued the proceedings, and the property was forfeited to the State. Recognising the judgment would therefore have amounted to indirectly enforcing a foreign penal law, and recognition was refused (*Maler* [68]–[69]; C&Y 5.92)."},{"p":"The decisive questions, as *Huntington* suggests, were who brought the proceedings and who benefited. Notice too that the rule is not limited to money judgments: it applies equally to non-money judgments, including judgments **in rem** (judgments deciding rights in property against the whole world, not only between the parties) (C&Y 5.92)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"***Maler* outcome:** a Philippine judgment forfeiting Swiss-held monies **to the State**, under a law on the forfeiture of public officials' property presumed to be unlawfully acquired, was refused recognition. It was civil in form, but the State pursued it and the State took the property (*Maler* [68]–[69]; C&Y 5.92)."},{"t":"The exclusionary rule also covers non-money and in rem judgments (C&Y 5.92)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-6.2"]},{"k":"step","t":"Money for a private claimant: the interest in *Sang Cheol Woo*"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"A late-payment charge you pay to the shop you owe is not the same as a fine you pay to the government. The first goes to the person you kept waiting and nudges you to pay on time. The second punishes you on behalf of everyone."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"*Sang Cheol Woo* sits on the private side of the line. Woo held a Korean judgment against Spackman carrying statutory interest at 20%. Spackman argued the rate was penal and contrary to Singapore public policy, relying on *Hong Leong Finance*, a Singapore case about a contractual default-interest rate."},{"p":"The High Court enforced the judgment. The interest was payable to the private claimant, not to the State, and its purpose was to deter delay in paying. Citing *Lewis v Eliades*, the court said the penalty principle concerns sums payable **to the State** [100]. Nor was enforcing the rate contrary to public policy: applying the *Poh Soon Kiat* indicators of public policy [97], it did not offend fundamental principles of justice or the sentiment of the general community [99]–[101]. *Hong Leong Finance* was a contractual default-interest case decided on its own facts [99]. And there was no dispute about Korean law: both sides' experts agreed on how the rate applied."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"***Sang Cheol Woo* [96]–[101]:** 20% Korean statutory interest, payable to the private creditor and aimed at deterring delay, was neither penal nor contrary to public policy.","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"The penalty principle concerns sums payable **to the State** (*Lewis v Eliades*) [100]."},{"t":"Public policy was tested with the *Poh Soon Kiat* indicators [97]. *Hong Leong Finance* was distinguished as a contractual default-interest case decided on its own facts [99]."}]}]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-6.4"]},{"k":"step","t":"Punitive damages for a private claimant: unsettled"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Punitive damages are extra money, on top of the real loss, ordered to punish the defendant. When that punishment money goes to the private winner, is it an ordinary private debt or a public fine in disguise? Different countries answer differently, and Singapore has not settled it."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"**Punitive** (or **exemplary**) **damages** go beyond compensation for the claimant's actual loss, to punish the defendant. They straddle the *Huntington* line: the purpose is punishment, but the money goes to a private party."},{"p":"On the English (*Huntington*) distinction, they should be enforceable, because a private litigant recovers them in its own interest (C&Y 5.89). *Schnabel v Lui* is sometimes seen as the opposite of the English approach (C&Y 5.90). In that case the Supreme Court of New South Wales refused to enforce part of a Californian judgment that punished the defendant for failing to comply with the Californian court's orders, although it was payable to a private party. In *Benefit Strategies v Prider*, the South Australian Full Court said in obiter (in passing, not as part of its decision) that the *Schnabel* award had \"an obvious 'public' connotation\" because it punished disobedience of court orders; punitive damages for a defendant's brazen and fraudulent conduct had no public element and would not be caught (C&Y 5.90)."},{"p":"Singapore's position is **uncertain** (C&Y 5.91). The Court of Appeal has adopted the *Huntington* understanding (*Maler* [68]–[69]), so it seems fairly clear that non-compensatory damages awarded to a foreign State would not be enforced. But the High Court in *Rodriguez Licea* left open whether US RICO treble damages awarded to a private litigant offend the rule [29]–[30]. Two further points pull in opposite directions. Singapore courts can themselves award punitive damages, at least in tort (*ACB v Thomson Medical*), so refusing a foreign award looks anomalous. Yet the statutes now limit non-compensatory awards, and C&Y suggest the courts may take their cue from them."},{"p":"Under REFJA (as amended), damages beyond compensation for the creditor's actual loss, including punitive damages, can be registered only for the compensatory amount, and the court must consider whether, and how far, the damages cover the costs and expenses of the foreign proceedings (s 4(5)–(6); C&Y 5.155). CCAA s 16 has the same costs point, framed as a discretion to refuse (C&Y 5.199)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Punitive damages awarded to a private litigant** (C&Y 5.89–5.91):","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"The English (*Huntington*) distinction suggests these are enforceable."},{"t":"*Schnabel v Lui* (NSW) refused a punitive component even though it was payable to a private party (C&Y 5.90)."},{"t":"*Benefit Strategies* treated that award as having a \"public\" element, because it punished non-compliance with the court's orders (C&Y 5.90).","b":["obiter"]},{"t":"The Singapore position is **uncertain**. The Court of Appeal adopted the *Huntington* understanding (*Maler* [68]–[69]), but the court left open whether RICO treble damages offend the rule (*Rodriguez Licea* [29]–[30]).","b":["open"]},{"t":"Punitive damages are available in Singapore for tortious claims at least (*ACB v Thomson Medical*), which makes refusing a foreign award look anomalous (C&Y 5.91).","b":["acad"]},{"t":"The statutes point towards a limit: REFJA (as amended) caps registration at the compensatory amount (s 4(5)–(6); C&Y 5.155), and **CCAA s 16** gives a discretion (C&Y 5.199)."},{"t":"The courts may prefer to take their point of reference from the statutes (C&Y 5.91).","b":["acad"]},{"t":"In *Sang Cheol Woo* [100], the penalty principle was said to concern sums payable **to the State** (citing *Lewis v Eliades*)."}]}]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-6.8","LAW302-Q13-6.9"]},{"k":"step","t":"Severance: cutting out the bad part"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"If one slice of a cake is burnt, you cut it off and serve the rest. You do not throw the whole cake away, as long as the burnt slice comes away cleanly. But if the burnt part runs right through the cake, slicing will not save it."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"**Severance** means the court divides a judgment into its objectionable and unobjectionable parts and gives effect only to the latter, provided the parts can be identified and separated (C&Y 5.99). It usually arises with the penal-law rule. In *Rodriguez Licea* [28], the High Court severed the punitive damages in a US judgment and enforced the compensatory damages. A judgment has also been severed into its unambiguous and ambiguous parts (C&Y 5.99)."},{"p":"Is severance available **only** where the penal-law rule is engaged? In *Giant Light*, a Chinese judgment ordered the seller to refund the price and pay damages, and the buyer to return the goods. The money order was enforceable on its own terms (Week 12, Module 3.3). The High Court added, in obiter, that severance would not have been appropriate, because the non-money order did not engage the penal-law rule ([78]; C&Y 5.100)."},{"p":"Chong & Yip disagree (C&Y 5.101–5.102). Severance makes more foreign judgments enforceable, so why should the penal-law rule be its only trigger? It has been allowed on other grounds before. They accept the *Giant Light* result could be justified differently: both sides' experts agreed the obligations were to be performed simultaneously, so the orders were inter-dependent ([71]). But that was not the court's reason. The two points fit together. The court enforced the money order without severance, because the buyer had already made the goods available for collection ([68]–[71]). That is why its comments on severance were obiter. Their preferred rule: severance should be possible whenever the money and non-money orders are **not inter-dependent**, so that a money order meeting the criteria for enforcement can be enforced."},{"p":"Both statutes provide for severance. REFJA permits registration of only those parts of a judgment that meet its requirements (s 4(10); C&Y 5.154). Under the CCAA, s 19 **requires** the court to recognise a severable part."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Severance** (C&Y 5.99): the court may enforce the unobjectionable part of a judgment if it can be clearly identified and separated (*Rodriguez Licea* [28]: punitive damages severed, compensatory damages enforced)."},{"t":"In *Giant Light*, the court thought severance inappropriate where the non-money order did not engage the penal-law rule ([78]; C&Y 5.100).","b":["obiter"]},{"t":"C&Y 5.101–5.102 argue severance should be available wherever the money and non-money orders are not inter-dependent. In *Giant Light* the obligations were to be performed simultaneously ([71]), which could have justified the result, though it was not the court's reason.","b":["acad"]},{"t":"**REFJA:** only the parts of a judgment that meet the Act's requirements may be registered (s 4(10); C&Y 5.154)."},{"t":"**CCAA:** s 19 **requires** the court to recognise a severable part."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-6.3"]},{"k":"take","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Singapore will not enforce, directly or indirectly, a foreign penal, revenue or other public law (*Maler* [68])."},{"t":"Singapore law decides what is penal. The test is whether the proceedings are in substance a suit by the State for penalties; form, labels and the criminal code are not decisive."},{"t":"Sums payable to a private claimant, such as statutory interest, are generally not penal (*Sang Cheol Woo* [100]). Punitive damages for a private claimant remain an open question in Singapore."},{"t":"Severance lets the court enforce the clean part of a judgment (*Rodriguez Licea* [28]); whether it reaches beyond penal-law cases is debated."}]}]},{"k":"traps","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Do not decide penal character by the foreign label, the criminal code or the civil form of the proceedings. Ask who sued and who benefits."},{"t":"Do not say that punitive damages awarded to a private claimant are settled as enforceable, or as unenforceable, in Singapore."},{"t":"Do not treat a high interest rate payable to a private creditor as a penalty just because it is high."},{"t":"Do not refuse a whole judgment when the penal part can be cleanly severed."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":true,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-6.10","LAW302-Q13-6.11","LAW302-Q13-6.12"]}],"ic":"target","nq":10},{"id":"w13.m2.8","code":"2.8","kind":"module","t":"Satisfaction and limitation","blocks":[{"k":"obj","t":"Get the time limits right under each regime, and know when a paid judgment can no longer be enforced."},{"k":"big","t":"At common law, enforcing a foreign judgment means suing on an implied debt, so the six-year limitation period applies, running from when the judgment became final and conclusive. Recognition, REFJA and the CCAA each work differently."},{"k":"step","t":"Satisfaction: nothing left to collect"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Once an IOU has been paid, there is nothing left to collect. Mei cannot ask a second court to make Bob pay the same debt again."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"A judgment is **satisfied** when the debtor has paid it. A judgment that has been paid cannot be enforced (slide 7). Enforcement exists to make the debtor pay, and there is nothing left to pay."},{"p":"REFJA spells this out. A judgment cannot be registered if, at the date of the application, it has been wholly satisfied or discharged, or could not be enforced in the country of the original court (s 4(3)). If it has been partly satisfied, it can be registered only for the balance still payable at that date (s 4(9)). A registration that breaks these rules must be set aside, because the judgment does not meet the Act's requirements (s 5(1)(a)(i); C&Y 5.160)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Satisfaction.** A judgment that has been paid cannot be enforced (slide 7)."},{"t":"**REFJA** (C&Y 5.160):","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"a judgment that, at the date of the application, has been wholly satisfied, discharged, or could not be enforced in the country of the original court cannot be registered (s 4(3));"},{"t":"a partly satisfied judgment can be registered only for the balance remaining payable at that date (s 4(9));"},{"t":"otherwise registration must be set aside (s 5(1)(a)(i))."}]}]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-6.13"]},{"k":"step","t":"Six years, not twelve: *Poh Soon Kiat*"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"At common law, a foreign judgment works like an IOU: the loser owes the money, and Mei sues on the IOU in Singapore. An IOU claim has a six-year deadline. The longer twelve-year deadline is for Singapore's own judgments, which carry the authority of a Singapore court."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"A **limitation period** is the deadline for bringing a legal action. The Limitation Act (LA) sets six years under s 6(1)(a) and twelve years for an action on a judgment under s 6(3). Which applies when Mei sues on a foreign judgment at common law?"},{"p":"The High Court judge in *Poh Soon Kiat* said twelve years, because a judgment is a judgment. The Court of Appeal disagreed: the period is **six years** under s 6(1)(a) ([41]–[54]). Its reasons connect to ideas you already know. A foreign judgment has no standing in Singapore until a Singapore court recognises it; treating it as if it were a Singapore judgment would let a foreign State exercise sovereign power in Singapore [41]. At common law, Mei is not executing the foreign judgment but suing on the **implied debt** it creates (Bob's obligation to pay the sum) [42]. REFJA's six-year window for registration points the same way [44]. The LA is a domestic statute, so \"judgment\" in s 6(3) means a Singapore judgment [45]. And the English courts reached the same answer on the equivalent English provisions in *Berliner Industriebank* [49]–[53]."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Common law limitation** (*Poh Soon Kiat* [41]–[54]): the period is **6 years under LA s 6(1)(a)**, not 12 years under s 6(3). The High Court judge's 12-year view was rejected. The reasons:","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"a foreign judgment has no standing in Singapore unless recognised; treating it as a domestic judgment would let a foreign State exercise sovereign power in Singapore [41];"},{"t":"at common law the action is on an **implied debt** [42];"},{"t":"REFJA's 6-year window for registration is consistent with this [44];"},{"t":"the LA is a domestic statute, so \"judgment\" in s 6(3) means a domestic judgment [45];"},{"t":"*Berliner Industriebank* decided the same point on the equivalent English provisions [49]–[53]."}]}]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-6.5"]},{"k":"step","t":"When does the clock start? When the judgment becomes final"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"The clock starts when the IOU is final, not when someone first writes it down in pencil. If the foreign country's law says a judgment is settled only once the last appeal is over, the clock starts then."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"*Sang Cheol Woo* answers the timing question. The Seoul High Court gave judgment in September 2011, and the judgment could be provisionally executed. Spackman appealed, and the Korean Supreme Court dismissed the appeal on 31 October 2013. Under Korean law, a judgment becomes **res judicata** (finally decided and binding, so it cannot be reopened) only when appeals are exhausted. Woo sued in Singapore in February 2019: more than six years after the 2011 judgment, but less than six years after the 2013 dismissal."},{"p":"The High Court held the action was **not** time-barred. The six years ran from when the judgment became final and conclusive under Korean law, in October 2013 [43]–[48]. Provisional enforceability did not make the judgment final, because it could be reversed if the appeal succeeded [44]. This matches REFJA s 4(1)(b), which lets an appellate judgment be registered after the last judgment in the appeal, and the *Merck* [37] call for convergence [46]–[47]. Whether a judgment is final is a question for the foreign court's own law, proved by expert evidence (Week 12, Module 3.6)."},{"p":"Chong & Yip state that time runs from the date the foreign judgment was rendered (C&Y 5.97). The course follows *Sang Cheol Woo*: look for the date on which the judgment became final and conclusive under the foreign law."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**When time starts.** The six years run from when the foreign judgment became **final and conclusive** under the foreign law (*Sang Cheol Woo* [43]–[48]).","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"A Korean judgment of September 2011 became res judicata only when the Korean Supreme Court ruled on 31 October 2013; provisional enforceability did not make it final [44]. The February 2019 action was not time-barred."},{"t":"This is consistent with REFJA s 4(1)(b) and the *Merck* [37] call for convergence [46]–[47]."}]}]},{"t":"C&Y 5.97 says time runs from the date of the foreign judgment. Follow *Sang Cheol Woo*."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-6.14"]},{"k":"step","t":"No clock for recognition; the statutory regimes"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"The six-year deadline is for using the IOU as a sword, to make Bob pay. Using a judgment as a shield, to stop someone re-arguing a case that has already been decided, has no deadline, because nobody is suing on a debt."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Recall the difference between **recognition** (treating the foreign judgment as having decided the matter, for example to raise an **estoppel** that stops a party re-litigating it) and **enforcement** (making the debtor pay). The six-year bar exists because the enforcement action is on an implied debt. Recognition sues on no debt, so it is not time-barred. The same goes for a foreign judgment **in rem** that declares the parties' rights in property (C&Y 5.98)."},{"p":"The statutes have their own rules. Under REFJA, Mei must apply to register within six years of the judgment (s 4(1)(a), noted in *Poh Soon Kiat* [44]). Where there has been an appeal, the appellate judgment may be registered at any time after the last judgment in the appeal (s 4(1)(b)). The High Court has said, in obiter (a remark made in passing, not part of the decision), that the six-year period should apply in that situation too (C&Y 5.150)."},{"p":"The CCAA sets **no limitation period**, and the implied-debt reasoning does not apply to judgments within it (C&Y 5.181). But that does not mean timing never matters. A CCAA judgment is recognised only if it has effect in the State of origin, and enforced only if it is enforceable there (s 13(2)). So if enforcement has become time-barred under the law of the State of origin, Singapore cannot enforce it either. The same applies to a final injunction that was stated to last for a period that has now passed (C&Y 5.181, 5.183)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Recognition is different.** Relying on a judgment as an estoppel is not suing on an implied debt, so it is not time-barred. Nor is an in rem declaratory judgment (C&Y 5.98)."},{"t":"**REFJA:** registration within 6 years of the judgment (s 4(1)(a), noted in *Poh Soon Kiat* [44]). An appellate judgment may be registered at any time after the last appellate judgment (s 4(1)(b)).","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"The High Court has said the 6-year period should apply to appellate judgments too (C&Y 5.150).","b":["obiter"]}]}]},{"t":"**CCAA:** the Act sets **no limitation period**. But the judgment must have effect, or be enforceable, in the State of origin (s 13(2); C&Y 5.181, 5.183). A judgment that is time-barred or has expired there cannot be enforced in Singapore."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-6.15"]},{"k":"take","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"A judgment that has been paid cannot be enforced. Under REFJA a wholly satisfied judgment cannot be registered, and a partly satisfied one only for the balance."},{"t":"A common law action on a foreign judgment must be brought within 6 years (LA s 6(1)(a)), not 12, because it is an action on an implied debt (*Poh Soon Kiat*)."},{"t":"The 6 years run from when the judgment became final and conclusive under the foreign law (*Sang Cheol Woo*)."},{"t":"Recognition (estoppel) is not time-barred. REFJA allows 6 years to register. The CCAA has no limitation period, but the judgment must still be enforceable in the State of origin."}]}]},{"k":"traps","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Do not apply the 12-year period in LA s 6(3) to a foreign judgment."},{"t":"Do not start the clock on a date when the judgment was not yet final under the foreign law, even if it could be provisionally executed."},{"t":"Do not apply the six-year bar to recognition."},{"t":"Do not say the CCAA imposes six years, or that \"no limitation period\" means timing is irrelevant under the CCAA (s 13(2))."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":true,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-6.16","LAW302-Q13-6.6"]}],"ic":"clock","nq":6}]},{"id":"w13.s3","n":"3","title":"Anti-enforcement injunctions (AEIs)","sub":"State the extra threshold an AEI must meet, the recognised categories, and the caveats.","lessons":[{"id":"w13.s3","code":"Stage 3","kind":"stage","t":"Anti-enforcement injunctions (AEIs)","blocks":[{"k":"obj","t":"State the extra threshold an AEI must meet, the recognised categories, and the caveats."},{"k":"big","t":"An AEI stops the creditor from enforcing a foreign judgment, in named countries or worldwide, on pain of contempt. That comes close to **nullifying** a judgment that only the foreign court can set aside, so it needs **exceptional circumstances tied to unconscionability**, over and above the usual requirements for an ASI."},{"k":"step","t":"What an AEI is, and what it is not"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"An anti-enforcement injunction is Singapore telling the winner: \"You may not use your foreign win, in the places we name or anywhere at all.\" It does not speak to the foreign court; it speaks to the person who won. The effect is almost like tearing up another court's decision, so it is kept for exceptional cases. Where the picture breaks down: the judgment itself is not torn up. It still exists, and only the foreign court can set it aside."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"An **injunction** is a court order telling a person to do, or not to do, something. An **anti-enforcement injunction** (AEI) forbids a successful judgment creditor, such as Mei, from taking enforcement proceedings on her foreign judgment in a particular country, in several countries, or worldwide. It acts **in personam**: against Mei personally, not against any foreign court. If she disobeys, she faces contempt of court proceedings in the court that granted it (C&Y 5.207)."},{"p":"Compare an **anti-suit injunction** (ASI), which restrains a party from bringing or continuing proceedings in a foreign court, while the foreign case is still to come or still running. An AEI comes after judgment. A **defence** is different again: if Bob pleads fraud or public policy to resist enforcement in Singapore, he protects himself in Singapore only. An AEI is a remedy, not a defence, and can reach enforcement elsewhere."},{"p":"The starting principle is that each country's courts decide for themselves whether a foreign judgment deserves recognition and enforcement, so an AEI is exceptional (C&Y 5.207). The leading case is the Court of Appeal's decision in *Sun Travels & Tours v Hilton International Manage (Maldives)*."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"*Sun Travels* [88]–[114]; C&Y 5.207–5.209; slide 8","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"An AEI prohibits the judgment creditor from pursuing enforcement proceedings in a particular country or countries, or worldwide. Non-compliance is punishable by contempt proceedings in the court that granted it (C&Y 5.207)."},{"t":"It operates against the party personally, not against the foreign court."},{"t":"Generally, each court decides for itself whether a foreign judgment is entitled to recognition and enforcement under its own laws; an AEI is exceptional (C&Y 5.207)."},{"t":"An AEI is not a defence. A defence resists enforcement in Singapore only; an AEI restrains the creditor from enforcing elsewhere."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-7.6"]},{"k":"step","t":"Why the bar is higher than for an anti-suit injunction"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Stopping a race before it starts is one thing. Stopping the winner from collecting the prize after the race is over is much bigger: it comes close to cancelling the result. It also takes away every other referee's chance to judge the result for themselves."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"*Sun Travels* starts from the position that once a foreign judgment exists, an AEI should generally be refused. If the applicant had a real complaint about the foreign proceedings, why wait until they were over? Applying only after judgment suggests undue delay almost by definition [89]. The courts urge \"great caution\", even more than for ASIs [90]."},{"p":"The deeper reason is **comity**, the respect that courts of different countries owe each other's decisions. An AEI raises comity concerns beyond those of an ASI [97]–[98]. It takes away the power of other foreign courts to decide for themselves whether to recognise the judgment. It indirectly interferes with execution in the country that gave the judgment. And it is akin to \"nullifying the foreign judgment or stripping the judgment of any legal effect when only the foreign court can set aside or vary its own judgments\" ([98]; C&Y 5.208). Because the effect is so far-reaching, the applicant must show exceptional circumstances over and above the usual requirements for an ASI (C&Y 5.208)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Starting point.** Once a foreign judgment exists, an AEI should generally be refused. Applying only after judgment suggests undue delay almost by definition [89]. The courts urge \"great caution\", even more than for ASIs [90]."},{"t":"**Extra comity concerns** beyond those raised by an ASI [97]–[98]:","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"(i) an AEI takes away other foreign courts' power to decide for themselves whether to recognise the judgment;"},{"t":"(ii) it indirectly interferes with execution in the country that gave the judgment;"},{"t":"(iii) it is akin to stripping the judgment of legal effect, when only the foreign court can set aside or vary its own judgments."}]}]},{"t":"So the applicant must show **exceptional circumstances** over and above the usual ASI requirements (C&Y 5.208)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-7.1"]},{"k":"step","t":"Breach of a clause is not enough"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"A broken promise about where to sue is a good reason to stop a race before it starts. Once the race has been run and the result declared, the broken promise on its own is not enough to stop the winner collecting the prize."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Two things are not enough, on their own, for an AEI: a breach of the applicant's legal right, and vexatious or oppressive conduct by the other side [98]. The obvious example of a breach of legal right is a foreign suit brought in breach of an exclusive jurisdiction or arbitration agreement. The Court of Appeal noted, citing *Ecobank*, that no AEI has been granted simply because the foreign proceedings breached such a clause [98]. It confirmed that a judgment obtained in breach of a choice of court agreement does not, in and of itself, justify an AEI ([104]; C&Y 5.209). This is the point flagged in Module 2.6: the breach may help Bob resist enforcement or claim damages, but it does not by itself let him stop Mei enforcing abroad."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**What is not enough:** a breach of a legal right on its own, or vexatious or oppressive conduct on its own. **No AEI has been granted simply because the foreign proceedings breached an exclusive jurisdiction or arbitration clause** [98], citing *Ecobank*."},{"t":"Breach of a choice of court agreement does not, in and of itself, entitle a party to an AEI ([104]; C&Y 5.209)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-7.2"]},{"k":"step","t":"What is enough: fraud, or no knowledge until judgment"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Two situations change the picture. If Mei cheated to win, it would be wrong to let her keep using the win. And if Bob never knew there was a race until the result was announced, nobody can blame him for not objecting sooner."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"An injunction is an **equitable** remedy: one the court grants as a matter of conscience and fairness, looking at how both parties have behaved. So the exceptional circumstances for an AEI must be tied to **unconscionability**, meaning conduct that it would be against conscience to let a party benefit from ([105]; C&Y 5.208). *Sun Travels* identified two categories, both rooted in equity [100]–[101], [105]–[111]."},{"p":"The first is **fraud**. Following *Ellerman Lines v Read*, a creditor who obtained a judgment by fraud is \"in conscience bound\" not to enforce it. The second is **lack of knowledge**: the applicant did not know of the foreign proceedings until judgment was given. Such a party cannot have been dilatory, because it had no chance to act earlier. Each category answers the starting-point worry about delay in its own way. Fraud makes the creditor's conduct unconscionable; lack of knowledge explains why the applicant did not act before judgment."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Recognised exceptional circumstances**, both rooted in equity [100]–[101], [105]–[111]:","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Fraud** (*Ellerman Lines v Read*). The creditor is \"in conscience bound\" not to enforce a judgment obtained by fraud."},{"t":"**No knowledge** of the foreign proceedings until the judgment was rendered. A party who did not know cannot have been dilatory."}]}]},{"t":"The exceptional circumstances must be tied to unconscionability ([105]; C&Y 5.208)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-7.3"]},{"k":"step","t":"Delay, and how *Arkhangelsky* fits"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Time passing is not the same as dragging your feet. You drag your feet only if you knew you should act, or if your waiting hurt someone."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Because delay is the central worry, the court explained what counts. The passage of time alone is not enough. Delay matters when coupled with **knowledge** (the applicant knew of the proceedings and sat back) and/or **prejudice** (someone was harmed by the wait), which can include prejudice to third parties such as the foreign court [108]."},{"p":"The Court of Appeal also explained an earlier case that might have looked like a third category. In *Bank St Petersburg v Arkhangelsky*, an AEI was granted after the parties agreed exclusive English jurisdiction **after** the foreign judgment. Commentators had read it as creating a new category for jurisdiction agreements made after judgment. The Court of Appeal disagreed. The agreement did not itself justify the AEI. The case is explained by the absence of delay by the applicant and by the allegations of fraud, which place it in the *Ellerman* category [102]–[104]."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Delay** [108]: the passage of time alone is not enough. It must be coupled with **knowledge** and/or **prejudice**, and prejudice can include prejudice to third parties such as the foreign court."},{"t":"***Arkhangelsky* reinterpreted** [102]–[104]. In that case, an AEI was granted after the parties agreed exclusive English jurisdiction *after* the judgment. The Court of Appeal said this is **not** a new category for post-judgment jurisdiction agreements. It is explained by the absence of delay and by the allegations of fraud (the *Ellerman* category)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-7.8"]},{"k":"step","t":"Both sides' conduct, and a list that is not closed"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Someone who asks for fairness must have acted fairly. If Bob knew Mei was cheating and kept quiet, hoping to win anyway, he cannot complain as loudly afterwards."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Because the remedy is equitable, the applicant's conduct matters as well as the creditor's (C&Y 5.209). If Bob knew of Mei's fraud while the foreign case was running, said nothing, lost, and only then sought an AEI, the equities may shift against him. The Court of Appeal left that scenario for a suitable case [112]."},{"p":"Nor are fraud and lack of knowledge the only possible categories. In the court's words: \"while there is no closed list of exceptional circumstances where an anti-enforcement injunction may be warranted notwithstanding that a foreign judgment had been issued, any new category would only be recognised when the equities of the case are in favour of granting the anti-enforcement injunction: either as a response to unconscionable conduct (as in the case of fraud), or when the applicant has not lost its entitlement to equitable relief on account of unconscionable delay (such as when the applicant had no knowledge of the foreign proceedings until the judgment was rendered)\" [113]. Each case calls for a close examination of its circumstances."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Caveat** [112]: if the applicant **knew of the fraud** before judgment and kept silent, the equities may shift against it. The court left this scenario open.","b":["open"]},{"t":"**No closed list** [113]: a new category will be recognised only where the equities favour it, either as a response to unconscionable conduct or where the applicant has not lost its entitlement through unconscionable delay. A close examination of the circumstances of each case is needed (C&Y 5.208)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-7.9","LAW302-Q13-7.4"]},{"k":"take","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"An AEI restrains the creditor personally from enforcing a foreign judgment, in named countries or worldwide, on pain of contempt. It is a remedy, not a defence."},{"t":"Because it comes after judgment and raises extra comity concerns, it needs exceptional circumstances tied to unconscionability, beyond the usual ASI requirements (*Sun Travels* [89]–[98], [105]; C&Y 5.208)."},{"t":"Breach of a jurisdiction or arbitration clause alone is not enough [98], [104]."},{"t":"The recognised categories are fraud (*Ellerman Lines*) and no knowledge of the proceedings until judgment. The list is not closed, but new categories must rest on the equities [113]."},{"t":"Delay counts only with knowledge and/or prejudice [108]; the applicant's own conduct, such as silence about known fraud, may tilt the equities against it [112]."}]}]},{"k":"traps","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"\"The judgment was obtained in breach of the clause, so we get an AEI\" is wrong. Breach alone is not enough."},{"t":"Do not confuse **resisting** enforcement in Singapore (a defence, which only works in Singapore) with an **AEI** (a remedy that restrains the creditor from enforcing anywhere)."},{"t":"Do not describe an AEI as setting aside the foreign judgment or binding the foreign court. It binds the creditor personally."},{"t":"Do not read *Arkhangelsky* as creating a category for post-judgment jurisdiction agreements."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":true,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-7.5","LAW302-Q13-7.7","LAW302-Q13-7.10"]}],"ic":"lock","nq":10}]},{"id":"w13.s4","n":"4","title":"Choice of Court Agreements Act 2016, Part 3","sub":"Decide whether a judgment falls within Part 3 of the CCAA, explain how it takes effect in Singapore, and apply the mandatory and discretionary grounds for refusal.","lessons":[{"id":"w13.s4","code":"Stage 4","kind":"stage","t":"Choice of Court Agreements Act 2016, Part 3","blocks":[{"k":"obj","t":"Decide whether a judgment falls within Part 3 of the CCAA, explain how it takes effect in Singapore, and apply the mandatory and discretionary grounds for refusal."},{"k":"big","t":"This is the third core principle of the Hague Convention: a judgment of the **chosen** court must be recognised and enforced in other Contracting States, subject to an **exhaustive** list of grounds for refusal (C&Y 5.177, 5.186)."},{"k":"step","t":"What the CCAA is and why Singapore joined"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"The countries in the Hague Convention made a deal about one kind of contract clause: the clause in which business people pick one court to hear their disputes. Each member promises three things. The chosen court will hear the case. Other courts will stay out. And every member will honour the chosen court's judgment, checking it against only a short, closed list of problems."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"The Choice of Court Agreements Act 2016 (CCAA) enacts the Hague Convention on Choice of Court Agreements 2005 (the HCCCA, or \"the Convention\") into Singapore law, with effect from 1 October 2016 (C&Y 5.174). The Convention is built around the **exclusive choice of court agreement**: a clause in which the parties agree that one court, and no other, will decide their disputes."},{"p":"Why did Singapore join? Because of the Singapore International Commercial Court (SICC), established on 5 January 2015. Singapore signed the Convention on 25 March 2015, as part of a wider liberalisation aimed at making SICC judgments easier to enforce abroad (C&Y 5.02, 5.174)."},{"p":"In legal terms, the three promises in the ELI5 are the Convention's three core principles, binding each Contracting State (a country that has joined the Convention). The first two concern jurisdiction, meaning which court hears the case. Part 3 of the CCAA implements only the third."}]},{"k":"call","c":"why","n":[{"p":"The CCAA also shapes the common law. In *Merck* [37] the Court of Appeal said the common law defences should develop broadly consistently with the statutes, especially the CCAA, because it reflects a transnational consensus."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Three core principles** (C&Y 5.177): (1) the chosen court must hear the case; (2) a non-chosen court must refuse to hear it; (3) a judgment of the chosen court must be recognised and enforced in other Contracting States. **Part 3 implements the third.**"},{"t":"The Singapore courts treat the Convention's Explanatory Report (the Hartley and Dogauchi Report) as authoritative on interpretation (C&Y 5.177)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-8.11"]},{"k":"step","t":"Which judgments are covered"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Part 3 is a fast lane reserved for judgments of the court the parties picked, or of the court that court handed the case on to. A judgment from any other court, even one in a member country, must queue in the ordinary lanes. And the fast lane does not care which rule the court used to take the case: the parties' pick is what counts."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Part 3 asks who gave the judgment and what kind of decision it is. On **who**, Mei need not prove, as she must at common law, that the Country X court had international jurisdiction over Bob by Singapore's standards (presence or submission). The parties' choice does that work, whatever ground the chosen court actually relied on. On **what**, the definition is wide and includes **non-money judgments**, unlike common law enforcement, which needs a fixed sum. Interim relief, such as a freezing order, and procedural rulings are left out, because they are not decisions on the merits."},{"p":"A **judicial settlement** is a civil law device with no exact common law twin: a contract between the parties, approved by or concluded before the court during the proceedings, which ends them and is recorded by the court in an official document. It is not a consent order, and not an out-of-court settlement. The CCAA lets it be enforced but says nothing about recognising it (C&Y 5.180)."}]},{"k":"call","c":"example","n":[{"p":"Mei and Bob's exclusive choice of court agreement named the courts of Country X, a Contracting State. X's court grants Mei a freezing injunction (outside Part 3), then gives final judgment ordering Bob to deliver goods and to pay her costs (both inside). Had they settled before the X court, which recorded the settlement officially, Mei could enforce it under s 20."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Which courts** (s 2(1); C&Y 5.178; slide 9): the **chosen court** of a Contracting State (other than Singapore), or a court to which it **transferred** the case under that State's internal rules. The basis of jurisdiction the chosen court relied on is irrelevant."},{"t":"**\"Judgment\"** (s 2(1); C&Y 5.179): a final decision on the merits, a consent order or judgment, a default judgment, or a costs determination. Non-money judgments are included.","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Excluded:** interim measures such as Mareva injunctions (s 10, per slide 9), and procedural rulings."}]}]},{"t":"**Judicial settlements** (a civil law concept) of the chosen court can be **enforced** under s 20, with the same procedure and grounds of refusal as judgments (C&Y 5.178, 5.182). They do not include consent orders. Recognition is left to the common law, for example as a contractual defence to a claim on the merits (C&Y 5.180)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-8.1","LAW302-Q13-8.12"]},{"k":"step","t":"How a CCAA judgment takes effect"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Think of a gift card from a store chain. The Singapore branch accepts it at face value, with no fresh paperwork, but only for what it would buy at home: if it has expired where it was issued, it is expired here too. The analogy has a limit: Singapore can still refuse the card on a few listed grounds, covered next."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"At common law, Mei must bring a fresh action on the implied debt the foreign judgment creates. Under the CCAA she simply applies, and the judgment is treated like a Singapore one, but never with more effect than it has at home (in the \"State of origin\"). So a final injunction that has expired at home cannot be enforced here (C&Y 5.183). There is no implied-debt action, so the six years in LA s 6(1)(a) do not apply, but a judgment time-barred at home cannot be enforced here either (C&Y 5.181)."},{"p":"The Singapore court also respects the chosen court's work. It does not review the merits, and it is bound by the chosen court's jurisdictional **findings of fact**, but not by its **legal evaluation** of them. C&Y's example: an agreement may be concluded by any means of communication that makes the information accessible for later reference. If the chosen court found the agreement was made by email, Singapore is bound by that fact, but decides for itself whether email meets the legal requirement of accessibility (C&Y 5.185)."}]},{"k":"call","c":"tip","n":[{"p":"Keep two \"binding\" rules apart. Under s 13(3)(b), jurisdictional facts bind, but not the legal evaluation, and not in a default judgment. Under s 15(1)(a), a ruling that the agreement is **valid** binds even in a default judgment, legal evaluation included."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Procedure and effect** (s 13; C&Y 5.181–5.184): the creditor applies to the General Division (O 37 ROC 2021). **No fresh action** is needed. The judgment is recognised and enforced \"in the same manner and to the same extent\" as a General Division judgment, and the order may be served out of jurisdiction without leave."},{"t":"Recognised only if it has effect, and enforced only if it is enforceable, in the State of origin (s 13(2); C&Y 5.183)."},{"t":"The Act sets **no limitation period**, but a judgment time-barred in the State of origin cannot be enforced (C&Y 5.181)."},{"t":"**No review of the merits** (s 13(3)(a)), except so far as needed to apply Part 3 (C&Y 5.183)."},{"t":"**Binding findings** (s 13(3)(b); C&Y 5.185): findings of fact on which the chosen court assumed jurisdiction bind, unless the judgment was by default. Only findings of fact bind; the chosen court's **legal evaluation** of those facts does not.","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"C&Y suggest that findings of fact could also cover consent to the agreement (though consent can raise a legal question too, such as whether silence is acceptance), whether the agreement is exclusive, which court was chosen, whether the matter is civil or commercial, and whether the case is \"international\" under s 4 (C&Y 5.185).","b":["acad"]}]}]},{"t":"A non-money remedy that Singapore procedure cannot give need not be enforced, but the judgment should be given as much effect as possible (C&Y 5.184)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-8.2","LAW302-Q13-8.3"]},{"k":"step","t":"The mandatory grounds: s 14"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Some problems are so serious that Singapore **must** say no: Bob was not told about the case in time to defend it, Mei cheated in the way the case was run, or honouring the judgment would clearly offend Singapore's basic values. But late notice is \"use it or lose it\": if Bob turned up and argued his case without complaining when he could have, it is too late to complain in Singapore."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Section 14 says \"must\"; s 15 says \"may\". Two points about s 14 go beyond its bare wording."},{"p":"**Notice:** use it or lose it. The ground protects the defendant, but expects it to complain in the right place (C&Y 5.189). Compare REFJA s 5(1)(a)(iii), which C&Y describe as narrower than the common law defence: the debtor must not have received notice in time **and** must not have appeared, so any appearance defeats it (C&Y 5.167)."},{"p":"**Fraud:** only procedural fraud counts. That roughly equates to **extrinsic** fraud at common law: fraud outside the trial, which the other side had no fair chance to answer. **Intrinsic** fraud, such as lying about the merits at a contested trial, is not covered by s 14(b), though it might at most be argued under s 14(c) (C&Y 5.190–5.191)."}]},{"k":"call","c":"example","n":[{"p":"Mei wins in the chosen court of Country X after bribing a witness. That is one of C&Y's examples of procedural fraud, so Singapore must refuse under s 14(b). If Mei's witnesses had simply lied about the merits and Bob had fought the point at trial, s 14(b) would not help him."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Exhaustive grounds:** a judgment within the CCAA cannot be refused on any ground outside the Act, so common law defences cannot be added (C&Y 5.186)."},{"t":"Under s 14 the court **must** refuse, or must set aside an order recognising or enforcing the judgment (C&Y 5.187)."},{"t":"**s 14(a) Notice:** the defendant was not notified of the document instituting the proceedings in time to defend. **Excluded** if the law of origin allowed a challenge to notification **and** the defendant appeared and presented its case without making it (C&Y 5.188–5.189)."},{"t":"**s 14(b) Procedural fraud:** \"fraud in connection with a matter of procedure\", roughly extrinsic fraud. Examples: deliberately serving at the wrong address; giving false hearing details; bribing a judge, juror or witness; deliberately concealing key evidence (C&Y 5.190).","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"Substantive (intrinsic) fraud might at most be argued under s 14(c) (C&Y 5.191)."}]}]},{"t":"**s 14(c) Public policy:** recognition would be **manifestly** incompatible with Singapore public policy, including fundamental principles of procedural fairness. \"Manifestly\" signals a high threshold, consistent with the common law, where the defence rarely succeeds. It overlaps with s 14(a) (C&Y 5.191)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-8.15","LAW302-Q13-8.6"]},{"k":"step","t":"The discretionary grounds: s 15"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Other problems let Singapore say no but do not force it to: the choice of court agreement was invalid, a party lacked capacity to make it, the judgment clashes with a Singapore judgment or an earlier foreign one, or an appeal is still pending at home. \"May\" means the judge weighs it up."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Three features of s 15 need care."},{"p":"**Validity and capacity:** these are tested by two different laws, and a ruling by the chosen court that the agreement is valid binds Singapore more strongly than s 13(3)(b) findings do (C&Y 5.192–5.193)."},{"p":"**Conflicting judgments:** Singapore protects its own judgments more than other countries'. A clash is possible because the Convention lets a non-chosen court hear a case on certain grounds. If Singapore took jurisdiction because the agreement was void under the chosen State's law, it may refuse the chosen court's later inconsistent judgment under (d), even if the chosen court held the agreement valid (C&Y 5.196). At common law an inconsistent Singapore judgment **always** prevails (*Merck* [36](b)); under the CCAA it is a discretion."},{"p":"**Service:** this ground was written for States that treat service of a foreign claim on their territory as a sovereign act. Singapore does not, so it will rarely matter here (C&Y 5.194)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**s 15(1)(a) Validity:** the agreement is null and void under the law of the chosen court's State (including that State's choice of law rules), **unless the chosen court has determined it valid**. That determination binds **even if the judgment was by default**, and binds as to the legal evaluation as well as the facts. If the chosen court did not consider validity, Singapore may examine it (C&Y 5.192)."},{"t":"**s 15(1)(b) Capacity:** a party lacked capacity under **Singapore** law (including Singapore's choice of law rules). So incapacity is tested by both the chosen court's law (via (a)) and Singapore law (C&Y 5.193)."},{"t":"**s 15(1)(c) Service:** incompatible with Singapore's fundamental principles on service. Rarely relevant in Singapore (C&Y 5.194)."},{"t":"**s 15(1)(d) Inconsistent Singapore judgment** between the same parties. The **timing does not matter**, and it need **not** be on the same cause of action (C&Y 5.195)."},{"t":"**s 15(1)(e) Inconsistent foreign judgment** of another State. It must be **earlier**, between the **same parties**, on the **same cause of action**, and **recognisable** in Singapore (C&Y 5.195)."},{"t":"**s 15(2)–(3) Pending review or appeal** in the State of origin, or time for one not expired: the court may refuse, set aside or postpone, and the creditor may apply again later (C&Y 5.197)."},{"t":"**s 15(4) Transfers:** see the transfers step."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-8.4","LAW302-Q13-8.17","LAW302-Q13-8.19"]},{"k":"step","t":"Damages beyond compensation, and severance"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Imagine a bill for a meal plus a \"punishment surcharge\". Singapore may refuse to collect the surcharge but will still collect for the meal. Before striking it out, though, it checks whether the \"surcharge\" really pays for something, such as the costs of the case abroad."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Some legal systems award damages beyond the claimant's actual loss, such as exemplary or punitive damages. Singapore may refuse the excess, but must first look at whether it in fact covers the costs of the foreign proceedings, so a \"punitive\" label is not decisive. Whatever happens to the excess, the rest survives through **severance**: cutting out the objectionable part of a judgment and giving effect to the rest."},{"p":"Compare: at common law, whether punitive damages payable to a private litigant can be enforced is uncertain in Singapore (*Rodriguez Licea* [29]–[30]), though severance is available where the good part can be clearly separated (*Rodriguez Licea* [28]). REFJA caps registration at the compensatory amount."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**s 16(1):** the court may refuse to the extent that the damages (including exemplary or punitive damages) exceed compensation for the actual loss or harm (C&Y 5.199)."},{"t":"**s 16(2):** it must consider whether, and to what extent, the damages cover the costs and expenses of the foreign proceedings (C&Y 5.199)."},{"t":"**s 19 (severance):** the court **must** recognise a severable part of the judgment."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-8.8"]},{"k":"step","t":"Transfers: the Stockholm and Göteborg examples"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Mei and Bob booked one particular referee, and the association moved the match to another. If Bob protested in time and then lost, Singapore may let him off: he never agreed to that referee. If Bob asked for the switch, or won anyway, he has nothing to complain about. And if they had booked \"any referee from the association\", there was no real switch at all."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"A transferee court's judgment is still within Part 3, but a transfer can take a party to a court it never chose. The standard examples, from the Hartley and Dogauchi Report, use a clause choosing \"the Stockholm district court\" and a discretionary transfer to Göteborg. P is the claimant and D the defendant. For each, ask whom the judgment is against, and whether that party objected."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**s 15(4):** where the chosen court had a **discretion** to transfer and did so, recognition may be refused **against a party who objected to the transfer in time** (C&Y 5.198)."},{"t":"**The examples** (slide 11; C&Y 5.198):","c":[{"ul":[{"t":"(a) D asked for the transfer, P objected in time, and judgment was **for D**. Recognition **may be refused against P**."},{"t":"(b) Same facts, but judgment was **for P**. It is against D, who did not object (D asked for the transfer), so s 15(4) gives D nothing and it must be recognised unless another ground applies."},{"t":"(c) The court transferred on its own motion; D objected and P did not; judgment was **for P**. Recognition **may be refused against D**."},{"t":"If the clause had chosen \"the courts of Sweden\", a Göteborg judgment would be as much a judgment of the chosen court as a Stockholm one, even after a transfer, and is entitled to recognition under the CCAA (C&Y 5.198)."}]}]},{"t":"**Implication for Singapore judgments** (slide 10 prompt): transfers between the General Division and the SICC raise the same question abroad. Whether a transferred judgment is still the \"chosen court's\" judgment turns on what court the clause designates (compare the \"courts of Sweden\" point). This links to the Week 10 discussion."}]}]},{"k":"call","c":"tip","n":[{"p":"Ask three questions in order. Did the clause name one specific court, or a State's courts generally? Was the transfer discretionary? Did the party the judgment is against object in time?"}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-8.7","LAW302-Q13-8.21"]},{"k":"step","t":"How the CCAA fits with REFJA and the common law"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Picture three doors into the same room. If the CCAA door fits your judgment, you must use it rather than the REFJA door, and if the CCAA door is shut in your face, you cannot walk round to the REFJA door. The common law door stays open, but not for arguing the same points again."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"The CCAA sits at the top: a CCAA refusal cannot be side-stepped by switching to REFJA. The common law stays open, subject to abuse of process (an unfair use of the court's procedure, such as relitigating the same issues). In practice the CCAA will almost always suit the creditor better, because the procedure is simpler and non-money judgments can be enforced (C&Y 5.201, n 499)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"The CCAA **prevails over REFJA**. If a judgment falls within both, the creditor must use the CCAA. If the CCAA route is refused, the creditor cannot then register under REFJA (C&Y 5.200)."},{"t":"The CCAA does **not** bar the **common law** route, unless pursuing it would be an abuse of process, such as re-ventilating the same issues (C&Y 5.201)."},{"t":"REFJA bars a common law action for judgments within its scope, but REFJA does not apply to judgments within the CCAA (C&Y 5.173)."},{"t":"**s 21:** the Act is not exclusive. Other law still governs judgments and agreements outside it."},{"t":"A **non-chosen** court's judgment, even one given in breach of an exclusive agreement for a Contracting State's court, is outside Part 3 but may be recognised at common law (C&Y 5.186; slide 10; s 21). Whether the breach is itself a common law defence is open (C&Y 5.96).","b":["open"]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-8.22"]},{"k":"take","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Part 3 covers only judgments (and judicial settlements) of the **chosen** court of a Contracting State, or of a court it transferred the case to. Non-money judgments are included."},{"t":"The judgment is recognised and enforced on application, like a General Division judgment, but never with more effect than it has at home."},{"t":"The grounds are exhaustive. Section 14 (notice, procedural fraud, manifest public policy) is mandatory; ss 15 and 16 are discretionary."},{"t":"Jurisdictional findings of fact bind (not in default; not the legal evaluation). A ruling that the agreement is valid binds even in default."},{"t":"The CCAA shuts out REFJA but not the common law."}]}]},{"k":"traps","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Do not apply common law defences under the CCAA. Its grounds are exhaustive."},{"t":"Section 14(b) does not cover all fraud."},{"t":"Section 15(1)(d) has **no** timing requirement or same-cause requirement. Section 15(1)(e) has both."},{"t":"Do not apply the six-year limitation period to a CCAA application. Ask whether the judgment is still enforceable in the State of origin."},{"t":"Do not say the CCAA bars the common law route. It bars REFJA."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":true,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-8.13","LAW302-Q13-8.14","LAW302-Q13-8.5","LAW302-Q13-8.16","LAW302-Q13-8.18","LAW302-Q13-8.9","LAW302-Q13-8.20","LAW302-Q13-8.10"]}],"ic":"book","nq":22}]},{"id":"w13.s5","n":"5","title":"Side-by-side comparison of the three regimes","sub":"Compare the defences and grounds of refusal under the common law, REFJA and the CCAA, explain why they differ, and choose the ground that fits a set of facts under the right regime.","lessons":[{"id":"w13.s5","code":"Stage 5","kind":"stage","t":"Side-by-side comparison of the three regimes","blocks":[{"k":"obj","t":"Compare the defences and grounds of refusal under the common law, REFJA and the CCAA, explain why they differ, and choose the ground that fits a set of facts under the right regime."},{"k":"big","t":"All three regimes protect the same three things (fair procedure, Singapore's public policy and Singapore's own judgments), and none lets the Singapore court re-try the merits. They differ in the wording of each ground, in whether refusal is mandatory or discretionary, and in whether the list is still developing (common law) or closed (CCAA)."},{"k":"step","t":"Same complaints, three rulebooks"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"There are three doors into the same room. The **CCAA** door is for judgments of chosen courts in Hague Convention countries, and has the shortest checklist. The **REFJA** door is for listed countries: you simply register the judgment. The **common law** door is for everyone else: you start a fresh case in Singapore on the foreign judgment. Whichever door Mei uses, Bob can make much the same complaints, but each door has its own rulebook."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"At common law the defences are judge-made and still developing, and the Court of Appeal wants them to develop \"broadly\" consistently with the statutes, especially the CCAA, which reflects a transnational consensus (*Merck* [37]). REFJA lists its grounds in s 5 as reasons to set aside a registration. Most are mandatory; a couple give the court a discretion (C&Y 5.159). The CCAA has a closed list: s 14 says the court **must** refuse, s 15 says it **may**, and s 16 deals with damages beyond compensation. Nothing outside the list counts (C&Y 5.186). That closed list is part of the Convention bargain: every Contracting State (a country that has joined the Convention) gives effect to the chosen court's judgment, subject only to the agreed exceptions (C&Y 5.177)."},{"p":"One rule is constant: the Singapore court is not an appeal court. It will not re-examine the merits, so a foreign court's mistake of fact or law is not a defence under any regime. The reason is finality: if every judgment could be re-argued when the winner tried to enforce it, litigation would never end (C&Y 5.66). The open edges are the ones *Merck* left undecided: whether a judgment resting on an error of **Singapore** law deserves special treatment ([59]–[61]), and whether public policy might reach a judgment that is perverse or contains a sufficiently serious and material error ([58])."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**No review of the merits:** at common law (*Hong Pian Tee* [28], [31]; C&Y 5.66), under REFJA, and under the CCAA (s 13(3)(a))."},{"t":"Whether a judgment resting on an error of Singapore law deserves special treatment at common law was left open (*Merck* [59]–[61]).","b":["open"]},{"t":"Whether public policy could reach a perverse judgment, or one with a sufficiently serious and material error, was also left open (*Merck* [58]).","b":["open"]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-10.1"]},{"k":"step","t":"Procedure, time and pending appeals"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"The doors also differ in how Mei gets through and how long she has. The common law door means a whole new case within six years. The REFJA door means registering within six years; once Mei has registered, Bob may apply to have the registration set aside. The CCAA door means an application with no deadline in the Act, as long as the judgment still works at home. And if Bob has appealed back home, every door lets Singapore wait."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"The procedure tells you where Bob complains: as defences to Mei's fresh action at common law, by applying to set aside the registration under REFJA (s 5), and by resisting Mei's s 13 application (or applying to set aside the order) under the CCAA (C&Y 5.187)."},{"p":"The time limits differ for a reason. At common law Mei sues on an **implied debt** (the obligation to pay that the judgment creates), so the six-year period in LA s 6(1)(a) applies, not the twelve years for Singapore judgments (*Poh Soon Kiat* [41]–[54]). Time runs from when the judgment became final and conclusive under the foreign law (*Sang Cheol Woo* [43]–[48]; Week 12 Module 3.6). C&Y 5.97 dates it from the judgment itself; the course follows *Sang Cheol Woo*. Holding the judgment up as a shield is not suing on a debt, so recognition is not time-barred (C&Y 5.98). The CCAA, which involves no implied debt, sets no limitation period at all (C&Y 5.181)."},{"p":"A pending appeal in the country of origin is not automatically fatal under any regime, but each regime gives the court a way to wait. That balances two concerns: Singapore should not enforce a judgment that may soon be overturned, but Bob should not be able to hold Mei off simply by appealing."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Time under the statutes:** REFJA, 6 years to register (s 4(1)(a)); CCAA, none, but the judgment must be enforceable in the State of origin (s 13(2); C&Y 5.181)."},{"t":"**Pending appeal at common law:** a judgment under appeal can still be final, and enforcement may be stayed (C&Y 5.59); an appeal pending on a natural justice point may make it premature for Singapore to decide that point (C&Y 5.77; *Tannos*)."},{"t":"**Pending appeal under the statutes:** REFJA s 6 (C&Y 5.157); CCAA s 15(2)–(3) (C&Y 5.197)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-10.2"]},{"k":"step","t":"Notice and fraud: protecting a fair process"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Two complaints are about how the game was run: \"nobody told me it was on\" and \"the other side cheated\". The common law hears both, using Singapore's own idea of fairness. The statutes are narrower. REFJA's notice ground helps only a debtor who got no notice in time **and** stayed away. The CCAA's fraud ground covers cheating in the procedure, not lies about the facts."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"**Natural justice** (the basic rules of fair procedure) at common law requires notice of the proceedings and an opportunity to present one's case, judged by Singapore's standards, not the foreign court's (*Tannos* [28], [42]; C&Y 5.77). REFJA's version is narrower: Bob must not have received notice in time to defend, and must also not have appeared, though the ground applies even if he was duly served under the foreign law (C&Y 5.167). The CCAA's s 14(a) is lost if the law of origin allowed Bob to challenge the notification and he appeared and argued his case without doing so (C&Y 5.189). The thread running through both statutes is \"use it or lose it\": a defendant who turns up abroad is expected to complain there."},{"p":"Fraud has the same shape. At common law, **extrinsic fraud** (fraud outside the trial, such as bribing a witness or keeping the proceedings from the defendant) can simply be pleaded. **Intrinsic fraud** (lies at trial that the court has already weighed) needs fresh evidence that reasonable diligence would not have found and that would likely have changed the result (*Hong Pian Tee* [30]; *Ong Han Nam* [49]). REFJA says only \"obtained by fraud\". The CCAA's s 14(b) covers only \"fraud in connection with a matter of procedure\", roughly extrinsic fraud; substantive fraud may fall under s 14(c) (C&Y 5.190–5.191). At common law and under the CCAA, fraud about the procedure gets the easiest hearing, because hearing a complaint about fraud on the merits comes close to re-trying the merits. Whether REFJA draws the same line is unclear (C&Y 5.168)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Notice at common law:** the \"substantial justice\" extension is unsettled (*Tannos* [28]–[29], [42]; *Sang Cheol Woo* [68]–[95])."},{"t":"**Notice under the CCAA:** besides s 14(a) (C&Y 5.188–5.189), s 14(c) covers procedural fairness, and s 15(1)(c) on service is rarely relevant in Singapore (C&Y 5.194)."},{"t":"C&Y suggest that REFJA could adopt the common law approach to fraud, in line with *Merck*'s call for convergence (C&Y 5.168).","b":["acad"]}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-10.3"]},{"k":"step","t":"Public policy and penal laws: Singapore's house rules"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Every door has a house-rules check. Singapore will not let a foreign judgment make it break its deepest values, or turn it into a debt collector for a foreign government. The words differ from door to door, but the bar is high at each."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"At common law only the narrower, \"higher\" (international) public policy counts, and what must offend it is the **effect** of giving effect to the judgment (C&Y 5.80, 5.83; *Merck* [58]). REFJA s 5(1)(a)(v) likewise asks whether **enforcement** of the judgment would be contrary to public policy, so it operates against the judgment (C&Y 5.169). That wording mattered in *Venetian Macau*: *Burswood*, decided under the former RECJA provision that looked at the cause of action, did not bind the High Court, which held that enforcing a foreign judgment founded on a gambling debt is contrary to the public policy embodied in CLA s 5(2) (*Venetian Macau* [27]–[33], [56]). The CCAA's s 14(c) requires **manifest** incompatibility with Singapore public policy, a word meant to signal a high threshold (C&Y 5.191)."},{"p":"Penal laws and punitive damages show the statutes tidying up an uncertain common law. At common law Singapore will not enforce a foreign penal, revenue or other public law (*Huntington*; *Maler* [68]). Whether **punitive damages** (damages meant to punish rather than compensate) payable to a private claimant fall foul of this is uncertain, but the court can use **severance** (cutting off the objectionable part) and enforce the compensatory part (*Rodriguez Licea* [28]–[30]; C&Y 5.89–5.91). REFJA fixes a cap: damages beyond compensation are registered only up to the compensatory amount. The CCAA gives a discretion instead: the court may refuse the excess (s 16), and must recognise any severable part (s 19; C&Y 5.199)."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Penal and punitive damages under REFJA:** judgments for taxes, fines and penalties are excluded (s 2(1); C&Y 5.154), the registrable part of a judgment can be severed (s 4(10); C&Y 5.154), and non-compensatory damages are registered only up to the compensatory amount, taking costs into account (s 4(5)–(6); C&Y 5.155)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-10.4"]},{"k":"step","t":"Conflicting judgments and broken promises"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Two more complaints: \"Singapore has already decided this differently\" and \"Mei promised to sue somewhere else\". Singapore's own judgments get strong protection at every door, though the timing rules differ. A broken promise about where to sue is where the three doors differ most."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"At common law an inconsistent **Singapore** judgment prevails whether it came before or after the foreign one (*Merck* [36]). Even a later local judgment wins, because the party relying on the foreign judgment should have raised it in the Singapore proceedings, and because preferring local judgments keeps Singapore's legal system coherent (C&Y 5.93). REFJA's s 5(1)(b) speaks only of a judgment given **before** the foreign judgment, whether by a Singapore or a foreign court (C&Y 5.170). The CCAA protects a Singapore judgment whatever its timing, but another foreign judgment only if it is earlier and meets further conditions (C&Y 5.195)."},{"p":"Breach of an agreement to resolve disputes elsewhere shows the widest spread. At common law no court has yet decided whether it is a defence (C&Y 5.96). REFJA deals with it through jurisdiction: the foreign court is not deemed to have jurisdiction if the proceedings breached such an agreement, unless the debtor submitted by appearing voluntarily, by suing or counterclaiming, or by agreeing in advance (C&Y 5.166). Residence is not among those exceptions. Under the CCAA, a non-chosen court's judgment is simply outside Part 3, although Singapore may still recognise it at common law (C&Y 5.186). Separately, whatever the regime, breach of the clause on its own will not earn an **anti-enforcement injunction** (an order restraining the creditor from enforcing the judgment anywhere) (*Sun Travels* [98])."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Breach of a dispute-resolution agreement at common law:** not yet considered by the courts (C&Y 5.96).","b":["open"]},{"t":"C&Y argue for recognising a defence: damages for the breach are available anyway, REFJA s 5(4)(b) already deals with it, *Merck* calls for convergence, and the CCAA reflects party autonomy (C&Y 5.96).","b":["acad"]},{"t":"**Breach under the statutes:** REFJA s 5(4)(b) applies unless the debtor submitted under s 5(3)(a)(i)–(iii) or s 5(3)(c) applies (C&Y 5.166); a non-chosen court's judgment is outside CCAA Part 3 but may be recognised at common law (C&Y 5.186; s 21)."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-10.5"]},{"k":"step","t":"The full comparison"},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Each issue below lists the regimes in the same order: **CL** (common law) | **REFJA** | **CCAA**. Use it to revise, and to check that an answer uses the right column."}]},{"k":"cmp","cols":[["CL","Common law"],["REFJA","REFJA"],["CCAA","CCAA"]],"rows":[{"h":"Procedure","cells":{"CL":"a fresh action on the implied debt, usually decided by summary judgment.","REFJA":"registration (s 4), with set-aside applications under s 5.","CCAA":"an application under s 13 (O 37 ROC 2021)."},"note":[]},{"h":"Time limit","cells":{"CL":"6 years under LA s 6(1)(a) for enforcement, running from when the judgment became final and conclusive under the foreign law (*Sang Cheol Woo* [43]–[48]); none for recognition.","REFJA":"registration within 6 years of the judgment (s 4(1)(a), noted in *Poh Soon Kiat* [44]).","CCAA":"no limitation period, but the judgment must be enforceable in the State of origin."},"note":[]},{"h":"Review of the merits or mistake","cells":{"CL":"no review of the merits, so mistake is not a defence. The *Merck* points on errors of Singapore law and on perverse or seriously erroneous judgments are open.","REFJA":"no review of the merits, so mistake is not a ground for setting aside.","CCAA":"no review of the merits (s 13(3)(a)), so mistake is not a ground for refusal."},"note":[]},{"h":"Fraud","cells":{"CL":"the extrinsic/intrinsic distinction (*Hong Pian Tee*).","REFJA":"s 5(1)(a)(iv) \"obtained by fraud\". Whether the distinction applies is unclear.","CCAA":"s 14(b), procedural fraud only; s 14(c) possibly covers substantive fraud."},"note":[]},{"h":"Notice and natural justice","cells":{"CL":"notice and opportunity; \"substantial justice\" is unsettled.","REFJA":"s 5(1)(a)(iii): no notice in time **and** the debtor did not appear.","CCAA":"s 14(a) (lost if the law of origin allowed a challenge to notification and the defendant appeared and argued its case without making it); s 14(c) procedural fairness; s 15(1)(c) service."},"note":[]},{"h":"Public policy","cells":{"CL":"higher public policy; the **effect** of enforcing the judgment must offend.","REFJA":"s 5(1)(a)(v): **enforcement** contrary to public policy (*Venetian Macau*).","CCAA":"s 14(c), \"manifestly incompatible\"."},"note":[]},{"h":"Conflicting judgments","cells":{"CL":"the earlier of two foreign judgments wins; a Singapore judgment, earlier or later, always wins (*Merck* [36]).","REFJA":"s 5(1)(b), a discretion where an earlier final and conclusive judgment exists.","CCAA":"s 15(1)(d) (Singapore judgment, any timing) and s 15(1)(e) (earlier foreign judgment, same cause of action)."},"note":[]},{"h":"Breach of a dispute-resolution agreement","cells":{"CL":"open.","REFJA":"s 5(4)(b), unless the debtor submitted under s 5(3)(a)(i)–(iii) or s 5(3)(c) applies.","CCAA":"a non-chosen court's judgment is outside Part 3; s 15(1)(a) deals with the validity of the agreement."},"note":[]},{"h":"Penal and punitive damages; severance","cells":{"CL":"the *Huntington* and *Maler* test; severance (*Rodriguez Licea*).","REFJA":"registration is limited to the compensatory amount; judgments for taxes, fines and penalties are excluded, and registrable parts can be severed (C&Y 5.154–5.155).","CCAA":"s 16 discretion; s 19 severance is mandatory."},"note":[]},{"h":"Pending appeal in the State of origin","cells":{"CL":"can still be final and conclusive if the foreign law treats it as res judicata (finally decided between the parties); enforcement may be stayed (C&Y 5.59; *Sang Cheol Woo*); a pending appeal may make a natural justice challenge premature.","REFJA":"s 6 (set aside or adjourn).","CCAA":"s 15(2)–(3) (refuse, set aside or postpone, with a later application allowed)."},"note":[]},{"h":"Exclusivity of the grounds","cells":{"CL":"the list of defences is developing (*Merck* [37] urges convergence).","REFJA":"the grounds are listed in s 5.","CCAA":"an exhaustive list."},"note":[]},{"h":"Relationship between the regimes","cells":{"CL":"excluded for a judgment within REFJA; not excluded by the CCAA.","REFJA":"excludes the common law action (s 7(1)); does not apply to a judgment within the CCAA.","CCAA":"prevails over REFJA; does not exclude the common law (s 21)."},"note":[]}]},{"k":"take","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"No regime reviews the merits, so a foreign court's mistake is not a defence (subject to the points *Merck* left open at common law). The common law list is developing; the CCAA's is closed."},{"t":"The statutes are narrower: REFJA's notice ground needs non-appearance, and CCAA s 14(b) covers procedural fraud only."},{"t":"Singapore judgments are protected everywhere, but REFJA s 5(1)(b) covers only earlier judgments."},{"t":"Breach of an agreement to sue elsewhere is open at common law, a jurisdiction rule under REFJA (s 5(4)(b)), and a scope question under the CCAA."}]}]},{"k":"traps","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Do not add common law defences to the CCAA's closed list."},{"t":"Do not assume that REFJA's notice ground helps a debtor who appeared."},{"t":"Do not treat CCAA s 14(b) as covering all fraud."},{"t":"Do not treat a pending appeal as automatically fatal under any regime."},{"t":"Do not assume that residence saves a REFJA judgment given in breach of an agreement to sue elsewhere."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":true,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-10.6","LAW302-Q13-10.7"]}],"ic":"layers","nq":7}]},{"id":"w13.s6","n":"6","title":"Wrap-up: the whole topic as one story","sub":"Combine both weeks into one order of analysis (regime, requirements, defences, remedies) and apply it to problem questions that mix several issues.","lessons":[{"id":"w13.s6","code":"Stage 6","kind":"stage","t":"Wrap-up: the whole topic as one story","blocks":[{"k":"obj","t":"Combine both weeks into one order of analysis (regime, requirements, defences, remedies) and apply it to problem questions that mix several issues."},{"k":"big","t":"Work in a fixed order inside the right regime: requirements, then defences, then remedies. A judgment that fails a requirement never needs a defence, and a defence protects only assets in Singapore; only an anti-enforcement injunction reaches further."},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Mei wins a judgment against Bob in Country X and wants Singapore to make Bob pay."},{"ol":[{"t":"**Which door?** A chosen court in a Hague Convention country means the CCAA. A listed country means REFJA. Anyone else means the common law (Stage 5)."},{"t":"**Basic checks** (Week 12): Was Bob present in X, or did he agree to take part? Is the decision finished and about who is right? Is it a fixed sum of money?"},{"t":"**Bob's complaints** (this week): Did Mei cheat (fraud)? Was Bob never told about the case (natural justice)? Would paying offend Singapore's deepest rules (public policy)? Is this a fine owed to a government (penal law)? Has Singapore already decided the matter differently (conflicting judgments)? Is it too late (limitation)?"},{"t":"**Bigger weapons, rarely available:** if Mei cheated, or Bob only found out after the judgment, Bob might even stop Mei using the win anywhere (an anti-enforcement injunction, Stage 3)."}]},{"p":"If you can explain each step in your own words, you understand the topic. The practice round at the end of this lesson lets you check yourself."}]},{"k":"step","t":"Sort every complaint before you argue it"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"Bob will throw several complaints at Mei's judgment at once. Before arguing any of them, sort them into three boxes. \"The judgment fails a basic check\" is for Mei to answer, because she must prove the checks. \"The judgment has a recognised defect\" is for Bob to prove. \"The foreign court got it wrong\" goes in the bin, because it is not a defence at all."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"At common law a foreign judgment is recognised if it is a final and conclusive judgment on the merits, given by a court with international jurisdiction, and no defence is available (*Ong Han Nam* [48]). The party relying on the judgment proves the requirements; the judgment debtor proves any defence (Stage 0; C&Y 5.65). The order matters. If Bob was never in Country X and never took part, the judgment fails at the jurisdiction stage, and Bob does not need an uncertain defence such as breach of a jurisdiction clause, though he may raise it in the alternative."},{"p":"Then sort the defences themselves. Is the alleged fraud extrinsic or intrinsic? Is the \"unfairness\" really a complaint about the merits dressed up as natural justice (*Sang Cheol Woo* [94])? Is the \"error\" just a mistake, which is never a defence?"}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"**Common law recognition** (*Ong Han Nam* [48], citing *Humpuss*): a final and conclusive judgment on the merits, of a court with international jurisdiction under Singapore's rules, with no defence available. Enforcement adds a fresh money judgment for a fixed sum that is not a tax, fine or penalty (*Poh Soon Kiat* [25]–[34])."},{"t":"**Burden:** the party relying on the judgment proves the requirements in *Ong Han Nam* [48] (Stage 0); the judgment debtor proves any defence (C&Y 5.65)."},{"t":"**No review of the merits:** mistake is not a defence, and a merits complaint cannot be repackaged as natural justice (*Hong Pian Tee* [28], [31]; *Sang Cheol Woo* [94])."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-9.5"]},{"k":"step","t":"Resist in Singapore, or stop enforcement everywhere?"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"A defence is a shield Bob holds up in Singapore's court. It protects his assets here and nowhere else. An anti-enforcement injunction is an order telling Mei herself not to use her judgment anywhere. Because that comes close to cancelling another court's judgment, it is kept for exceptional cases: Mei cheated to win, or Bob never knew about the case until it was over, so he cannot be blamed for not objecting sooner."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"An **anti-enforcement injunction** (AEI) is a remedy, not a defence. It restrains the creditor personally, on pain of contempt of court, from enforcing the judgment (C&Y 5.207). Because that is close to stripping the judgment of legal effect, the applicant must show exceptional circumstances, over and above what an **anti-suit injunction** (an order restraining a party from suing abroad) requires (*Sun Travels* [97]–[98]; C&Y 5.208). Those circumstances must be tied to **unconscionability**, equity's idea that it would be against conscience to let a party insist on its strict legal rights. Two categories are recognised. Fraud by the creditor is a response to unconscionable conduct. A debtor who did not know of the proceedings until judgment cannot have lost the right to equitable relief through unconscionable delay (*Sun Travels* [100]–[101], [105]–[111], [113]; C&Y 5.208)."},{"p":"So, in a problem, separate the two tools. First ask whether Bob can resist in Singapore. Then ask whether he needs, and can justify, an order that reaches Mei's enforcement elsewhere."}]},{"k":"sec","title":"The law","sub":"","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"An AEI restrains the creditor from enforcing the judgment, in a particular country or worldwide; a defence works only in Singapore (C&Y 5.207)."},{"t":"**Exceptional circumstances tied to unconscionability:** fraud (*Ellerman Lines v Read*), or no knowledge of the proceedings until judgment (*Sun Travels* [100]–[101], [105]–[111]). The categories are not closed ([113])."},{"t":"**Not enough on its own:** breach of an exclusive jurisdiction or arbitration clause (*Sun Travels* [98]). Delay counts against the applicant when coupled with knowledge or prejudice ([108]), and silence about a known fraud may tilt the equities against it ([112])."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-9.6"]},{"k":"step","t":"Same facts, different door"},{"k":"eli5","n":[{"p":"The same complaint can win at one door and lose at another, because each door has its own rulebook. Before you argue a point, check which door you are standing at."}]},{"k":"prose","n":[{"p":"Take a judgment given in breach of an exclusive Singapore jurisdiction clause, where Bob was never in Country X and took no part. At common law his best point is that X lacked international jurisdiction over him; the breach itself is only an open defence. Under REFJA the breach removes X's deemed jurisdiction unless Bob submitted (s 5(4)(b)), even if he lived there. Now change the facts: the parties' exclusive choice of court agreement named a court of a Contracting State, and Mei sued there. Bob's complaint that he was never in that country now gets him nowhere: under the CCAA, Singapore does not ask how the chosen court took jurisdiction, and it refuses only on the closed list in ss 14–16 (C&Y 5.178, 5.186)."}]},{"k":"call","c":"tip","n":[{"p":"Use one structure for every answer: (1) the regime and why; (2) the requirements, in that regime's terms; (3) Bob's defences or grounds of refusal under that regime; (4) any wider remedy, such as an AEI; (5) time limits, and what the judgment can do once recognised. Where the regime is unclear on the facts, answer in the alternative."}]},{"k":"quiz","end":false,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-9.7"]},{"k":"take","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Identify the regime first. It fixes the procedure, the requirements and the grounds for refusal."},{"t":"Requirements come before defences. Mei proves the requirements, Bob proves the defences, and mistake is no defence."},{"t":"A defence protects assets in Singapore only. An AEI reaches further but needs exceptional circumstances tied to unconscionability."},{"t":"Time limits bite only on enforcement: six years at common law and for REFJA registration; none in the CCAA itself, though the judgment must still be enforceable in the State of origin; and none for recognition."}]}]},{"k":"traps","n":[{"ul":[{"t":"Do not jump to a defence when a requirement fails first."},{"t":"Do not seek an AEI on breach of a jurisdiction clause alone."},{"t":"Do not apply common law reasoning on presence and submission to a CCAA judgment."}]}]},{"k":"quiz","end":true,"ids":["LAW302-Q13-9.1","LAW302-Q13-9.2","LAW302-Q13-9.3","LAW302-Q13-9.4","LAW302-Q13-9.8"]}],"ic":"trophy","nq":8}]}]}]};
